KANT_TR['jaesche/guyer'] = {"jaesche-s01-001":["Preface. [Jäsche]",[]],"jaesche-s01-002":["It is already a year and a half since Kant gave me the commission to prepare his logic, just as he had delivered it in public lectures to his auditors, for the press, and to hand the same over to the public in the shape of a compendious handbook. I received from him for this end the very manuscript in his own hand which he had used in his lectures, with expression of the special, honorable trust in me that I, acquainted with the principles of his system in general, would here too easily enter into his course of ideas, would not distort or falsify his thoughts, but would present them with the requisite clarity and determinacy and at the same time in the proper order. Since now in this way, inasmuch as I have taken on the honorable commission and have sought to carry out the same as well as I was able, in accordance with the wish and the expectation of the praiseworthy sage, my much-revered teacher and friend, everything that concerns the delivery, the clothing and execution, the presentation and arrangement of the thoughts, is in part to be put to my account: so it is naturally also incumbent upon me to give the readers of this new Kantian work some account of this. On this point, then, here one or another more detailed explanation.",[]],"jaesche-s01-003":["Since the year 1765 Herr Prof. Kant had uninterruptedly laid at the ground of his lectures on logic Meier’s textbook (George Friedrich Meier’s Excerpt from the Doctrine of Reason, Halle, Gebauer 1752) as guide; on grounds about which he declared himself in a program issued by him for the announcement of his lectures in the year 1765. The copy of the aforementioned compendium which he used in his lectures is, like all the other textbooks that he used for the same end, interleaved with paper; his general remarks and elucidations as well as the more special ones, which relate most directly to the text of the compendium in the individual §§., are found partly on the interleaved paper, partly on the empty margin of the textbook itself. And this, recorded in writing here and there in scattered remarks and elucidations, now makes up together the storehouse of materials that Kant laid out here for his lectures, and that he from time to time partly expanded through new ideas, partly, in regard to various individual matters, again and again revised anew and improved. It thus contains at least the essential of all that which the famous commentator of Meier’s textbook was accustomed to communicate to his auditors on logic in his lectures, held in a free manner, and which he had considered worthy of recording.",[]],"jaesche-s01-004":["Now as regards the presentation and arrangement of matters in this work, I believed I would carry out the ideas and principles of the great man most aptly if, with respect to the economy and the division of the whole in general, I held to his express declaration, according to which nothing further may be admitted into the proper treatise of logic, and in particular into its doctrine of elements, than the theory of the three essential principal functions of thinking: concepts, judgments and inferences. Everything, therefore, that treats merely of cognition in general and its logical perfections, and that in Meier’s textbook precedes the doctrine of concepts and takes up nearly half of the whole, must accordingly still be counted as part of the introduction. “Previously,” remarks Kant right at the entrance to the eighth section, in which his author delivers the doctrine of concepts — “Previously cognition in general was treated, as propaedeutic of logic; now follows logic itself.”",[]],"jaesche-s01-005":["In accordance with this express hint I have therefore taken over into the introduction everything that occurs up to the mentioned section, which for this reason has received a much greater domain than it is otherwise accustomed to occupy in other handbooks of logic. The sequel of this was then also that the doctrine of method, as the other principal part of the treatise, had to turn out so much the shorter, the more matters, which incidentally are now rightly drawn by our more recent logicians into the territory of the doctrine of method, had already been treated in the introduction, as e.g. the doctrine of proofs and the like. It would have been a repetition as unnecessary as unseemly to make mention of these matters once again here in their right place, merely in order to make the incomplete complete and to put everything in its proper place. The latter I have nevertheless done with respect to the doctrine of definitions and of the logical division of concepts, which in Meier’s compendium already belongs to the eighth section, namely to the doctrine of elements concerning concepts; an order which Kant too left unchanged in his delivery.",[]],"jaesche-s01-006":["It is, moreover, surely self-evident that the great reformer of philosophy, and, as regards the economy and external form of logic, of this part of theoretical philosophy in particular as well, would have worked up logic according to his architectonic plan, whose essential outlines are drawn in the Critique of Pure Reason, if it had pleased him and if his business of a scientific grounding of the entire system of philosophy proper, the philosophy of the real true and certain, this far more important and more difficult business, which only he first and he too alone only in his originality could carry out, had permitted him to think of working up a logic himself. But this work he could quite well leave to others, who with insight and unbiased estimation could use his architectonic ideas for a truly purposive and well-ordered working up and treatment of this science. This was to be expected from several thorough and unbiased thinkers among our German philosophers. And this expectation has also not deceived Kant and the friends of his philosophy. Several more recent textbooks of logic are, more or less, as regards the economy and disposition of the whole, to be regarded as a fruit of those Kantian ideas on logic. And that this science has actually gained thereby, that it has become neither richer nor, properly speaking, more solid as to its import or more grounded in itself, but certainly more purified partly of all components foreign to it, partly of so many useless subtleties and mere dialectical playthings, that it has become more systematic and yet, with all scientific rigor of method, at the same time simpler: of this even the most fleeting comparison of the older with the more recent textbooks of logic, worked up according to Kantian principles, must surely convince anyone who otherwise only has correct and clear concepts of the peculiar character and the lawful boundaries of logic. For however much so many among the older handbooks of this science may distinguish themselves by scientific rigor in method, by clarity, determinacy and precision in definitions and by cogency and evidence in proofs: yet there is almost none among them in which the boundaries of the various territories belonging to general logic in the wider domain, of the merely propaedeutic, of the dogmatic and technical, of the pure and empirical, did not so run into one another and through one another that the one cannot be determinately distinguished from the other.",[]],"jaesche-s01-007":["To be sure, Herr Jakob remarks in the preface to the first edition of his Logic: “Wolff had grasped the idea of a general logic excellently, and if this great man had hit upon delivering pure logic entirely separately, he would certainly, by virtue of his systematic head, have furnished us a masterpiece which would have made all future works of this kind useless.” But he has, as it happens, not carried out this idea, and none among his successors has carried it out either, however great and well-grounded otherwise in general is the merit that the Wolffian school has acquired with respect to what is properly logical, the formal perfection in our philosophical cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s01-008":["But now apart from what, with regard to the external form, still could and had to happen for the perfecting of logic through the necessary separation of pure and merely formal propositions from empirical and real or metaphysical ones, Kant’s judgment on this point, when it is a matter of the estimation and determination of the inner import of this science as science, is not doubtful. He has several times declared himself determinately and expressly on it: that logic is to be regarded as a separate science, subsisting for itself and grounded in itself, and that consequently also since its origin and first development from Aristotle up to our times it has properly been able to gain nothing in scientific grounding. In accordance with this assertion Kant has thus thought neither of a grounding of the logical principles of identity and of contradiction themselves through a higher principle nor of a deduction of the logical forms of judgments. He has recognized and treated the principle of contradiction as a proposition that has its evidence in itself and needs no derivation from a higher principle. Only the use, the validity of this principle did he restrict, in that he banished it from the territory of metaphysics, in which dogmatism sought to make it count, and limited it to the merely logical use of reason, as valid alone only for this use.",[]],"jaesche-s01-009":["Whether now, however, the logical proposition of identity and of contradiction is actually in itself and absolutely neither capable of nor in need of any further deduction, that is admittedly another question, which leads to the momentous question: whether there is in general an absolutely first principle of all cognition and science, whether such a one is possible and can be found?",[]],"jaesche-s01-010":["The doctrine of science believes that it has discovered such a principle in the pure, absolute I and thereby completely grounded the entire philosophical knowledge not merely as to form but also as to import. And under presupposition of the possibility and apodictic validity of this absolutely single and unconditioned principle it therefore also acts entirely consistently when it does not let the logical principles of identity and of contradiction, the propositions: A = A and –A= –A /count as unconditioned, but only declares them subaltern propositions, which can and must first be proved and determined through it and its highest proposition: I am. (See Grundl. d.W.L. p.13 etc.) In an equally consistent way Schelling too, in his System of Transcendental Idealism, declares himself against the presupposition of the logical principles as unconditioned, i.e., as not to be derived from any higher ones, inasmuch as logic in general can arise only through abstraction from determinate propositions and, insofar as it arises in a scientific way, only through abstraction from the highest principles of knowledge, and consequently already presupposes these highest principles of knowledge and with them the doctrine of science itself. Since, however, from the other side these highest principles of knowledge, considered as principles, just as necessarily already presuppose the logical form: so there arises precisely from this that circle which, to be sure, cannot be resolved for science, but can nevertheless be explained, explained through recognition of a first principle of philosophy at once as to form and as to import (formal and material), in which both, form and import, reciprocally condition and ground each other. In this principle would then lie the point in which the subjective and the objective, identical and synthetic knowledge, would be one and the same.",[]],"jaesche-s01-011":["Under presupposition of such a dignity as must without doubt belong to such a principle, logic, like every other science, would accordingly have to be subordinated to the doctrine of science and its principles.",[]],"jaesche-s01-012":["But whatever may be the case with this, so much is settled: in every case logic remains, within the interior of its district, as regards the essential, unchanged; and the transcendental question: whether the logical propositions are still capable of and in need of a derivation from a higher absolute principle, can have as little influence on it itself and on the validity and evidence of its laws as the transcendental problem has on pure mathematics with regard to its scientific import: How are synthetic judgments *a priori* possible in mathematics? Just as the mathematician as mathematician, so too can the logician as logician, within the district of his science, go forward calmly and securely on his way in defining and proving, without having to concern himself with the transcendental question, lying outside his sphere, of the transcendental philosopher and teacher of the doctrine of science: How is pure mathematics or pure logic possible as science?",[]],"jaesche-s01-013":["With this general recognition of the correctness of general logic, therefore, the dispute between the skeptics and the dogmatists about the ultimate grounds of philosophical knowledge has also never been conducted in the territory of logic, whose rules every rational skeptic as well as the dogmatist recognized as valid, but always in the territory of metaphysics. And how could it be otherwise? The highest problem of philosophy proper concerns, after all, by no means subjective but objective, not identical but synthetic knowledge. Here, then, logic as such remains entirely out of play, and it could not occur either to the critique or to the doctrine of science — nor can it occur anywhere to a philosophy that knows how to distinguish determinately the transcendental standpoint from the merely logical one — to seek the ultimate grounds of real philosophical knowledge within the territory of mere logic and to want to pick out a real object from a proposition of logic, considered merely as such.",[]],"jaesche-s01-014":["Whoever has determinately fixed his eye upon, and never again loses sight of, the vast difference between logic proper (general), as a merely formal science, the science of mere thinking considered as thinking, and transcendental philosophy, this single material or real pure science of reason, the science of knowledge proper, will therefore easily be able to assess what is to be thought of the more recent attempt which Herr Bardili has recently undertaken (in his Outline of First Logic), to make out for logic itself yet its *prius*, in the expectation of finding on the path of this investigation: “a real object, either posited through it (mere logic) or else none at all positable; the key to the essence of nature either given through it or else no logic and no philosophy at all possible.” It is truly not to be seen in what possible way Herr Bardili could find out a real object from his established *prius* of logic, the principle of the absolute possibility of thinking, according to which we can repeat one as one and the very same in the many (not manifold) infinitely many times. This supposedly newly discovered *prius* of logic is after all obviously nothing more and nothing less than the old, long recognized principle of identity, lying within the territory of logic and placed at the head of this science: What I think, I think, and just this and nothing else can I now think repeated to infinity. — Who indeed, with the well-understood logical proposition of identity, will think of a manifold and not of a mere many, which certainly arises and can arise through nothing else than through mere repetition of one and the very same thinking, the mere repeated positing of an A = A = A and so on further to infinity. Hardly, therefore, may there well be found on the path which Herr Bardili has taken for this and according to that heuristic method which he has used for it, that which is of concern to philosophizing reason, the starting and end point from which it could proceed in its investigations and to which it could in turn return. The principal and most significant objections which Herr Bardili opposes to Kant and his method of philosophizing could thus also strike not so much Kant the logician as rather Kant the transcendental philosopher and metaphysician. We can there|fore",[]],"jaesche-s01-015":["here leave them all undecided in their proper place.",[]],"jaesche-s01-016":["Finally I will still remark here: that I shall, as soon as leisure permits me, work up and publish the Kantian metaphysics, for which I likewise already have the manuscript in hand, according to the same manner. Königsberg, the 20th of September 1800.",[]],"jaesche-s01-017":["Gottlob Benjamin Jäsche,",[]],"jaesche-s01-018":["Doctor and Privatdozent of Philosophy",[]],"jaesche-s01-019":["at the University in Königsberg,",[]],"jaesche-s01-020":["Member of the Learned Society",[]],"jaesche-s01-021":["at Frankfurt an der Oder.",[]],"jaesche-s02-001":["Introduction.",[]],"jaesche-s02-002":["I. Concept of logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-003":["Everything in nature, in the inanimate as well as in the animate world, happens according to rules, although we do not always know these rules. — Water falls according to laws of gravity, and with animals the motion of walking also happens according to rules. The fish in the water, the bird in the air moves according to rules. The whole of nature in general is properly nothing other than an interconnection of appearances according to rules; and there is nowhere any rulelessness. If we believe we find such, then in this case we can only say: that the rules are unknown to us.",[]],"jaesche-s02-004":["The exercise of our powers also happens according to certain rules, which we follow, at first unconscious of them, until we gradually arrive at cognition of them through attempts and a longer use of our powers, indeed in the end make them so familiar to ourselves that it costs us much effort to think them *in abstracto*. Thus, e.g., general grammar is the form of a language in general. But one also speaks without knowing grammar; and he who speaks without knowing it actually has a grammar and speaks according to rules of which, however, he is not conscious.",[]],"jaesche-s02-005":["Now just as all our powers taken together, so too in particular is the understanding bound in its actions to rules, which we can investigate. Indeed, the understanding is to be regarded as the source and the faculty of thinking rules in general. For just as sensibility is the faculty of intuitions, so the understanding is the faculty of thinking, i.e., of bringing the representations of the senses under rules. It is therefore eager to seek rules, and satisfied when it has found them. The question is thus, since the understanding is the source of rules, according to which rules it itself proceeds?",[]],"jaesche-s02-006":["For it admits of no doubt at all: we cannot think, or cannot use our understanding otherwise than according to certain rules. But these rules we can now in turn think for themselves, i.e., we can think them without their application or *in abstracto*. Now which are these rules?",[]],"jaesche-s02-007":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-008":["All rules according to which the understanding proceeds are either necessary or contingent. The former are those without which no use of the understanding at all would be possible; the latter those without which a certain determinate use of the understanding would not take place. The contingent rules, which depend on a determinate object of cognition, are as manifold as these objects themselves. Thus there is, e.g., a use of the understanding in mathematics, metaphysics, morals, etc. The rules of this particular determinate use of the understanding in the sciences mentioned are contingent, because it is contingent whether I think this or that object to which these particular rules relate.",[]],"jaesche-s02-009":["If, however, we now set aside all cognition that we must borrow merely from objects and reflect solely on the use of the understanding in general: then we discover those rules of it which are absolutely necessary in every respect and regardless of all particular objects of thinking, because without them we would not think at all. These rules can therefore also be discerned *a priori*, i.e., independently of all experience, because, without distinction of objects, they contain merely the conditions of the use of the understanding in general, whether it be pure or empirical. And from this it follows at the same time: that the general and necessary rules of thinking in general can concern solely its form, by no means its matter. Accordingly the science that contains these general and necessary rules is merely a science of the form of our cognition of the understanding or of thinking. And we can therefore make for ourselves an idea of the possibility of such a science, just as of a general grammar, which contains nothing further than the mere form of language in general, without words, which belong to the matter of language.",[]],"jaesche-s02-010":["This science of the necessary laws of the understanding and of reason in general, or, what is the same, of the mere form of thinking in general, we now call logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-011":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-012":["As a science that pertains to all thinking in general, regardless of objects as the matter of thinking, logic is",[]],"jaesche-s02-013":["1) to be regarded as basis for all other sciences and as the propaedeutic of all use of the understanding. It can, however, also just for that reason, because it abstracts entirely from all objects,",[]],"jaesche-s02-014":["2) be no organon of the sciences.",[]],"jaesche-s02-015":["By an organon, namely, we understand an instruction as to how a certain cognition is to be brought about. For this, however, it is required that I already know the object of the cognition to be produced according to certain rules. An organon of the sciences is therefore not mere logic, because it presupposes the exact knowledge of the sciences, of their objects and sources. Thus, e.g., mathematics is an excellent organon, as a science that contains the ground of the amplification of our cognition with regard to a certain use of reason. Logic, on the other hand, since as general propaedeutic of all use of the understanding and of reason in general it may not go into the sciences and anticipate their matter, is only a general art of reason (*canonica Epicuri*) of making cognitions in general conform to the form of the understanding, and thus only insofar to be called an organon, which, however, admittedly serves not for the amplification but merely for the estimation and correction of our cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-016":["3) As a science of the necessary laws of thinking, without which no use at all of the understanding and of reason takes place, which consequently are the conditions under which the understanding can and should agree solely with itself, — the necessary laws and conditions of its correct use — logic, however, is a canon. And as a canon of the understanding and of reason it may therefore also borrow no principles either from any science or from any experience; it must contain nothing but laws *a priori*, which are necessary and pertain to the understanding in general.",[]],"jaesche-s02-017":["Some logicians, to be sure, presuppose psychological principles in logic. But to bring such principles into logic is as absurd as to take morals from life. If we took the principles from psychology, i.e., from observations about our understanding, we would merely see how thinking goes on and how it is under the manifold subjective hindrances and conditions; this would thus lead to cognition of merely contingent laws. In logic, however, the question is not about contingent but about necessary rules; not how we think, but how we ought to think. The rules of logic must therefore be taken not from the contingent but from the necessary use of the understanding, which one finds in oneself without any psychology. We do not want to know in logic: how the understanding is and thinks and how it has hitherto proceeded in thinking, but how it ought to proceed in thinking. It is to teach us the correct use of the understanding, i.e., the use that agrees with itself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-018":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-019":["From the given definition of logic the remaining essential properties of this science can now also be derived; namely that it",[]],"jaesche-s02-020":["4) is a science of reason not as to mere form but as to matter, since its rules are not taken from experience and since at the same time it has reason as its object. Logic is therefore a self-cognition of the understanding and of reason, not, however, according to their faculties with regard to objects, but solely as to form. I shall not ask in logic: What does the understanding cognize and how much can it cognize or how far does its cognition go? For that would be self-cognition with regard to its material use and thus belongs in metaphysics. In logic the question is only: How will the understanding cognize itself?",[]],"jaesche-s02-021":["As a science rational as to matter and as to form, logic is finally also",[]],"jaesche-s02-022":["5) a doctrine or demonstrated theory. For since it occupies itself not with the common and, as such, merely empirical use of the understanding and of reason, but solely with the general and necessary laws of thinking in general: it rests on principles *a priori*, from which all its rules can be derived and proved, as ones to which all cognition of reason would have to conform.",[]],"jaesche-s02-023":["Through the fact that logic, as a science *a priori* or as a doctrine, is to be taken for a canon of the use of the understanding and of reason, it is essentially distinguished from aesthetics, which as mere critique of taste has no canon (law), but only a norm (model or guideline merely for estimation), which consists in general concurrence. Aesthetics, namely, contains the rules of the agreement of cognition with the laws of sensibility; logic, on the other hand, the rules of the agreement of cognition with the laws of the understanding and of reason. The former has only empirical principles and thus can never be science or doctrine, if by doctrine one understands a dogmatic instruction from principles *a priori*, where one discerns everything through the understanding without teachings obtained otherwise, from experience, and which gives us rules whose following procures the required perfection.",[]],"jaesche-s02-024":["Some, especially orators and poets, have tried to rationalize about taste, but they have never been able to pronounce a decisive judgment about it. The philosopher Baumgarten in Frankfurt had made the plan for an aesthetics, as science. But more correctly Home has called aesthetics critique, since it gives no rules *a priori* that sufficiently determine judgment, like logic, but takes its rules *a posteriori*, and only makes the empirical laws according to which we cognize the less perfect and more perfect (beautiful) more general through comparison.",[]],"jaesche-s02-025":["Logic is thus more than mere critique; it is a canon, which afterward serves for critique, i.e., as principle of the estimation of all use of the understanding in general, although only of its correctness with regard to mere form, since it is no organon, as little as general grammar.",[]],"jaesche-s02-026":["As propaedeutic of all use of the understanding in general, general logic is now at the same time also distinguished from another side from transcendental logic, in which the object itself is represented as an object of the mere understanding; whereas general logic pertains to all objects in general.",[]],"jaesche-s02-027":["If we now take together all essential marks that belong to the exhaustive determination of the concept of logic, then we shall thus have to set up the following concept of it:",[]],"jaesche-s02-028":["Logic is a science of reason not as to mere form but as to matter; a science *a priori* of the necessary laws of thinking, not, however, with regard to particular objects, but to all objects in general; — thus a science of the correct use of the understanding and of reason in general, not, however, subjectively, i.e., not according to empirical (psychological) principles, how the understanding thinks, but objectively, i.e., according to principles *a priori*, how it ought to think.",[]],"jaesche-s02-029":["[Introduction.] II. Principal divisions of logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-030":["— Delivery. — Utility of this science. — Outline of a history of it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-031":["Logic is divided",[]],"jaesche-s02-032":["1) into analytic and into dialectic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-033":["Analytic discovers through dissection all actions of reason that we exercise in thinking in general. It is thus an analytic of the form of the understanding and of reason and is also rightly called the logic of truth, because it contains the necessary rules of all (formal) truth, without which our cognition, regardless of objects, is also in itself untrue. It is thus also nothing further than a canon for dijudication (of the formal correctness of our cognition).",[]],"jaesche-s02-034":["If one wanted to use this merely theoretical and general doctrine for a practical art, i.e., for an organon: then it would become dialectic. A logic of illusion (*ars sophistica, disputatoria*), which arises from a mere misuse of analytic, insofar as according to the mere logical form the illusion of a true cognition, whose marks must nevertheless be taken from agreement with objects, thus from content, is artfully contrived.",[]],"jaesche-s02-035":["In former times dialectic was studied with great industry. This art delivered false principles under the illusion of truth and sought, in accordance with these, to assert things according to illusion. Among the Greeks the dialecticians were the advocates and orators who could lead the people wherever they wanted, because the people let themselves be deceived by illusion. Dialectic was thus at that time the art of illusion. In logic it was also for a time delivered under the name of the art of disputation, and for so long all logic and philosophy was the culture of certain garrulous heads for artfully contriving every illusion. Nothing, however, can be more unworthy of a philosopher than the culture of such an art. It must therefore in this signification fall away entirely, and instead of it rather a critique of this illusion be introduced into logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-036":["We would accordingly have two parts of logic: analytic, which would deliver the formal criteria of truth, and dialectic, which would contain the marks and rules according to which we could cognize that something does not agree with the formal criteria of truth, although it seems to agree with them. Dialectic in this signification would thus have its good utility as cathartic of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-037":["One is further accustomed to divide logic",[]],"jaesche-s02-038":["2) into natural or popular and into artificial or scientific logic (*logica naturalis, log. scholastica s. artificialis*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-039":["But this division is inadmissible. For natural logic or the logic of common reason (*sensus communis*) is properly no logic, but an anthropological science, which has only empirical principles, inasmuch as it treats of the rules of the natural use of the understanding and of reason, which are cognized only *in concreto*, thus without consciousness of them *in abstracto*. — Artificial or scientific logic therefore alone deserves this name, as a science of the necessary and general rules of thinking, which can and must be cognized independently of the natural use of the understanding and of reason *in concreto* *a priori*, although they can at first be found only through observation of that natural use.",[]],"jaesche-s02-040":["3) Yet another division of logic is that into theoretical and practical logic. But this division too is incorrect.",[]],"jaesche-s02-041":["General logic, which, as a mere canon, abstracts from all objects, can have no practical part. This would be a *contradictio in adjecto*, because a practical logic presupposes knowledge of a certain kind of objects to which it is applied. We can therefore call every science a practical logic; for in each we must have a form of thinking. General logic, considered as practical, can therefore be nothing further than a technique of erudition in general, an organon of the method of the schools.",[]],"jaesche-s02-042":["In accordance with this division, logic would thus have a dogmatic and a technical part. The first could be called the doctrine of elements, the other the doctrine of method. The practical or technical part of logic would be a logical art with regard to arrangement and logical technical expressions and distinctions, in order thereby to facilitate for the understanding its acting.",[]],"jaesche-s02-043":["In both parts, the technical as well as the dogmatic, however, neither to objects nor to the subject of thinking would it be permitted to pay the least regard. In the latter relation logic could be divided",[]],"jaesche-s02-044":["4) into pure and into applied logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-045":["In pure logic we separate the understanding from the remaining powers of the mind and consider what it does by itself alone. Applied logic considers the understanding insofar as it is mixed with the other powers of the mind, which influence its actions and give it a wrong direction, so that it does not proceed according to the laws of which it itself well sees that they are the correct ones. Applied logic should properly not be called logic. It is a psychology, in which we consider how things tend to go on in our thinking, not how they ought to go on. In the end it says, to be sure, what one ought to do in order to make a correct use of the understanding under manifold subjective hindrances and restrictions; we can also learn from it what promotes the correct use of the understanding, the aids to it or the remedies for logical mistakes and errors. But a propaedeutic it is nevertheless not. For psychology, from which in applied logic everything must be taken, is a part of the philosophical sciences, for which logic is supposed to be the propaedeutic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-046":["It is said, to be sure: technique, or the way and manner of building a science, is to be presented in applied logic. That, however, is futile, indeed even harmful. One then begins to build before one has materials and gives perhaps the form, but there is a lack of content. Technique must be presented with each science.",[]],"jaesche-s02-047":["Finally, as concerns",[]],"jaesche-s02-048":["5) the division of logic into the logic of the common and that of the speculative understanding: we remark in this regard that this science cannot be so divided at all.",[]],"jaesche-s02-049":["It cannot be a science of the speculative understanding. For as a logic of speculative cognition or of the speculative use of reason it would be an organon of other sciences and no mere propaedeutic, which is supposed to concern all possible use of the understanding and of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-050":["Just as little can logic be a product of the common understanding. The common understanding, namely, is the faculty of seeing into the rules of cognition *in concreto*. Logic, however, is supposed to be a science of the rules of thinking *in abstracto*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-051":["One can, however, take the universal human understanding as the object of logic, and to that extent it will abstract from the particular rules of speculative reason and thus be distinguished from the logic of the speculative understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-052":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-053":["As concerns the presentation of logic: it can be either scholastic or popular.",[]],"jaesche-s02-054":["It is scholastic insofar as it is suited to the desire for knowledge, the capacities and the culture of those who want to treat the cognition of the logical rules as a science. Popular, however, when it condescends to the capacities and needs of those who do not study logic as a science but want only to use it in order to enlighten their understanding. — In scholastic presentation the rules must be exhibited in their universality or *in abstracto*; in popular, on the contrary, in the particular or *in concreto*. Scholastic presentation is the foundation of the popular; for only he can present something in a popular way who could also present it more thoroughly.",[]],"jaesche-s02-055":["We distinguish here, moreover, presentation from method. By method, namely, is to be understood the way and manner in which a certain object, for the cognition of which it is to be applied, is to be completely cognized. It must be taken from the nature of the science itself and thus, as an order of thinking determined thereby and necessary, cannot be altered. Presentation signifies only the manner of communicating one's thoughts to others in order to make a doctrine understandable.",[]],"jaesche-s02-056":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-057":["From what we have said thus far about the essence and the end of logic, the worth of this science and the utility of its study can now be estimated according to a correct and determinate standard.",[]],"jaesche-s02-058":["Logic is thus, to be sure, no universal art of invention and no organon of truth — no algebra, with the help of which hidden truths could be discovered.",[]],"jaesche-s02-059":["It is, however, useful and indispensable as a critique of cognition, or for the judgment of common as well as of speculative reason, not in order to teach it, but only in order to make it correct and in agreement with itself. For the logical principle of truth is agreement of the understanding with its own universal laws.",[]],"jaesche-s02-060":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-061":["Finally, as concerns the history of logic, we will adduce on this only the following:",[]],"jaesche-s02-062":["Present-day logic derives from Aristotle's Analytic. This philosopher can be regarded as the father of logic. He presented it as organon and divided it into analytic and dialectic. His manner of teaching is very scholastic and concerns the development of the most universal concepts that lie at the ground of logic, from which, however, one has no utility, because almost everything comes down to mere subtleties, except that one drew from it the terms for various actions of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-063":["Moreover, logic has not gained much in content since Aristotle's times, and by its nature it cannot do so either. But it can well gain in regard to exactness, determinacy and distinctness. There are only a few sciences that can come into a persisting state where they are no longer altered. To these belong logic and also metaphysics. Aristotle had omitted no moment of the understanding; we are in this only more exact, more methodical and more orderly.",[]],"jaesche-s02-064":["Of Lambert's Organon it was believed, to be sure, that it would greatly augment logic. But it contains nothing more than only subtler divisions, which, like all correct subtleties, sharpen the understanding, to be sure, but are of no essential use.",[]],"jaesche-s02-065":["Among the more recent philosophers there are two who have set universal logic going: Leibniz and Wolff.",[]],"jaesche-s02-066":["Malebranche and Locke have treated no logic proper, since they treat also of the content of cognition and of the origin of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-067":["The universal logic of Wolff is the best that one has. Some have combined it with the Aristotelian, as e.g. Reusch.",[]],"jaesche-s02-068":["Baumgarten, a man who has much merit in this, concentrated the Wolffian logic, and Meier then commented in turn on Baumgarten.",[]],"jaesche-s02-069":["To the more recent logicians belongs also Crusius, who however did not consider what the case is with logic. For his logic contains metaphysical principles and thus to that extent oversteps the bounds of this science; moreover it sets up a criterion of truth that can be no criterion, and thus to that extent gives free rein to all enthusiasms.",[]],"jaesche-s02-070":["In present times there has been no famous logician to speak of, and we also need no new inventions for logic, because it contains merely the form of thinking.",[]],"jaesche-s02-071":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-072":["[Introduction.] III. Concept of philosophy in general.",[]],"jaesche-s02-073":["— Philosophy considered according to the scholastic concept and according to the worldly concept. — Essential requirements and ends of philosophizing. — Most universal and highest problems of this science.",[]],"jaesche-s02-074":["It is sometimes difficult to explain what is understood by a science. But the science gains in precision through the fixing of its determinate concept, and thus many mistakes are avoided for certain reasons, which otherwise creep in when one cannot yet distinguish the science from the sciences related to it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-075":["Before we attempt to give a definition of philosophy, however, we must first investigate the character of the various cognitions themselves and, since philosophical cognitions belong to cognitions of reason, explain in particular what is to be understood by these latter.",[]],"jaesche-s02-076":["Cognitions of reason are opposed to historical cognitions. The former are cognitions from principles (*ex principiis*); the latter cognitions from data (*ex datis*). — A cognition can, however, have arisen from reason and nonetheless be historical; as when e.g. a mere man of letters learns the products of another's reason, then his cognition of such products of reason is merely historical.",[]],"jaesche-s02-077":["One can, namely, distinguish cognitions",[]],"jaesche-s02-078":["1) according to their objective origin, i.e., according to the sources from which alone a cognition is possible. In this respect all cognitions are either rational or empirical;",[]],"jaesche-s02-079":["2) according to their subjective origin, i.e., according to the way in which a cognition can be acquired by human beings. Considered from this latter point of view, cognitions are either rational or historical, however they may have arisen in themselves. Thus something can objectively be a cognition of reason which subjectively is nevertheless only historical.",[]],"jaesche-s02-080":["With some rational cognitions it is harmful to know them merely historically, with others, on the contrary, this is indifferent. Thus e.g. the mariner knows the rules of navigation historically from his tables; and that is enough for him. But if the jurist knows jurisprudence merely historically, then he is completely spoiled for a genuine judge and still more for a lawgiver.",[]],"jaesche-s02-081":["From the stated distinction between objectively and subjectively rational cognitions it now becomes evident also that one can in a certain respect learn philosophy without being able to philosophize. He, then, who properly wants to become a philosopher must practice making a free and not a merely imitative and, so to speak, mechanical use of his reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-082":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-083":["We have declared cognitions of reason to be cognitions from principles, and from this it follows: that they must be *a priori*. There are, however, two kinds of cognitions that are both *a priori* but nevertheless have many notable differences, namely mathematics and philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-084":["It is commonly asserted that mathematics and philosophy are distinguished from one another as to the object, in that the former treats of quantity, the latter of quality. All this is false. The distinction of these sciences cannot rest on the object, for philosophy concerns everything, thus also *quanta*, and mathematics in part also, insofar as everything has a magnitude. Only the different kind of cognition of reason or use of reason in mathematics and philosophy alone constitutes the specific distinction between these two sciences. Philosophy, namely, is cognition of reason from mere concepts, mathematics, on the contrary, cognition of reason from the construction of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-085":["We construct concepts when we exhibit them in intuition *a priori* without experience or, when we exhibit in intuition the object that corresponds to our concept of it. — The mathematician can never avail himself of his reason according to mere concepts, the philosopher never of it through construction of concepts. In mathematics one uses reason *in concreto*, but the intuition is not empirical, rather one here makes something *a priori* into the object of intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-086":["And in this, then, as we see, mathematics has an advantage over philosophy, that the cognitions of the former are intuitive, those of the latter, on the contrary, only discursive cognitions. The cause, however, why in mathematics we weigh magnitudes more lies in this, that magnitudes can be constructed in intuition *a priori*, qualities, on the contrary, cannot be exhibited in intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-087":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-088":["Philosophy is thus the system of philosophical cognitions or of cognitions of reason from concepts. That is the scholastic concept of this science. According to the worldly concept it is the science of the final ends of human reason. This high concept gives philosophy dignity, i.e., an absolute worth. And actu|ally",[]],"jaesche-s02-089":["it is also it which alone has inner worth, and first gives a worth to all other cognitions.",[]],"jaesche-s02-090":["One always asks in the end, after all, what purpose does philosophizing serve and the final end of it, philosophy itself considered as science according to the scholastic concept?",[]],"jaesche-s02-091":["In this scholastic significance of the word philosophy concerns only skill; in relation to the worldly concept, on the contrary, utility. In the former respect it is thus a doctrine of skill; in the latter, a doctrine of wisdom the lawgiver of reason and the philosopher to that extent not an artist of reason, but a lawgiver.",[]],"jaesche-s02-092":["The artist of reason or, as Socrates calls him, the philodox, strives merely for speculative knowledge, without looking to how much the knowledge contributes to the final end of human reason; he gives rules for the use of reason for all sorts of arbitrary ends. The practical philosopher, the teacher of wisdom through doctrine and example, is the philosopher proper. For philosophy is the idea of a perfect wisdom, which shows us the final ends of human reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-093":["To philosophy according to the scholastic concept belong two pieces:",[]],"jaesche-s02-094":["First a sufficient supply of cognitions of reason, secondly: a systematic interconnection of these cognitions or a combination of them in the idea of a whole.",[]],"jaesche-s02-095":["Not only does philosophy permit such a strictly systematic interconnection, but it is even the only science that has a systematic interconnection in the most proper sense and gives systematic unity to all other sciences.",[]],"jaesche-s02-096":["As concerns philosophy according to the worldly concept (*in sensu cosmico*), however: one can also call it a science of the highest maxim of the use of our reason, insofar as one understands by maxim the inner principle of choice among various ends.",[]],"jaesche-s02-097":["For philosophy in the latter significance is indeed the science of the relation of all cognition and use of reason to the final end of human reason, to which, as the supreme one, all other ends are subordinated and in which they must unite into unity.",[]],"jaesche-s02-098":["The field of philosophy in this cosmopolitan significance can be brought down to the following questions: 1) What can I know? 2) What ought I to do? 3) What may I hope? 4) What is the human being?",[]],"jaesche-s02-099":["The first question is answered by metaphysics, the second by morals, the third by religion and the fourth by anthropology. Fundamentally, however, one could count all this as anthropology, because the first three questions relate to the last.",[]],"jaesche-s02-100":["The philosopher must thus be able to determine 1) the sources of human knowledge, 2) the domain of the possible and useful use of all knowledge and finally 3) the bounds of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-101":["The last is the most necessary but also the most difficult, about which, however, the philodox does not concern himself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-102":["To a philosopher belong principally two things: 1) Culture of talent and of skill, in order to use them for all sorts of ends. 2) Proficiency in the use of all means to arbitrary ends. Both must be united; for without information one will never become a philosopher, but information alone will also never make the philosopher, unless a purposive combination of all cognitions and skills into unity is added and an insight into the agreement of them with the highest ends of human reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-103":["No one at all can call himself a philosopher who cannot philosophize. Philosophizing, however, can be learned only through practice and one's very own use of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-104":["How should philosophy properly be learned, after all? Every philosophical thinker builds, so to speak, his own work on the ruins of another, but never has one come about that would have been persisting in all its parts. One therefore cannot learn philosophy already for the reason that it is not yet given. But supposing also that one were actually present: still no one who learned it would be able to say of himself that he is a philosopher, for his information of it would still always be only subjectively-historical.",[]],"jaesche-s02-105":["In mathematics the matter stands otherwise. This science one can well learn to a certain extent, for the proofs are here so evident that everyone can be convinced of them; also, on account of its evidence, it can as it were be preserved as a certain and persisting doctrine.",[]],"jaesche-s02-106":["He who wants to learn to philosophize may, on the contrary, regard all systems of philosophy only as history of the use of reason and as objects of the exercise of his philosophical talent.",[]],"jaesche-s02-107":["The true philosopher must thus as self-thinker make a free and very own, not slavishly imitative, use of his reason. But also not a dialectical one, i.e., not such a use as aims only at giving cognitions an illusion of truth and wisdom. This is the business of the mere sophist, but entirely incompatible with the dignity of the philosopher, as a connoisseur and teacher of wisdom.",[]],"jaesche-s02-108":["For science has an inner, true worth only as organ of wisdom. As such, however, it is also indispensable to it, so that one may well assert: wisdom without science is a silhouette of a perfection that we shall never attain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-109":["He who hates science but loves wisdom all the more, one calls a misologist. Misology arises commonly from an emptiness of scientific information and a certain kind of vanity combined with it. Sometimes, however, those also fall into the mistake of misology who at first had pursued the sciences with great diligence and good fortune, but in the end found no satisfaction in all their knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-110":["Philosophy is the only science that knows how to procure for us this inner contentment, for it closes as it were the scientific circle and through it the sciences then first obtain order and interconnection.",[]],"jaesche-s02-111":["We shall thus, for the sake of practice in self-thinking or philosophizing, have to look more to the method of our use of reason than to the propositions themselves at which we have arrived through it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-112":["[Introduction.] IV. Brief outline of a history of philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-113":["It is of some difficulty to determine the bounds where the common use of the understanding ceases and the speculative begins, or where common cognition of reason becomes philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-114":["Nevertheless there is here a fairly sure distinguishing mark, namely the following:",[]],"jaesche-s02-115":["Cognition of the universal *in abstracto* is speculative cognition, cognition of the universal *in concreto* common cognition. Philosophical cognition is speculative cognition of reason and it thus begins where the common use of reason begins to make attempts in the cognition of the universal *in abstracto*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-116":["From this determination of the distinction between common and speculative use of reason it can now be judged from which people one must date the beginning of philosophizing. Among all peoples, then, the Greeks first began to philosophize. For they first attempted to cultivate cognitions of reason not by the guiding thread of images, but *in abstracto*; whereas the other peoples always sought to make concepts understandable to themselves only through images *in concreto*. Thus there are still today peoples, like the Chinese and some Indians, who treat, to be sure, of things that are taken merely from reason, such as God, the immortality of the soul and the like, but yet do not seek to investigate the nature of these objects according to concepts and rules *in abstracto*. They make here no separation between the use of reason *in concreto* and that *in abstracto*. Among the Persians and Arabs there is found, to be sure, some speculative use of reason, but the rules for it they borrowed from Aristotle, thus after all from the Greeks. In Zoroaster's Zend-Avesta one discovers not the least trace of philosophy. Just this holds also of the vaunted Egyptian wisdom, which in comparison with Greek philosophy has been a mere child's play.",[]],"jaesche-s02-117":["As in philosophy, so also in regard to mathematics the Greeks were the first who cultivated this part of cognition of reason according to a speculative, scientific method, in that they demonstrated every theorem from elements.",[]],"jaesche-s02-118":["When and Where, however, among the Greeks the philosophical spirit first sprang up, that one cannot properly determine.",[]],"jaesche-s02-119":["The first who introduced the use of speculative reason, and from whom one also derived the first steps of the human understanding toward scientific culture, is Thales, the founder of the Ionian sect. He bore the epithet physicist, although he was also a mathematician; just as in general mathematics has always preceded philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-120":["Moreover, the first philosophers clothed everything in images. For poetry, which is nothing other than a clothing of thoughts in images, is older than prose. One therefore had at first, even with things that are solely objects of pure reason, to avail oneself of the language of images and poetic style of writing. Pherecydes is said to have been the first prose writer.",[]],"jaesche-s02-121":["The Ionians were followed by the Eleatics. The principle of Eleatic philosophy and of its founder Xenophanes was: in the senses is deception and illusion, only in the understanding alone lies the source of truth.",[]],"jaesche-s02-122":["Among the philosophers of this school Zeno distinguished himself as a man of great understanding and acuity and as a subtle dialectician.",[]],"jaesche-s02-123":["Dialectic signified at first the art of the pure use of the understanding in regard to abstract concepts separated from all sensibility. Hence the many encomiums of this art among the ancients. Subsequently, when those philosophers who entirely rejected the testimony of the senses necessarily had to fall, with this assertion, into many subtleties, dialectic degenerated into the art of asserting and contesting every proposition. And so it became a mere exercise for the sophists, who wanted to reason about everything and applied themselves to giving illusion the coating of the true and making black white. For this reason the name sophist, under which one otherwise thought of a man who could speak rationally and insightfully about all matters, also became now so hated and contemptible, and instead of it the name philosopher was introduced.",[]],"jaesche-s02-124":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-125":["Around the time of the Ionian school there arose in Magna Graecia a man of strange genius, who not only also established a school, but at the same time designed and brought about a project that had never yet had its like. This man was Pythagoras, born at Samos. He founded, namely, a society of philosophers who were united among themselves into a league by the law of secrecy. His hearers he divided into two classes: into that of the acusmatics (ἀκουσμαθικοι), who merely had to listen, and that of the acroamatics (ἀκροαμαθικοι), who were also permitted to ask.",[]],"jaesche-s02-126":["Among his doctrines there were some exoteric ones, which he presented to the whole people; the rest were secret and esoteric, intended only for the members of his league, some of whom he took into his most intimate friendship and separated entirely from the rest. He made physics and theology, thus the doctrine of the visible and of the invisible, the vehicle of his secret doctrines. He also had various symbols, which presumably were nothing other than certain signs that served the Pythagoreans for communicating with one another.",[]],"jaesche-s02-127":["The end of his league seems to have been no other than: to purify religion of the delusion of the people, to moderate tyranny and to introduce more lawfulness into states.This league, however, which the tyrants began to fear, was destroyed shortly before Pythagoras' death, and this philosophical society dissolved, partly through execution, partly through the flight and banishment of the greatest part of the confederates. The few who still remained were novices. And since these did not know much of Pythagoras' own doctrines, one can also say nothing certain and determinate about them. Subsequently many doctrines were ascribed to Pythagoras, who moreover was also a very mathematical mind, which however are certainly only fabricated.",[]],"jaesche-s02-128":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-129":["The most important epoch of Greek philosophy finally begins with Socrates. For it was he who gave the philosophical spirit and all speculative minds an entirely new practical direction. He was also almost the only one among all human beings whose conduct comes closest to the idea of a sage.",[]],"jaesche-s02-130":["Among his pupils Plato, who occupied himself more with the practical doctrines of Socrates, and among the pupils of Plato Aristotle, who brought speculative philosophy higher again, is the most famous.",[]],"jaesche-s02-131":["Plato and Aristotle were followed by the Epicureans and the Stoics, who were both the most sworn enemies of one another. The former placed the highest good in a cheerful heart, which they called pleasure; the latter found it solely in the loftiness and strength of soul, with which one could do without all the agreeable things of life.",[]],"jaesche-s02-132":["The Stoics were moreover dialectical in speculative philosophy, dogmatic in moral philosophy, and showed in their practical principles, through which they scattered the seed for the most sublime dispositions that ever existed, uncommonly much dignity. The founder of the Stoic school is Zeno of Citium. The most famous men of this school among the Greek philosophers are Cleanthes and Chrysippus.",[]],"jaesche-s02-133":["The Epicurean school has never been able to come into the repute in which the Stoic was. But whatever one may say of the Epicureans, this much is certain: they displayed the greatest moderation in enjoyment and were the best natural philosophers among all the thinkers of Greece.",[]],"jaesche-s02-134":["We further remark here that the foremost Greek schools bore particular names. Thus the school of Plato was called Academy, that of Aristotle Lyceum, the school of the Stoics *Porticus* (στοα), a covered walk, from which the name Stoic derives, the school of Epicurus *horti*, because Epicurus taught in gardens.",[]],"jaesche-s02-135":["Plato's Academy was followed by three other academies, which were founded by his pupils. The first was founded by Speusippus, the second by Arcesilaus and the third by Carneades.",[]],"jaesche-s02-136":["These academies inclined toward skepticism. Speusippus and Arcesilaus, both tuned their way of thinking to skepsis, and Carneades carried it still higher in this. For this reason the skeptics, these subtle, dialectical philosophers, are also called Academics. The Academics thus followed the first great doubter Pyrrho and his successors. Their teacher Plato himself had given them occasion for this, in that he presented many of his doctrines dialogically, so that grounds *pro* and *contra* were adduced, without his himself deciding about them, although he was otherwise very dogmatic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-137":["If one begins the epoch of skepticism with Pyrrho, then one gets a whole school of skeptics, who were essentially distinguished from the dogmatists in their way of thinking and method of philosophizing, in that they made it the first maxim of all philosophizing use of reason: to withhold one's judgment even with the greatest illusion of truth, and set up the principle: philosophy consists in the equilibrium of judging and teaches us to uncover false illusion. Of these skeptics, however, nothing further has remained to us than the two works of Sextus Empiricus, in which he brought together all doubts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-138":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-139":["When subsequently philosophy passed over from the Greeks to the Romans, it did not extend itself; for the Romans always remained only pupils.",[]],"jaesche-s02-140":["Cicero was in speculative philosophy a pupil of Plato, in morals a Stoic. To the Stoic sect belonged Epictetus, Antoninus the philosopher and Seneca, as the most famous. Teachers of nature there were none among the Romans, except Pliny the younger, who left behind a description of nature.",[]],"jaesche-s02-141":["Finally culture vanished also among the Romans and barbarism arose, until the Arabs in the 6th and 7th centuries began to apply themselves to the sciences and to bring Aristotle into flower again. Now, then, the sciences came up again in the Occident and in particular the authority of Aristotle, whom, however, one followed in a slavish way. In the 11th and 12th centuries the scholastics appeared; they elucidated Aristotle and drove his subtleties to infinity. One occupied oneself with nothing but sheer abstractions. This scholastic method of pseudo-philosophizing was displaced at the time of the Reformation, and now there were eclectics in philosophy, i.e., such self-thinkers as professed no school, but sought the truth and accepted it where they found it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-142":["Its improvement in more recent times, however, philosophy owes partly to the greater study of nature, partly to the combination of mathematics with natural science. The order which arose in thinking through the study of these sciences spread also over the particular branches and parts of philosophy proper. The first and greatest investigator of nature of more recent times was Bacon of Verulam. In his investigations he took the path of experience and called attention to the importance and indispensability of observations and experiments for the discovery of truth. It is moreover difficult to say from where the improvement of speculative philosophy properly comes. No small merit in it was acquired by Descartes, in that he contributed much to giving distinctness to thinking through the criterion of truth he set up, which he placed in the clarity and evidence of cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-143":["Among the greatest and most meritorious reformers of philosophy in our times, however, is to be counted Leibniz and Locke. The latter sought to analyze the human understanding and to show which powers of the soul and which operations of them belonged to this or that cognition. But he did not complete the work of his investigation, also his procedure is dogmatic, although he brought about the utility that one began to study the nature of the soul better and more thoroughly.",[]],"jaesche-s02-144":["As concerns the particular dogmatic method of philosophizing proper to Leibniz and Wolff, it was very faulty. There also lies in it so much that is deceptive that it is indeed necessary to suspend the whole procedure and instead of it to set going another, the method of critical philosophizing, which consists in investigating the procedure of reason itself, in analyzing the entire human faculty of cognition and examining: how far its bounds may well go.",[]],"jaesche-s02-145":["In our age natural philosophy is in the most flourishing state, and among the investigators of nature there are great names, e.g. Newton. More recent philosophers cannot now properly be named as distinguished and lasting names, because here everything goes on as it were in flux. What the one builds, the other tears down.",[]],"jaesche-s02-146":["In moral philosophy we have not come further than the ancients. But as concerns metaphysics: it seems as though we had become hesitant in the investigation of metaphysical truths. There now shows itself a kind of indifferentism toward this science, since one seems to make it a point of honor to speak contemptuously of metaphysical researches as of mere broodings. And yet metaphysics is the proper, true philosophy!",[]],"jaesche-s02-147":["Our age is the age of critique, and one must see what will become of the critical attempts of our time, with respect to philosophy and metaphysics in particular.",[]],"jaesche-s02-148":["[Introduction.] V. Cognition in general.",[]],"jaesche-s02-149":["— Intuitive and discursive cognition; intuition and concept and their distinction in particular. — Logical and aesthetic perfection of cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-150":["All our cognition has a twofold relation: first a relation to the object, second a relation to the subject. In the former respect it relates to representation, in the latter to consciousness, the universal condition of all cognition in general. — (Properly consciousness is a representation that another representation is in me.)",[]],"jaesche-s02-151":["In every cognition there must be distinguished matter, i.e., the object, and form, i.e., the way in which we cognize the object. — If e.g. a savage sees a house from a distance, whose use he does not know: then he has, to be sure, just the same object as another, who knows it determinately as a dwelling arranged for human beings, before him in representation. But as to form this cognition of one and the same object is different in the two. With the one it is mere intuition, with the other intuition and concept at the same time.",[]],"jaesche-s02-152":["The difference of the form of cognition rests on a condition that accompanies all cognizing, on consciousness. If I am conscious of the representation: then it is clear; if I am not conscious of it, obscure.",[]],"jaesche-s02-153":["Since consciousness is the essential condition of all logical form of cognitions, logic can and may also occupy itself only with clear but not with obscure representations. In logic we do not see how representations arise, but solely how they agree with logical form. — In general logic also cannot treat at all of mere representations and of their possibility. That it leaves to metaphysics. It itself occupies itself merely with the rules of thinking in concepts, judgments, and inferences, as that through which all thinking takes place. To be sure, something precedes before a representation becomes a concept. That we shall also indicate in its place. But we shall not investigate: How do representations arise? Logic does indeed also treat of cognizing, because in cognizing thinking already takes place. But representation is not yet cognition, rather cognition always presupposes representation. And this latter can also not be explained at all. For one would always have to explain what representation is? by means of yet another representation.",[]],"jaesche-s02-154":["All clear representations, to which alone the logical rules can be applied, can now be distinguished in regard to distinctness and indistinctness. If we are conscious of the whole representation, but not of the manifold that is contained in it, then the representation is indistinct. — For clarification of the matter, first an example in intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-155":["We catch sight of a country house in the distance. If we are conscious that the intuited object is a house, then we must necessarily also have a representation of the various parts of this house, the windows, doors, etc. For if we did not see the parts, we would also not see the house itself. But we are not conscious of this representation of the manifold of its parts, and our representation of the object thought of itself is therefore an indistinct representation.",[]],"jaesche-s02-156":["If we want further an example of indistinctness in concepts, then let the concept of beauty serve for it. Everyone has a clear concept of beauty. But in this concept various marks occur, among others, that the beautiful must be something that 1) falls into the senses and that 2) pleases universally. Now if we cannot lay out for ourselves the manifold of these and other marks of the beautiful, then our concept of it is still always indistinct.",[]],"jaesche-s02-157":["Wolff's disciples call the indistinct representation a confused one. But this expression is not fitting, because the opposite of confusion is not distinctness but order. Distinctness is indeed an effect of order and indistinctness an effect of confusion; and thus every confused cognition is also an indistinct one. But the proposition does not hold conversely: not all indistinct cognition is a confused one. For in cognitions in which no manifold is present, no order, but also no confusion, takes place.",[]],"jaesche-s02-158":["This is how matters stand with all simple representations, which never become distinct; not because there is confusion in them, but because no manifold is to be met with in them. One must therefore call them indistinct, but not confused.",[]],"jaesche-s02-159":["And even in composite representations, in which a manifold of marks can be distinguished, the indistinctness often stems not from confusion but from weakness of consciousness. Something can namely be distinct as to form, i.e., I can be conscious of the manifold in the representation; but as to matter the distinctness can diminish, if the degree of consciousness becomes smaller, although all order is there. This is the case with abstract representations.",[]],"jaesche-s02-160":["Distinctness itself can be twofold:",[]],"jaesche-s02-161":["First, a sensible one. This consists in the consciousness of the manifold in intuition. I see, e.g., the Milky Way as a whitish stripe; the light rays from the individual stars located in it must necessarily have come into my eye. But the representation of it was only clear and becomes distinct only through the telescope, because I now catch sight of the individual stars contained in that milky stripe.",[]],"jaesche-s02-162":["Second, an intellectual one; distinctness in concepts or distinctness of the understanding. This rests on the analysis of the concept in regard to the manifold that lies contained in it. Thus, e.g., in the concept of virtue there are contained as marks 1) the concept of freedom, 2) the concept of adherence to rules (of duty), 3) the concept of overpowering of the might of the inclinations, insofar as they conflict with those rules. Now if we thus resolve the concept of virtue into its individual constituents, then we make it distinct to ourselves precisely through this analysis. Through this making distinct itself, however, we add nothing to a concept; we only explain it. In distinctness, therefore, concepts are improved not as to matter, but only as to form.",[]],"jaesche-s02-163":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-164":["If we reflect on our cognitions in regard to the two essentially different fundamental faculties of sensibility and of under|standing,",[]],"jaesche-s02-165":["from which they arise, then we come here upon the distinction between intuitions and concepts. All our cognitions, namely, considered in this respect, are either intuitions or concepts. The former have their source in sensibility, the faculty of intuitions, the latter in the understanding, the faculty of concepts. This is the logical distinction between understanding and sensibility, according to which the latter supplies nothing but intuitions, the former on the contrary nothing but concepts. Both fundamental faculties can of course also be considered from yet another side and defined in another way; namely sensibility as a faculty of receptivity, the understanding as a faculty of spontaneity. But this way of defining is not logical but metaphysical. One is also accustomed to call sensibility the lower, the understanding on the contrary the higher faculty, on the ground that sensibility gives the mere material for thinking, but the understanding disposes over this material and brings it under rules or concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-166":["On the distinction indicated here between intuitive and discursive cognitions, or between intuitions and concepts, is grounded the difference between the aesthetic and the logical perfection of cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-167":["A cognition can be perfect either according to laws of sensibility or according to laws of the understanding; in the former case it is aesthetically, in the other logically perfect. Both, the aesthetic and the logical perfection, are thus of different kinds, the former relates to sensibility, the latter to the understanding. The logical perfection of cognition rests on its agreement with the object; thus on universally valid laws, and can consequently also be judged according to norms *a priori*. Aesthetic perfection consists in the agreement of cognition with the subject and is grounded on the particular sensibility of the human being. In aesthetic perfection, therefore, there take place no objectively and universally valid laws in relation to which it could be judged *a priori* in a way universally valid for all thinking beings in general. Insofar, however, as there are also universal laws of sensibility which, although not objectively and for all thinking beings in general, nonetheless have validity subjectively for the whole of humanity, an aesthetic perfection can also be thought which contains the ground of a subjectively universal satisfaction. This is beauty, that which pleases the senses in intuition and can precisely therefore be the object of a universal satisfaction, because the laws of intuition are universal laws of sensibility.",[]],"jaesche-s02-168":["Through this agreement with the universal laws of sensibility the proper, self-sufficient beautiful, whose essence consists in mere form, is distinguished in kind from the agreeable, which pleases solely in sensation through charm or emotion, and for that reason can also be only the ground of a mere private satisfaction.",[]],"jaesche-s02-169":["It is also this essential aesthetic perfection which among all is compatible with logical perfection and can best be combined with it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-170":["Considered from this side, therefore, aesthetic perfection in regard to that essentially beautiful can be advantageous to logical perfection. In another respect, however, it is also detrimental to it, insofar as in aesthetic perfection we look only to the inessentially beautiful, the charming or moving, which pleases the senses in mere sensation and relates not to the mere form but to the matter of sensibility. For charm and emotion can most of all corrupt logical perfection in our cognitions and judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s02-171":["In general there of course always remains between the aesthetic and the logical perfection of our cognition a kind of conflict that cannot be entirely removed. The understanding wants to be taught, sensibility to be enlivened, the first desires insight, the second graspability. If cognitions are to instruct, then they must to that extent be thorough; if they are at the same time to entertain, then they must also be beautiful. If a presentation is beautiful but shallow, then it can please only sensibility but not the understanding; if it is conversely thorough but dry, only the understanding but not also sensibility.",[]],"jaesche-s02-172":["Since, however, the need of human nature and the end of popularity of cognition require that we seek to unite both perfections with each other, we must also make it our concern to procure for those cognitions that are capable of an aesthetic perfection at all, this perfection, and to make a scholastically correct, logically perfect cognition popu|lar",[]],"jaesche-s02-173":["through aesthetic form. In this endeavor to combine aesthetic with logical perfection in our cognitions, however, we must not leave the following rules out of account: namely 1) that logical perfection is the basis of all other perfections and therefore may not wholly stand behind or be sacrificed to any other; 2) that one look principally to formal aesthetic perfection; the agreement of a cognition with the laws of intuition, because precisely in this consists the essentially beautiful, which can best be united with logical perfection; 3) that one must be very cautious with charm and emotion, whereby a cognition affects sensation and obtains an interest for it, because through this attention can so easily be drawn from the object to the subject, from which then evidently a very detrimental influence on the logical perfection of cognition must arise.",[]],"jaesche-s02-174":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-175":["In order to make the essential differences that obtain between the logical and the aesthetic perfection of cognition more recognizable not merely in general but from several particular sides, we will compare them both with each other in respect of the four chief moments of quantity, of quality, of relation, and of modality, on which it depends in the judgment of the perfection of cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-176":["A cognition is perfect 1) as to quantity, if it is universal; 2) as to quality, if it is distinct; 3) as to relation, if it is true, and finally 4) as to modality, if it is certain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-177":["Considered from these indicated points of view, a cognition will thus be logically perfect as to quantity: if it has objective universality (universality of the concept or of the rule), as to quality: if it has objective distinctness (distinctness in the concept), as to relation: if it has objective truth, and finally as to modality: if it has objective certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-178":["To these logical perfections there now correspond the following aesthetic perfections in relation to those four chief moments; namely",[]],"jaesche-s02-179":["1) aesthetic universality. This consists in the applicability of a cognition to a multitude of objects that serve as examples, in which the application of it can be made, and whereby it becomes at the same time usable for the end of popularity;",[]],"jaesche-s02-180":["2) aesthetic distinctness. This is distinctness in intuition, wherein through examples a concept thought abstractly is presented or clarified *in concreto*;",[]],"jaesche-s02-181":["3) aesthetic truth. A merely subjective truth, which consists only in the agreement of cognition with the subject and the laws of sense-illusion and is consequently nothing more than a universal illusion;",[]],"jaesche-s02-182":["4) aesthetic certainty. This rests on what is necessary according to the testimony of the senses, i.e., what is confirmed through sensation and experience.",[]],"jaesche-s02-183":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-184":["In the perfections just named there always occur two pieces which in their harmonious union make up perfection in general, namely: manifoldness and unity. With the understanding the unity lies in the concept, with the senses in intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-185":["Mere manifoldness without unity cannot satisfy us. And therefore among all truth is the chief perfection, because it is the ground of unity through the relation of our cognition to the object. Even in aesthetic perfection truth always remains the *conditio sine qua non*, the foremost negative condition, without which something cannot universally please taste. No one may therefore hope to advance in the beautiful sciences if he has not laid logical perfection at the ground of his cognition. In the greatest possible union of logical with aesthetic perfection in general in respect of such knowledge as is to do both, at once instruct and entertain, there is also actually shown the character and the art of genius.",[]],"jaesche-s02-186":["[Introduction.] VI. Particular logical perfections of cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-187":["A) Logical perfection of cognition as to quantity. — Magnitude. — Extensive and intensive magnitude. — Extensiveness and thoroughness or importance and fruitfulness of cognition. — Determination of the horizon of our cognitions.",[]],"jaesche-s02-188":["The magnitude of cognition can be taken in a twofold sense, either as extensive or as intensive magnitude. The former relates to the domain of cognition and thus consists in the multitude and manifoldness of it; the latter relates to its content, which concerns the manifold validity or the logical importance and fruitfulness of a cognition, insofar as it is considered as ground of many and great consequences (*non multa sed multum*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-189":["In the extension of our cognitions or in the perfecting of them as to their extensive magnitude, it is good to make for oneself an estimate of how far a cognition harmonizes with our ends and capacities. This reflection concerns the determination of the horizon of our cognitions, by which is to be understood the adequacy of the magnitude of the totality of cognitions to the capacities and ends of the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s02-190":["The horizon can be determined",[]],"jaesche-s02-191":["1) logically, according to the faculty or the cognitive powers in relation to the interest of the understanding. Here we have to judge: how far we can come in our cognitions, how far we must go in them, and to what extent certain cognitions serve in a logical aim as means to these or those chief cognitions, as our ends;",[]],"jaesche-s02-192":["2) aesthetically, according to taste in relation to the interest of feeling. He who determines his horizon aesthetically seeks to arrange science according to the taste of the public, i.e., to make it popular, or in general to acquire for himself only such cognitions as can be universally communicated and in which the class of the unlearned too finds pleasure and interest;",[]],"jaesche-s02-193":["3) practically, according to utility in relation to the interest",[]],"jaesche-s02-194":["of the will. The practical horizon, insofar as it is determined according to the influence that a cognition has on our morality, is pragmatic and of the greatest importance.",[]],"jaesche-s02-195":["The horizon thus concerns the judgment and determination of what the human being can know, what he may know, and what he ought to know.",[]],"jaesche-s02-196":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-197":["Now as concerns in particular the theoretically or logically determined horizon — and of this alone can there be talk here —, we can consider it either from the objective or from the subjective point of view.",[]],"jaesche-s02-198":["In regard to objects the horizon is either historical or rational. The former is much wider than the other, indeed it is immeasurably great, for our historical cognition has no boundaries. The rational horizon on the contrary can be fixed, it can be determined, e.g., to what kind of objects mathematical cognition cannot be extended. So also with respect to philosophical cognition of reason, how far reason may well go here *a priori* without any experience?",[]],"jaesche-s02-199":["In relation to the subject the horizon is either the universal and absolute, or a particular and conditioned one (private horizon).",[]],"jaesche-s02-200":["By the absolute and universal horizon is to be understood the congruence of the boundaries of human cognitions with the boundaries of the whole of human perfection in general. And here therefore the question is: What can the human being as human being know in general?",[]],"jaesche-s02-201":["The determination of the private horizon depends on manifold empirical and special considerations, e.g., of age, of sex, station, way of life, and the like. Every particular class of human beings thus has, in relation to its special cognitive powers, ends, and standpoints, its particular horizon, every head, according to the measure of the individuality of its powers and of its standpoint, its own horizon. Finally we can also think for ourselves a horizon of healthy reason and a horizon of science, the latter of which still requires principles in order to determine according to them what we can know and cannot know.",[]],"jaesche-s02-202":["What we cannot know is above our horizon, what we may not know or do not need to know, outside our horizon. This latter, however, can hold only relatively in relation to these or those particular private ends, to the attainment of which certain cognitions not only contribute nothing but could even be a hindrance to it. For no cognition is after all absolutely and in every aim useless and unusable, although we cannot always see its utility. It is therefore a reproach as unwise as it is unjust, made by shallow heads against great men who work at the sciences with laborious diligence, when the former ask here: what is that good for? This question one must not even raise at all, if one wants to occupy oneself with sciences. Supposing a science could give elucidations only about some possible object, it would for that reason already be useful enough. Every logically perfect cognition always has some possible utility, which, although unknown to us as yet, will perhaps be found by posterity. — Had one in the cultivation of the sciences always looked only to the material gain, the utility of them, we would have no arithmetic and geometry. Our understanding is moreover so arranged that it finds satisfaction in mere insight, and more even than in the utility that arises from it. This Plato already remarked. The human being feels his own excellence therein, he senses what it means to have understanding. Human beings who do not sense this must envy the animals. The inner worth that cognitions have through logical perfection is not to be compared with their outer one, the worth in application.",[]],"jaesche-s02-203":["As that which lies outside our horizon, insofar as according to our aims we may not know it, as dispensable for us, so also that which lies below our horizon, insofar as we ought not to know it, as harmful to us, is to be understood only in a relative, but by no means in the absolute sense.",[]],"jaesche-s02-204":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-205":["With respect to the extension and demarcation of our cognition the following rules are to be recommended:",[]],"jaesche-s02-206":["One must",[]],"jaesche-s02-207":["1) determine one's horizon early, to be sure, but of course only then when one can determine it oneself, which usually does not take place before the 20th year;",[]],"jaesche-s02-208":["2) not change it lightly and often (not fall from one thing to another);",[]],"jaesche-s02-209":["3) not measure the horizon of others according to one's own, and not hold that to be useless which is of no use to us; it would be presumptuous to want to determine the horizon of others, because one does not know well enough partly their capacities, partly their aims;",[]],"jaesche-s02-210":["4) neither extend it too much nor restrict it too much. For he who wants to know too much knows nothing in the end, and he who conversely believes of some things that they do not concern him often deceives himself; as when, e.g., the philosopher believed of history that it is dispensable to him.",[]],"jaesche-s02-211":["One should also seek",[]],"jaesche-s02-212":["5) to determine in advance the absolute horizon of the whole human race (as to past and future time), as also in particular",[]],"jaesche-s02-213":["6) to determine the place that our science occupies in the horizon of the totality of cognition. For this the universal encyclopedia serves, as a universal map (*mappe-monde*) of the sciences.",[]],"jaesche-s02-214":["7) In the determination of one's particular horizon itself one should examine carefully: for which part of cognition one has the greatest capacity and satisfaction, what is more or less needful in regard to certain duties, what cannot coexist with the necessary duties, and finally",[]],"jaesche-s02-215":["8) one should seek always to widen one's horizon rather than to narrow it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-216":["In general one need not fear from the extension of cognition what d'Alembert fears from it. For it is not the burden that presses on us, rather it is the volume of space for our cognitions that narrows us. Critique of reason, of history and historical writings, a universal spirit that goes to human cognition *en gros* and not merely *in detail*, will always make the domain smaller without diminishing anything in the content. Merely the dross falls away from the metal, or the less noble vehicle, the husk, which was needful until then. With the extension of natural history, of mathematics, etc., new methods will be invented which shorten the old and make the multitude of books dispensable. On the invention of such new methods and principles it will depend that we, without burdening memory, can find everything ourselves at will with their help. Hence he renders service to history like a genius who comprehends it under ideas that can always remain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-217":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-218":["To the logical perfection of cognition in regard to its domain ignorance stands opposed. A negative imperfection or imperfection of lack, which, on account of the limitations of the understanding, remains inseparable from our cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-219":["We can consider ignorance from an objective and from a subjective point of view.",[]],"jaesche-s02-220":["1) Taken objectively, ignorance is either a material or a formal one. The former consists in a lack of historical, the other in a lack of rational cognitions. One must be wholly ignorant in no field, but one can well restrict historical knowledge in order to apply oneself all the more to rational, or conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s02-221":["2) In subjective significance ignorance is either a learned, scientific or a common one. He who distinctly sees the limitations of cognition, thus the field of ignorance, from where it begins, the philosopher, e.g., who sees and proves how little one can know, for lack of the *data* required for it, in regard to the structure of gold, is ignorant artfully or in a learned way. He on the other hand who is ignorant without seeing the grounds of the boundaries of knowledge and concerning himself about them is so in a common, not scientific way. Such a one does not even know that he knows nothing. For one can never represent one's ignorance otherwise than through science, just as a blind man cannot represent darkness to himself until he has become sighted.",[]],"jaesche-s02-222":["Knowledge of one's ignorance thus presupposes science and at the same time makes modest, whereas imagined knowledge puffs up. Thus Socrates' not-knowing was a laudable ignorance, properly a knowledge of not-knowing according to his own confession. Those, therefore, who possess very much knowledge and with all that are nonetheless astonished at the multitude of what they do not know, the reproach of ignorance can hardly strike.",[]],"jaesche-s02-223":["Blameless (*inculpabilis*) in general is ignorance in things the cognition of which goes above our horizon, and permitted (although also only in the relative sense) it can be in regard to the speculative use of our cognitive faculties, insofar as the objects here lie, although not above, yet outside our horizon. Shameful, however, it is in things that are very needful and also easy for us to know.",[]],"jaesche-s02-224":["There is a difference between not knowing something and ignoring something, i.e., taking no notice of it. It is good to ignore much that it is not good for us to know. From both is distinguished yet abstracting. One abstracts, however, from a cognition when one ignores the application of it, whereby one gets it *in abstracto* and can then better consider it in general as principle. Such an abstracting from that which in the cognition of a thing does not belong to our aim is useful and praiseworthy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-225":["Teachers of reason are commonly historically ignorant.",[]],"jaesche-s02-226":["Historical knowledge without determinate boundaries is polyhistory; this puffs up. Polymathy goes to cognition of reason. Both, historical as well as rational knowledge extended without determinate boundaries, can be called pansophy. To historical knowledge belongs the science of the instruments of erudition — philology, which comprises within itself a critical acquaintance with books and languages (literature and linguistics).",[]],"jaesche-s02-227":["Mere polyhistory is a cyclopean erudition that lacks one eye, the eye of philosophy, and a cyclops of a mathematician, historian, describer of nature, philologist, and linguist is a learned man who is great in all these pieces but on that account holds all philosophy to be dispensable.",[]],"jaesche-s02-228":["One part of philology is made up by the *humaniora*, by which is understood acquaintance with the ancients, which furthers the union of science with taste, grinds off roughness, and furthers the communicability and urbanity in which humanity consists.",[]],"jaesche-s02-229":["The *humaniora* thus concern an instruction in that which serves for the cultivation of taste, in accordance with the models of the ancients. To this belong, e.g., eloquence, poetry, wide reading in the classical authors, and the like. All this humanistic knowledge one can reckon to the practical part of philology, aimed first of all at the formation of taste. But if we further separate the mere philologist from the humanist, then the two would be distinguished from each other in that the former seeks the instruments of erudition among the ancients, the latter on the other hand the instruments of the formation of taste.",[]],"jaesche-s02-230":["The belletrist or *bel esprit* is a humanist according to contemporary models in living languages. He is thus not a learned man, for only dead languages are now learned languages, but a mere dilettante in the knowledge of taste according to fashion, without needing the ancients. One could call him the ape of the humanist. The polyhistor must as philologist be linguist and literator, and as humanist he must be classicist and their interpreter. As philologist he is cultivated, as humanist civilized.",[]],"jaesche-s02-231":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-232":["In regard to the sciences there are two degenerations of the prevailing taste: pedantry and galantry. The one pursues the sciences merely for the school and thereby restricts them in respect of their use, the other pursues them merely for social intercourse or the world and thereby limits them with respect to their content.",[]],"jaesche-s02-233":["The pedant is either, as learned man, opposed to the man of the world and is to that extent the puffed-up learned man without knowledge of the world, i.e., without knowledge of the way and manner of bringing his science to people, or he is to be considered indeed as the man of skill in general, but only in formalities, not as to essence and end. In the latter significance he is a stickler for formalities; restricted in regard to the kernel of things, he looks only to the dress and the shell. He is the failed imitation or caricature of the methodical head. One can therefore also call pedantry the brooding fussiness and useless exactitude (micrology) in formalities. And such a formal element of the school method outside the school is to be met with not merely among learned men and in learned affairs, but also among other estates and in other things. The ceremonial at courts, in social intercourse, what is it other than a hunt for formalities and hairsplitting? In the military it is not wholly so, although it seems so. But in conversation, in dress, in diet, in religion much pedantry often prevails.",[]],"jaesche-s02-234":["An exactitude in formalities that is suited to its end is thoroughness (scholastically correct, scholastic perfection). Pedantry is thus an affected thoroughness, just as galantry, as a mere courtesan for the approval of taste, is nothing but an affected popularity. For galantry is only concerned to make the reader well-disposed to itself and therefore not even to offend him by a difficult word.",[]],"jaesche-s02-235":["To avoid pedantry, extensive knowledge is required not only in the sciences themselves but also in regard to their use. Hence only the true learned man can free himself from pedantry, which is always the property of a restricted head.",[]],"jaesche-s02-236":["In the endeavor to procure for our cognition the perfection of scholastic thoroughness and at the same time of popularity, without thereby falling into the aforementioned faults of an affected thoroughness or an affected popularity, we must above all look to the scholastic perfection of our cognition, the scholastically correct form of thoroughness, and only then take care how we make the cognition methodically learned in the school truly popular, i.e., so easily and universally communicable to others that thoroughness is nonetheless not displaced by popularity. For for the sake of popular perfection, to please the people, scholastic perfection must not be sacrificed, without which all science would be nothing but a plaything and trifling.",[]],"jaesche-s02-237":["But in order to learn true popularity one must read the ancients, e.g., Cicero's philosophical writings, the poets Horace, Virgil, etc., among the moderns Hume, Shaftesbury, and others. Men who all have had much intercourse with the refined world, without which one cannot be popular. For true popularity requires much practical knowledge of the world and of human beings, knowledge of the concepts, the taste, and the inclinations of human beings, to which constant regard is to be had in the presentation and even in the choice of fitting expressions adequate to popularity. Such a descent (condescen|sion)",[]],"jaesche-s02-238":["to the power of comprehension of the public and to the customary expressions, wherein scholastic perfection is not set aside, but only the clothing of the thoughts is so arranged that one does not let the scaffolding, the scholastically correct and technical part of that perfection, be seen (just as one draws lines with a pencil, on which one writes, and wipes them away afterwards), this truly popular perfection of cognition is in fact a great and rare perfection, which shows much insight into the science. It also has, besides many other merits, this one too, that it can give a proof of the complete insight into a thing. For the merely scholastic examination of a cognition still leaves the doubt: whether the examination is not one-sided, and whether the cognition itself also has a worth granted to it by all human beings? The school has its prejudices just as the common understanding does. The one improves the other here. It is therefore important to examine a cognition on human beings whose understanding depends on no school.",[]],"jaesche-s02-239":["This perfection of cognition, whereby it qualifies for an easy and universal communication, one could also call the outer extension or the extensive magnitude of a cognition, insofar as it is outwardly spread among many human beings.",[]],"jaesche-s02-240":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-241":["Since there are so many and manifold cognitions, one will do well to make oneself a plan according to which one orders the sciences as they best harmonize with one's ends and contribute to the furthering of them. All cognitions stand among one another in a certain natural connection. Now if in the endeavor after extension of cognitions one does not look to this their interconnection, then out of all much-knowing nothing further comes than mere rhapsody. But if one makes a chief science one's end and considers all other cognitions only as means to arrive at it, then one brings into one's knowledge a certain systematic character. — And in order to go to work according to such a well-ordered and purposive plan in the extension of one's cognitions, one must thus seek to come to know that interconnection of cognitions among one another. For this, guidance is given by the architectonic of the sciences, which is a system according to ideas, in which the sciences are considered in regard to their kinship and systematic combination in a whole of the cognition that interests humanity.",[]],"jaesche-s02-242":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-243":["As concerns now in particular, however, the intensive magnitude of cognition, i.e., its content, or its manifold validity and importance, which, as we remarked above, is essentially distinguished from the extensive magnitude, the mere extensiveness of the same: we wish to make on this only the following few remarks:",[]],"jaesche-s02-244":["1) A cognition that goes to the large, i.e., the whole in the use of the understanding, is to be distinguished from subtlety in the small (micrology).",[]],"jaesche-s02-245":["2) Every cognition is to be called logically important that furthers logical perfection as to form, e.g., every mathematical proposition, every distinctly understood law of nature, every correct philosophical definition. Practical importance one cannot foresee, but one must wait for it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-246":["3) One must not confuse importance with difficulty. A cognition can be difficult without being important, and conversely. Difficulty therefore decides neither for nor against the worth and the importance of a cognition. This rests on the magnitude or plurality of the consequences. The more or the greater consequences a cognition has, the more use can be made of it, the more important it is. A cognition without important consequences is called a rumination; such, e.g., was scholastic philosophy.",[]],"jaesche-s02-247":["[Introduction.] VII. B) Logical perfection of cognition as to relation.",[]],"jaesche-s02-248":["— Truth. — Material and formal or logical truth. — Criteria of logical truth. —Falsehood and error. — Illusion, as source of error. — Means for avoidance of errors.",[]],"jaesche-s02-249":["A chief perfection of cognition, indeed the essential and inseparable condition of all perfection of the same, is truth. Truth, it is said, consists in the agreement of cognition with the object. According to this mere nominal definition, then, my cognition, in order to count as true, is to agree with the object. Now I can, however, compare the object with my cognition only by cognizing it. My cognition is thus to confirm itself, which, however, is far from sufficient for truth. For since the object is outside me and the cognition in me, I can still only ever judge: whether my cognition of the object agrees with my cognition of the object. Such a circle in defining the ancients called diallelon. And actually this fault was also always charged against the logicians by the skeptics, who remarked: it is with that definition of truth just as when someone makes a statement before a court and in doing so appeals to a witness whom no one knows, but who wants to make himself credible by asserting that the one who called him as witness is an honest man. The accusation was certainly grounded. Only the solution of the problem in question is absolutely and for every human being impossible.",[]],"jaesche-s02-250":["The question here is namely: Whether and to what extent there is a certain, universal and in application usable criterion of truth? For that is what the question: What is truth? is to signify.",[]],"jaesche-s02-251":["In order to be able to decide this important question, we must well distinguish that which in our cognition belongs to the matter of the same and relates to the object, from that which concerns the mere form, as that condition without which a cognition would be no cognition at all. With regard to this difference between the objective, material and the subjective, formal relation in our cognition, the above question therefore falls into the two particular ones:",[]],"jaesche-s02-252":["1) Is there a universal material, and",[]],"jaesche-s02-253":["2) Is there a universal formal criterion of truth?",[]],"jaesche-s02-254":["A universal material criterion of truth is not possible; it is even in itself contradictory. For as a universal one, valid for all objects in general, it would have to abstract completely from all difference among them and yet at the same time, as a material criterion, go precisely to this difference, in order to be able to determine whether a cognition agrees precisely with that object to which it is related, and not with some object in general — with which actually nothing at all would be said. In this agreement of a cognition with that determinate object to which it is related, however, material truth must consist. For a cognition which is true in respect of One object can be false in relation to other objects. It is therefore absurd to demand a universal material criterion of truth, which is at the same time to abstract and also not to abstract from all difference of objects.",[]],"jaesche-s02-255":["If now, however, the question is about universal formal criteria of truth, then the decision here is easy, that there certainly can be such. For formal truth consists solely in the harmony of cognition with itself in complete abstraction from all objects whatsoever and from all difference among them. And the universal formal criteria of truth are accordingly nothing other than universal logical marks of the agreement of cognition with itself or — which is the same — with the universal laws of the understanding and of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-256":["These formal, universal criteria are to be sure not sufficient for objective truth, but they are nevertheless to be regarded as the *conditio sine qua non* of it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-257":["For before the question: whether cognition harmonizes with the object, there must precede the question, whether it harmonizes with itself (as to form)? And this is the business of logic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-258":["The formal criteria of truth in logic are",[]],"jaesche-s02-259":["1) the principle of contradiction,",[]],"jaesche-s02-260":["2) the principle of sufficient ground.",[]],"jaesche-s02-261":["Through the former the logical possibility, through the latter the logical actuality of a cognition is determined.",[]],"jaesche-s02-262":["To the logical truth of a cognition there belongs namely",[]],"jaesche-s02-263":["First: that it be logically possible, i.e., not contradict itself. This characteristic of internal logical truth is, however, only negative; for a cognition which contradicts itself is indeed false, but if it does not contradict itself, not always true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-264":["Second: that it be logically grounded, i.e., that it a) have grounds and b) not have false consequences.",[]],"jaesche-s02-265":["This second criterion, concerning the logical interconnection of a cognition with grounds and consequences, of external logical truth or of the rationability of cognition, is positive. And here the following rules hold:",[]],"jaesche-s02-266":["1) From the truth of the consequence one can infer the truth of the cognition as ground, but only negatively: if One false consequence flows from a cognition, then the cognition itself is false. For if the ground were true, then the consequence would also have to be true, because the consequence is determined by the ground. —",[]],"jaesche-s02-267":["One cannot, however, infer conversely: if no false consequence flows from a cognition, then it is true; for one can draw true consequences from a false ground.",[]],"jaesche-s02-268":["2) If all consequences of a cognition are true: then the cognition is also true. For were there only something false in the cognition, then a false consequence would also have to occur.",[]],"jaesche-s02-269":["From the consequence one can thus indeed infer a ground, but without being able to determine this ground. Only from the sum total of all consequences alone can one infer of a determinate ground that this is the true one.",[]],"jaesche-s02-270":["The former kind of inference, according to which the consequence can be only a negatively and indirectly sufficient criterion of the truth of a cognition, is called in logic the apagogic (*modus tollens*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-271":["This procedure, of which frequent use is made in geometry, has the advantage that I need derive from a cognition only One false consequence in order to prove its falsehood. In order, e.g., to show that the earth is not flat, I need, without bringing forward positive and direct grounds, only infer apagogically and indirectly thus: Were the earth flat, then the pole star would always have to be equally high; now this, however, is not the case, consequently it is not flat.",[]],"jaesche-s02-272":["With the other, the positive and direct kind of inference (*modus ponens*), the difficulty arises that the totality of consequences cannot be cognized apodictically, and that one is therefore led through the kind of inference in question only to a probable and hypothetically-true cognition (hypotheses), according to the presupposition: that where many consequences are true, the remaining ones may also all be true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-273":["We will thus be able here to set up three principles, as universal, merely formal or logical criteria of truth; these are",[]],"jaesche-s02-274":["1) the principle of contradiction and of identity (*principium contradictionis* and *identitatis*), through which the inner pos|sibility",[]],"jaesche-s02-275":["of a cognition for problematic judgments is determined;",[]],"jaesche-s02-276":["2) the principle of sufficient ground (*principium rationis sufficientis*), on which the (logical) actuality of a cognition rests, that it be grounded, as material for assertoric judgments;",[]],"jaesche-s02-277":["3) the principle of the excluded third (*principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria*), on which the (logical) necessity of a cognition is grounded — that one must necessarily judge thus and not otherwise, i.e., that the opposite is false — for apodictic judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s02-278":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-279":["The opposite of truth is falsehood, which, insofar as it is taken for truth, is called error. An erroneous judgment — for error as well as truth is only in judgment — is thus one which confuses the illusion of truth with truth itself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-280":["How truth is possible, that is easy to see, since here the understanding acts according to its essential laws.",[]],"jaesche-s02-281":["But how error in the formal significance of the word, i.e., how the form of thinking contrary to the understanding is possible, that is hard to comprehend, just as it is in general not to be comprehended how any power should deviate from its own essential laws. In the understanding itself and its essential laws we cannot, then, seek the ground of errors, as little as in the limitations of the understanding, in which indeed the cause of ignorance, but by no means of error, lies. Now had we no other cognitive power than the understanding: then we would never err. However, there lies in us, besides the understanding, yet another indispensable source of cognition. That is sensibility, which gives us the material for thinking and in doing so acts according to other laws than the understanding. From sensibility considered in and for itself, however, error also cannot arise, because the senses do not judge at all.",[]],"jaesche-s02-282":["The ground of origin of all error will therefore have to be sought solely and alone in the unnoticed influence of sensibility on the understanding, or to speak more exactly, on judgment. This influence namely brings it about that in judging we take merely subjective grounds for objective ones and consequently confuse the mere illusion of truth with truth itself. For in this consists precisely the essence of illusion, which on that account is to be regarded as a ground for taking a false cognition to be true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-283":["What makes error possible is thus illusion, according to which in judgment the merely subjective is confused with the objective.",[]],"jaesche-s02-284":["In a certain sense one can indeed also make the understanding the author of errors, insofar namely as, from lack of requisite attention to that influence of sensibility, it lets itself be misled by the illusion arising from this into taking merely subjective determining grounds of judgment for objective ones, or into letting what is true only according to laws of sensibility count as true according to its own laws.",[]],"jaesche-s02-285":["Only the guilt of ignorance lies accordingly in the limitations of the understanding, the guilt of error we have to ascribe to ourselves. Nature has indeed denied us many pieces of knowledge, it leaves us about so much in an unavoidable ignorance, but error it nevertheless does not cause. To this we are misled by our own propensity to judge and to decide, even there where on account of our limitedness we are not capable of judging and deciding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-286":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-287":["All error into which the human understanding can fall is, however, only partial, and in every erroneous judgment there must always lie something true. For a total error would be a complete conflict against the laws of the understanding and of reason. How could it, as such, in any way come from the understanding, and, insofar as it is nevertheless a judgment, be taken for a product of the understanding!",[]],"jaesche-s02-288":["With regard to the true and the erroneous in our cognition we distinguish an exact from a crude cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-289":["The cognition is exact if it is adequate to its object, or if in respect of its object not the least error occurs, it is crude if errors can be in it without precisely being a hindrance to the aim.",[]],"jaesche-s02-290":["This difference concerns the wider or narrower determinateness of our cognition (*cognitio late vel stricte determinata*). At the beginning it is sometimes necessary to determine a cognition in a wider domain (*late determinare*), especially in historical things. In cognitions of reason, however, everything must be exactly (*stricte*) determined. With late determination one says: a cognition is determined *praeter propter*. It always depends on the aim of a cognition whether it is to be crudely or exactly determined. Late determination still always leaves a latitude for error, which can, however, have its determinate boundaries. Error occurs especially where a late determination is taken for a strict one, e.g., in matters of morality, where everything must be strictly determined. Those who do not do this are called by the English latitudinarians.",[]],"jaesche-s02-291":["From exactness, as an objective perfection of cognition — since the cognition here completely congrues with the object — one can further distinguish subtlety as a subjective perfection of the same.",[]],"jaesche-s02-292":["A cognition of a thing is subtle if one discovers in it that which tends to escape the attention of others. It thus requires a higher degree of attention and a greater expenditure of power of understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-293":["Many censure all subtlety because they cannot attain it. But it in itself always does honor to the understanding and is even meritorious and necessary insofar as it is applied to an object worthy of observation. If, however, one could have attained the same end with a lesser attention and exertion of the understanding, and one nevertheless expends more on it: then one makes useless expenditure and falls into subtleties which are indeed difficult but of no use (*nugae difficiles*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-294":["Just as the crude is opposed to the exact, so the coarse is opposed to the subtle.",[]],"jaesche-s02-295":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-296":["From the nature of error, in whose concept, as we remarked, besides falsehood, the illusion of truth is also contained as an essential mark, there results for the truth of our cognition the following important rule:",[]],"jaesche-s02-297":["In order to avoid errors — and unavoidable at least absolutely or without qualification is no error, although it can be so relatively for the cases where it is unavoidable for us, even at the risk of erring, to judge — thus in order to avoid errors, one must seek to discover and to explain the source of them, illusion. That, however, very few philosophers have done. They have sought only to refute the errors themselves, without indicating the illusion from which they arise. This uncovering and resolution of illusion is, however, a far greater merit with respect to truth than the direct refutation of the errors themselves, through which one cannot stop up the source of them and cannot prevent that the very same illusion, because one does not know it, mislead in other cases again into errors. For even if we have been convinced that we have erred: still there remain to us, in case the illusion itself which lies at the ground of our error is not removed, scruples, however little we can bring forward in justification of them.",[]],"jaesche-s02-298":["Through explanation of illusion one moreover also lets a kind of fairness be done to the one who errs. For no one will concede that he has erred without some illusion of truth, which perhaps could also have deceived someone more acute, because here it depends on subjective grounds.",[]],"jaesche-s02-299":["An error where the illusion is evident even to the common understanding (*sensus communis*) is called an absurdity or preposterousness. The reproach of absurdity is always a personal censure, which one must avoid, especially in the refutation of errors.",[]],"jaesche-s02-300":["For to the one who asserts a preposterousness the illusion that lies at the ground of this evident falsehood is nevertheless itself not evident. One must first make this illusion evident to him. If he persists in it even then, then he is to be sure absurd; but then there is also nothing further to be done with him. He has thereby made himself just as incapable as unworthy of all further correction and refutation. For one can actually prove to no one that he is preposterous; here all rationalizing would be in vain. If one proves the preposterousness, then one speaks no longer with the one who errs, but with the rational one. But there the uncovering of the preposterousness (*deductio ad absurdum*) is not necessary.",[]],"jaesche-s02-301":["An absurd error one can also call one",[]],"jaesche-s02-302":["for which nothing, not even illusion, serves as excuse; just as a coarse error is an error which shows ignorance in common cognition or an offense against common attention.",[]],"jaesche-s02-303":["Error in principles is greater than in their application.",[]],"jaesche-s02-304":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-305":["An external mark or an external touchstone of truth is the comparison of our own judgments with those of others, because the subjective will not be present in all others in the same way, hence the illusion can thereby be explained. The incompatibility of the judgments of others with ours is therefore to be regarded as an external mark of error and as a hint to examine our procedure in judging, but not on that account to reject it at once. For one can perhaps nevertheless be right in the matter and wrong only in the manner, i.e., the presentation.",[]],"jaesche-s02-306":["The common human understanding (*sensus communis*) is also in itself a touchstone for discovering the faults of the artificial use of the understanding. That is called: orienting oneself in thinking or in the speculative use of reason through the common understanding, when one uses the common understanding as a test for the estimation of the correctness of the speculative.",[]],"jaesche-s02-307":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-308":["Universal rules and conditions of the avoidance of error in general are: 1) to think for oneself, 2) to think oneself in the place of another, and 3) always to think in agreement with oneself. The maxim of thinking for oneself one can call the enlightened; the maxim of putting oneself in thinking into the standpoints of others, the broadened; and the maxim of always thinking in agreement with oneself, the consistent or cogent way of thinking.",[]],"jaesche-s02-309":["[Introduction.] VIII. C) Logical perfection of cognition as to quality.",[]],"jaesche-s02-310":["— Clarity. — Concept of a mark in general. — Different kinds of marks. — Determination of the logical essence of a thing. — Difference of the same from the real essence. — Distinctness, a higher degree of clarity. — Aesthetic and logical distinctness. — Difference between analytic and synthetic distinctness.",[]],"jaesche-s02-311":["Human cognizing is on the side of the understanding discursive, i.e., it takes place through representations that make what is common to several things the ground of cognition, hence through marks as such. We thus cognize things only through marks and that is precisely what is called cognizing, which comes from knowing.",[]],"jaesche-s02-312":["A mark is that in a thing which constitutes a part of the cognition of it, or — which is the same — a partial representation, insofar as it is considered as ground of cognition of the whole representation. — All our concepts are accordingly marks and all thinking is nothing other than a representing through marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-313":["Every mark can be considered from two sides:",[]],"jaesche-s02-314":["First, as representation in itself;",[]],"jaesche-s02-315":["Second, as belonging like a partial concept to the whole representation of a thing and thereby as ground of cognition of this thing itself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-316":["All marks, considered as grounds of cognition, are of twofold use, either an internal or an external one. The inner use consists in derivation, in order to cognize the thing itself through marks, as its grounds of cognition. The outer use consists in comparison, insofar as we can compare a thing through marks with others according to the rules of identity or diversity.",[]],"jaesche-s02-317":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-318":["There are among marks various specific differences, on which the following classification of them is grounded.",[]],"jaesche-s02-319":["1) Analytic or synthetic marks. The former are partial concepts of my actual concept (which I already think in it), the latter on the other hand are partial concepts of the merely possible whole concept, (which thus is first to come to be through a synthesis of several parts). The former are all concepts of reason, the latter can be concepts of experience.",[]],"jaesche-s02-320":["2) Coordinate or subordinate. This division of marks concerns their connection next to or under one another.",[]],"jaesche-s02-321":["The marks are coordinate insofar as each of them is represented as an immediate mark of the thing, and subordinate insofar as one mark is represented in the thing only by means of the other. The combination of coordinate marks into the whole of the concept is called an aggregate, the combination of subordinate marks a series. The former, the aggregation of coordinate marks, constitutes the totality of the concept, which, however, in respect of synthetic empirical concepts can never be completed, but resembles a straight line without boundaries.",[]],"jaesche-s02-322":["The series of subordinate marks runs up *a parte ante*, or on the side of grounds, against unanalyzable concepts, which on account of their simplicity cannot be further dissected, *a parte post*, or in respect of consequences on the other hand, it is infinite, because we have indeed a highest *genus*, but no lowest *species*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-323":["With the synthesis of every new concept in the aggregation of coordinate marks the extensive or broad distinctness grows, just as with the further analysis of concepts in the series of subordinate marks the intensive or deep distinctness. This latter kind of distinctness, since it necessarily serves the thoroughness and cogency of cognition, is on that account chiefly the business of philosophy and is carried furthest especially in metaphysical investigations.",[]],"jaesche-s02-324":["3) Affirming or negating marks. Through the former we cognize what the thing is, through the latter, what it is not.",[]],"jaesche-s02-325":["The negating marks serve to keep us from errors. Hence they are unnecessary where it is impossible to err, and only necessary and of importance in those cases where they keep us from an important error into which we can easily fall. Thus, e.g., in respect of the concept of a being such as God, the negating marks are very necessary and important.",[]],"jaesche-s02-326":["Through affirming marks we thus wish to understand something, through negating ones — into which one can transform all marks whatsoever — only not to misunderstand or only not to err in it, even should we come to know nothing of it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-327":["4) Important and fruitful or empty and unimportant marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-328":["A mark is important and fruitful if it is a ground of cognition of great and numerous consequences, partly in respect of its inner use, the use in derivation, insofar as it is sufficient in order to cognize thereby very much in the thing itself, partly with regard to its outer use, the use in comparison, insofar as it serves to cognize both the similarity of a thing with many others and also the difference of the same from many others.",[]],"jaesche-s02-329":["Moreover we must here distinguish logical importance and fruitfulness from the practical, from usefulness and usability.",[]],"jaesche-s02-330":["5) Sufficient and necessary or insufficient and contingent marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-331":["A mark is sufficient insofar as it suffices to distinguish the thing always from all others; otherwise it is insufficient, as, e.g., the mark of barking of the dog. The sufficiency of marks, however, just as well as their importance, is to be determined only in a relative sense, in relation to the ends which are intended through a cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-332":["Necessary marks, finally, are those which must always be met with in the represented thing. Such marks are also called essential and are opposed to the extra-essential and contingent ones, which can be separated from the concept of the thing.",[]],"jaesche-s02-333":["Among the necessary marks there is, however, yet a difference.",[]],"jaesche-s02-334":["Some of them belong to the thing as grounds of other marks of One and the same thing, others on the other hand only as consequences of other marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-335":["The former are primitive and constitutive marks (*constitutiva, essentialia in sensu strictissimo*), the others are called attributes (*consectaria, rationata*) and belong indeed also to the essence of the thing, but only insofar as they must first be derived from those essential pieces of it; as, e.g., the three angles in the concept of a triangle from the three sides.",[]],"jaesche-s02-336":["The extra-essential marks are also again of twofold kind, they concern either inner determinations of a thing (*modi*) or its outer relations (*relationes*). Thus, e.g., the mark of learnedness designates an inner determination of the human being, being master or servant, only an outer relation of the same.",[]],"jaesche-s02-337":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-338":["The sum total of all essential pieces of a thing or the sufficiency of its marks as to coordination or subordination, is the essence (*complexus notarum primitivarum, interne conceptui dato sufficientium; s. complexus notarum, conceptum aliquem primitive constituentium*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-339":["In this definition, however, we must here not think at all of the real or natural essence of things, which we are in no way able to understand. For since logic abstracts from all content of cognition, consequently also from the thing itself: in this science there can be talk solely and only of the logical essence of things. And this we can easily understand. For to it belongs nothing further than the cognition of all the predicates in respect of which an object is determined through its concept; whereas for the real essence of the thing (*esse rei*) there is required the cognition of those predicates on which, as determining grounds, everything that belongs to its existence depends. If we wish, e.g., to determine the logical essence of body: then we have no need at all to seek out the data for this in nature; we need only direct our reflection to the marks which, as essential pieces (*constitutiva, rationes*), originally constitute its fundamental concept. For the logical essence is indeed itself nothing other than the first fundamental concept of all necessary marks of a thing (*esse conceptus*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-340":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-341":["The first stage of the perfection of our cognition as to quality is thus its clarity. A second stage, or a high|er",[]],"jaesche-s02-342":["degree of clarity, is distinctness. This consists in the clarity of marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-343":["We must here first of all distinguish logical distinctness in general from the aesthetic. The logical rests on the objective, the aesthetic on the subjective clarity of marks. The former is a clarity through concepts, the latter a clarity through intuition. The latter kind of distinctness thus consists in a mere vividness and intelligibility, i.e., in a mere clarity through examples *in concreto*, (for much can be intelligible which is nevertheless not distinct, and conversely much can be distinct which is nevertheless hard to understand, because it goes back to remote marks whose connection with intuition is possible only through a long series).",[]],"jaesche-s02-344":["Objective distinctness often causes subjective obscurity and conversely. Hence logical distinctness is not seldom possible only to the detriment of the aesthetic, and conversely aesthetic distinctness through examples and similes that do not fit exactly, but are taken only according to an analogy, often becomes harmful to logical distinctness. Moreover examples in general are also not marks and do not belong as parts to the concept, but as intuitions only to the use of the concept. A distinctness through examples, mere intelligibility, is therefore of a wholly different kind than distinctness through concepts as marks. In the combination of both, of the aesthetic or popular with the scholastic or logical distinctness, consists lucidity. For by a lucid head one thinks of the talent of a luminous presentation, adequate to the power of comprehension of the common understanding, of abstract and thorough cognitions.",[]],"jaesche-s02-345":["As concerns next in particular logical distinctness: it is to be called a complete distinctness insofar as all marks which taken together constitute the whole concept have come to clarity. A completely or *complet* distinct concept can in turn be so, either in respect of the totality of its coordinate or with regard to the totality of its subordinate marks. In the total clarity of the coordinate marks consists the extensively complete or sufficient distinctness of a concept, which is also called exhaustiveness. The total clarity of the subordinate marks constitutes the intensively complete distinctness, profundity.",[]],"jaesche-s02-346":["The former kind of logical distinctness can also be called the external completeness (*completudo externa*), just as the other, the internal completeness (*completudo interna*) of the clarity of marks. The latter can be attained only of pure concepts of reason and of arbitrary concepts, but not of empirical ones.",[]],"jaesche-s02-347":["The extensive magnitude of distinctness, insofar as it is not abundant, is called precision (measuredness). Exhaustiveness (*completudo*) and measuredness (*praecisio*) together constitute adequacy (*cognitionem, quae rem adaequat*); and in the intensively adequate cognition, in profundity, combined with the extensively adequate in exhaustiveness and precision, consists (as to quality) the consummate perfection of a cognition (*consummata cognitionis perfectio*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-348":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-349":["Since it is, as we have remarked, the business of logic to make clear concepts distinct, the question now is: In what way it makes them distinct?",[]],"jaesche-s02-350":["The logicians of the Wolffian school place all making distinct of cognitions in the mere dissection of them. However, not all distinctness rests on the analysis of a given concept. Through this it arises only in respect of those marks which we already thought in the concept, but by no means with regard to the marks which first come to be added to the concept, as parts of the whole possible concept.",[]],"jaesche-s02-351":["That kind of distinctness which arises not through analysis but through synthesis of marks is synthetic distinctness. And there is thus an essential difference between the two propositions: To make a distinct concept and to make a concept distinct.",[]],"jaesche-s02-352":["For if I make a distinct concept: then I begin from the parts and proceed from these to the whole. There are here as yet no marks present; I first obtain them through synthesis. From this synthetic procedure there thus issues synthetic distinctness, which actually extends my concept as to content through that which comes to be added beyond it in (pure or empirical) intuition as mark. — This synthetic procedure in making distinct|",[]],"jaesche-s02-353":["of concepts is employed by the mathematician and also by the natural philosopher. For all distinctness of properly mathematical as well as of all cognition of experience rests on such an extension of it through synthesis of marks.",[]],"jaesche-s02-354":["If, however, I make a concept distinct: then through this mere dissection my cognition does not grow at all as to content. This remains the same, only the form is altered, in that I learn only to distinguish better or to cognize with clearer consciousness that which already lay in the given concept. Just as through the mere illumination of a map nothing further is added to it itself: so too through the mere elucidation of a given concept by means of the analysis of its marks this concept itself is not in the least augmented.",[]],"jaesche-s02-355":["To synthesis belongs the making distinct of objects, to analysis the making distinct of concepts. Here the whole precedes the parts, there the parts precede the whole. The philosopher makes only given concepts distinct. Sometimes one proceeds synthetically, even if the concept which one wants to make distinct in this way is already given. This often occurs with propositions of experience, if one is not yet satisfied with the marks already thought in a given concept.",[]],"jaesche-s02-356":["The analytic procedure of producing distinctness, with which logic alone can occupy itself, is the first and chief requirement in the making distinct of our cognition. For the more distinct our cognition of a thing is: the stronger and more effective it can also be. Only the analysis must not go so far that the object itself in the end disappears over it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-357":["Were we conscious of everything that we know, then we would have to be astonished at the great multitude of our cognitions.",[]],"jaesche-s02-358":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-359":["In respect of the objective content of our cognition in general the following degrees can be thought, according to which it can be raised in this regard:",[]],"jaesche-s02-360":["The first degree of cognition is: to represent something to oneself;",[]],"jaesche-s02-361":["The second: to represent something to oneself with consciousness or to perceive (*percipere*);",[]],"jaesche-s02-362":["The third: to know something (*noscere*), or to represent something to oneself in comparison with other things, as to sameness as well as to difference;",[]],"jaesche-s02-363":["The fourth: to know something with consciousness, i.e., to cognize it (*cognoscere*). Animals also know objects, but they do not cognize them.",[]],"jaesche-s02-364":["The fifth: to understand something (*intelligere*), i.e., to cognize it through the understanding by means of concepts, or to conceive it. This is very different from comprehending. One can conceive much, although one cannot comprehend it, e.g., a *perpetuum mobile*, whose impossibility is shown in mechanics.",[]],"jaesche-s02-365":["The sixth: to cognize something through reason, or to have insight into it (*perspicere*). We get that far in few things, and our cognitions become ever fewer in number the more we want to perfect them in content.",[]],"jaesche-s02-366":["The seventh, finally: to comprehend something (*comprehendere*), i.e., to cognize it through reason or *a priori* to the degree that is sufficient for our aim. For all our comprehending is only relative, i.e., sufficient for a certain aim; absolutely we comprehend nothing at all. Nothing can be comprehended more than what the mathematician demonstrates, e.g., that all lines in the circle are proportional. And yet he does not comprehend: how it comes about that so simple a figure has these properties. The field of understanding or of the understanding is therefore in general far greater than the field of comprehending or of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-367":["[Introduction.] IX. D) Logical perfection of cognition as to modality.",[]],"jaesche-s02-368":["Certainty. — Concept of taking to be true in general. — Modes of taking to be true: opining, believing, knowing. — Conviction and persuasion. — Withholding and suspending a judgment. — Provisional judgments. — Prejudices, their sources and principal kinds.",[]],"jaesche-s02-369":["Truth is an objective property of cognition, the judgment through which something is represented as true; the relation to an understanding and thus to a particular subject is, subjectively, taking to be true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-370":["Taking to be true is in general of twofold kind, a certain or an uncertain one. Certain taking to be true, or certainty, is combined with consciousness of necessity, the uncertain, on the other hand, or uncertainty, with consciousness of contingency or of the possibility of the opposite. The latter is in turn either subjectively as well as objectively insufficient, or objectively insufficient but subjectively sufficient. The former is called opinion, the latter must be called belief.",[]],"jaesche-s02-371":["There are accordingly three kinds or modes of taking to be true: opining, believing, and knowing. Opining is a problematic, believing an assertoric, and knowing an apodictic judging. For what I merely opine, I take in judging, with consciousness, to be only problematic; what I believe, to be assertoric, but not as objectively, rather only as subjectively necessary (valid only for me); what, finally, I know, to be apodictically certain, i.e., universally and objectively necessary (valid for all), even supposing that the object itself to which this certain taking to be true refers were a merely empirical truth. For this distinction of taking to be true according to the three *modis* just named concerns only the power of judgment in regard to the subjective criteria of the subsumption of a judgment under objective rules.",[]],"jaesche-s02-372":["Thus, e.g., our taking immortality to be true would be merely problematic, provided we only act as if we were immortal, but assertoric, insofar as we believe that we are immortal, and apodictic, finally: insofar as we all knew that there is another life after this one.",[]],"jaesche-s02-373":["Between opining, believing, and knowing there accordingly obtains an essential difference, which we want to set out here still more exactly and in more detail.",[]],"jaesche-s02-374":["1) Opining. Opining, or taking to be true from a ground of cognition that is neither subjectively nor objectively sufficient, can be regarded as a provisional judging (*sub conditione suspensiva ad interim*), which one cannot easily do without. One must first opine before one accepts and asserts, but in doing so also guard against taking an opinion for something more than mere opinion. With opining we begin for the most part in all our cognizing. Sometimes we have an obscure presentiment of the truth, a thing seems to us to contain marks of truth; we already divine its truth even before we cognize it with determinate certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-375":["But where now does mere opining properly obtain? In no sciences that contain cognitions *a priori*, thus neither in mathematics, nor in metaphysics, nor in morals, but solely in empirical cognitions: in physics, psychology, and the like. For it is in itself absurd to opine *a priori*. Indeed, nothing could in fact be more ridiculous than, e.g., merely to opine in mathematics. Here, as in metaphysics and morals, it holds: either to know or not to know. Matters of opinion can therefore always only be objects of a cognition of experience that is in itself possible, to be sure, but impossible only for us according to the empirical restrictions and conditions of our faculty of experience and the degree of this faculty, dependent thereon, that we possess. Thus, e.g., the ether of the more recent physicists is a mere matter of opinion. For of this, as of every opinion in general, whatever it may be, I have insight: that the opposite could perhaps yet be proved. My taking to be true is thus here objectively as well as subjectively insufficient, although, considered in itself, it can become complete.",[]],"jaesche-s02-376":["2) Believing. Believing, or taking to be true from a ground that is objectively insufficient but subjectively sufficient, refers to objects in regard to which one not only can know nothing, but also can opine nothing, indeed cannot even allege probability, but can merely be certain that it is not contradictory to think such objects as one thinks them. The rest here is a free taking to be true, which is necessary only in a practical aim given *a priori*, thus a taking to be true of what I accept on moral grounds, and indeed in such a way that I am certain the opposite can never be proved.*[1]",[]],"jaesche-s02-377":["Matters of belief are thus I) not objects of empirical cognition. So-called historical belief can therefore proper|ly",[]],"jaesche-s02-378":["also not be called belief and as such be opposed to knowing, since it can itself be a knowing. Taking to be true on testimony is distinguished neither in degree nor in kind from taking to be true through one's own experience.",[]],"jaesche-s02-379":["II) also not objects of rational cognition (cognition *a priori*), neither of theoretical, e.g., in mathematics and metaphysics, nor of practical, in morals.",[]],"jaesche-s02-380":["Mathematical truths of reason one can indeed believe on testimony, because error here is partly not easily possible, partly also can easily be discovered, but one cannot in this way know them. Philosophical truths of reason, however, cannot even be believed; they must solely be known; for philosophy suffers in itself no mere persuasion. And as concerns in particular the objects of practical rational cognition in morals, rights and duties: in regard to these a mere believing can just as little obtain. One must be fully certain: whether something is right or wrong, in conformity with duty or contrary to duty, permitted or not permitted. In moral things one can risk nothing on the uncertain, resolve nothing at the risk of transgressing the law. Thus, e.g., it is not enough for the judge that he merely believe that the one accused of a crime actually committed this crime. He must know it (juridically), or acts unconscionably.",[]],"jaesche-s02-381":["III) Only those objects are matters of belief in which the taking to be true is necessarily free, i.e., not determined through objective grounds of truth independent of the nature and the interest of the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s02-382":["Believing therefore also, because of the merely subjective grounds, yields no conviction that can be communicated and commands universal assent, like the conviction that comes from knowing. I myself can only be certain of the validity and unalterability of my practical belief, and my belief in the truth of a proposition or the actuality of a thing is that which, in relation to me, only takes the place of a cognition, without itself being a cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-383":["Morally unbelieving is he who does not accept that which it is indeed impossible to know, but morally necessary to presuppose. At the ground of this kind of unbelief there always lies lack of moral interest. The greater the moral disposition of a human being: the firmer and livelier will also be his belief in all that which he feels himself necessitated to accept and presuppose from moral interest in a practically necessary aim.",[]],"jaesche-s02-384":["3) Knowing. Taking to be true from a ground of cognition that is objectively as well as subjectively sufficient, or certainty, is either empirical or rational, according as it is grounded either on experience — one's own as well as that of others communicated — or on reason. This distinction thus refers to the two sources from which our entire cognition is drawn: experience and reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-385":["Rational certainty is in turn either mathematical or philosophical certainty. The former is intuitive, the latter discursive.",[]],"jaesche-s02-386":["Mathematical certainty is also called evidence, because an intuitive cognition is clearer than a discursive one. Although, then, both, mathematical and philosophical rational cognition, are in themselves equally certain: yet the kind of certainty in the two is different.",[]],"jaesche-s02-387":["Empirical certainty is an original one (*originarie empirica*), insofar as I become certain of something from my own experience, and a derived one (*derivative empirica*), insofar as I become certain of something through the experience of others. This latter is usually also called historical certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-388":["Rational certainty is distinguished from empirical by the consciousness of necessity that is combined with it; it is thus an apodictic, the empirical, on the other hand, only an assertoric certainty. One is rationally certain of what one would have had insight into *a priori* even without any experience. Our cognitions can therefore concern objects of experience, and the certainty of them can yet be empirical and rational at the same time, insofar, namely, as we cognize an empirically certain proposition from principles *a priori*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-389":["Rational certainty we cannot have of everything, but where we can have it, we must prefer it to the empirical.",[]],"jaesche-s02-390":["All certainty is either an unmediated or a mediated one, i.e., it either requires a proof, or is capable of and in need of no proof. However much in our cognition may be certain only mediately, i.e., only through a proof: still there must also be something indemonstrable or immediately certain, and our entire cognition must proceed from immediately certain propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s02-391":["The proofs on which all mediated or mediate certainty of a cognition rests are either direct or indirect, i.e., apagogic proofs. When I prove a truth from its grounds, I conduct a direct proof for it, and when I infer from the falsity of the opposite to the truth of a proposition, an apagogic one. If this latter is to have validity, however, the propositions must be opposed to one another contradictorily or *diametraliter*. For two propositions merely contrarily opposed to one another (*contrarie opposita*) can both be false. A proof that is the ground of mathematical certainty is called demonstration, and that which is the ground of philosophical certainty, an acroamatic proof. The essential pieces of every proof in general are its matter and its form, or the ground of proof and the consequence.",[]],"jaesche-s02-392":["From knowing comes science, by which is to be understood the sum total of a cognition as system. It is opposed to common cognition, i.e., to the sum total of a cognition as mere aggregate. The system rests on an idea of the whole, which precedes the parts; in common cognition, on the other hand, or the mere aggregate of cognitions, the parts precede the whole. There are historical sciences and sciences of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s02-393":["In a science we often know only the cognitions, but not the things represented by them; thus there can be a science of that of which our cognition is no knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-394":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-395":["From the previous remarks on the nature and the kinds of taking to be true we can now draw the general result: that all our conviction, then, is either logical or practical. Namely, when we know that we are free of all subjective grounds and yet the taking to be true is sufficient, then we are convinced, and indeed convinced logically or from objective grounds (the object is certain).",[]],"jaesche-s02-396":["Complete taking to be true from subjective grounds, which in practical relation are as valid as objective ones, is however also conviction, only not logical but practical (I am certain). And this practical conviction or this moral belief of reason is often firmer than all knowing. In knowing one still listens to counter-grounds, but in believing not, because here it depends not on objective grounds but on the moral interest of the subject.*[2]",[]],"jaesche-s02-397":["Opposed to conviction is persuasion, a taking to be true from insufficient grounds of which one does not know whether they are merely subjective or also objective.",[]],"jaesche-s02-398":["Persuasion often precedes conviction. We are conscious of many cognitions only in such a way that we cannot judge whether the grounds of our taking to be true are objective or subjective. We must therefore, in order to be able to get from mere persuasion to conviction, first of all reflect, i.e., see to which cognitive power a cognition belongs, and then investigate, i.e., examine whether the grounds are sufficient or insufficient in regard to the object. With many it remains at persuasion. With some it comes to reflection, with few to investigation. He who knows what belongs to certainty will not easily confuse persuasion and conviction, and thus also will not easily let himself be persuaded. There is a determining ground for assent that is composed of objective and subjective grounds, and most human beings do not separate this mixed effect.",[]],"jaesche-s02-399":["Although every persuasion is false as to form (*formaliter*), insofar, namely, as here an uncertain cognition seems to be certain: yet it can be true as to matter (*materialiter*). And so it is distinguished also from opinion, which is an uncertain cognition insofar as it is held to be uncertain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-400":["The sufficiency of taking to be true (in belief) can be put to the test through betting or through swearing. For the first, comparative, for the second, absolute sufficiency of objective grounds is necessary, in place of which, if they are not present, a taking to be true that is absolutely subjectively sufficient is nonetheless valid.",[]],"jaesche-s02-401":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-402":["One is often accustomed to make use of the expressions: to concur with one's judgment, to withhold, suspend, or give up one's judgment. These and similar locutions seem to indicate that in our judging there is something voluntary, in that we take something to be true because we want to take it to be true. The question here is accordingly: Whether willing has an influence on our judgments?",[]],"jaesche-s02-403":["Immediately the will has no influence on taking to be true; this would also be very absurd. When it is said: We gladly believe",[]],"jaesche-s02-404":["what we wish, this signifies only our good-natured wishes, e.g., those of the father concerning his children. If the will had an immediate influence on our conviction of what we wish: then we would constantly make for ourselves chimeras of a happy state and then also always take them to be true. But the will cannot contend against convincing proofs of truths that are contrary to its wishes and inclinations.",[]],"jaesche-s02-405":["Insofar, however, as the will either impels the understanding to the inquiry into a truth or holds it back from it, one must concede to it an influence on the use of the understanding and hence also mediately on conviction itself, since this depends so much on the use of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-406":["As concerns in particular, however, the suspension or withholding of our judgment: it consists in the resolve not to let a merely provisional judgment become a determining one. A provisional judgment is one through which I represent to myself that there are, to be sure, more grounds for the truth of a thing than against it, but that these grounds do not yet suffice for a determining or definitive judgment, through which I decide straightforwardly for the truth. Provisional judging is thus a judging that is, with consciousness, merely problematic.",[]],"jaesche-s02-407":["The withholding of judgment can occur with a twofold aim: either in order to seek out the grounds of the determining judgment, or in order never to judge. In the former case the suspension of judgment is called a critical one (*suspensio judicii indagatoria*), in the latter a skeptical one (*suspensio judicii sceptica*). For the skeptic renounces all judging, the true philosopher, on the other hand, merely suspends his judgment as long as he does not yet have sufficient grounds to take something to be true.",[]],"jaesche-s02-408":["To suspend one's judgment according to maxims, for this a practiced power of judgment is required, which is found only with advancing age. In general the withholding of our assent is a very difficult thing, partly because our understanding is so eager to extend itself through judging and to enrich itself with knowledge, partly because our propensity is always directed more to certain things than to others. But he who has often had to take back his assent and has thereby become prudent and cautious will not give it so quickly, for fear of having to take his judgment back a|gain",[]],"jaesche-s02-409":["later. This retraction is always a mortification and a cause for placing a mistrust in all other knowledge.",[]],"jaesche-s02-410":["We further remark here: that it is something different to leave one's judgment *in dubio* than to leave it *in suspenso*. In the latter I always have an interest in the thing, in the former it is not always in accordance with my end and interest to decide whether the thing be true or not.",[]],"jaesche-s02-411":["Provisional judgments are very necessary, indeed indispensable, for the use of the understanding in all meditating and investigating. For they serve to guide the understanding in its inquiries and to put various means at its disposal for this.",[]],"jaesche-s02-412":["When we meditate on an object, we must always already judge provisionally and as it were already scent the cognition that will fall to our share through the meditation. And when one sets out on inventions or discoveries, one must always make oneself a provisional plan, otherwise the thoughts go merely at random. One can therefore think under provisional judgments maxims for the investigation of a thing. One could also call them anticipations, because one already anticipates one's judgment of a thing even before one has the determining one. Such judgments thus have their good use, and rules could even be given about how we should judge provisionally about an object.",[]],"jaesche-s02-413":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-414":["From provisional judgments prejudices must be distinguished.",[]],"jaesche-s02-415":["Prejudices are provisional judgments insofar as they are accepted as principles. Every prejudice is to be regarded as a principle of erroneous judgments, and from prejudices there arise not prejudices but erroneous judgments. One must therefore distinguish the false cognition that arises from the prejudice from its source, the prejudice itself. Thus, e.g., the significance of dreams is in itself not a prejudice, but an error that arises from the accepted universal rule: What happens several times happens always, or is always to be taken to be true. And this principle, under which the significance of dreams also belongs, is a prejudice.",[]],"jaesche-s02-416":["Sometimes prejudices are true provisional judgments; only that they count for us as principles or as determining judgments is wrong. The cause of this deception is to be sought in this, that subjective grounds are falsely held to be objective, from lack of reflection, which must precede all judging. For even if we can accept many a cognition, e.g., immediately certain propositions, without investigating them, i.e., without examining the conditions of their truth: still we can and may judge about nothing without reflecting, i.e., without comparing a cognition with the cognitive power from which it is supposed to arise (sensibility or the understanding). If we now accept judgments without this reflection, which is necessary even where no investigation takes place: then from this arise prejudices, or principles for judging from subjective causes that are falsely held to be objective grounds.",[]],"jaesche-s02-417":["The principal sources of prejudices are: imitation, habit, and inclination.",[]],"jaesche-s02-418":["Imitation has a universal influence on our judgments, for it is a strong ground to take to be true that which others have passed off as such. Hence the prejudice: what all the world does is right. As concerns the prejudices that have arisen from habit, they can be rooted out only through length of time, in that the understanding, held up and delayed in judging little by little by counter-grounds, is thereby gradually brought to an opposed way of thinking. But if a prejudice of habit has at the same time arisen through imitation: then the human being who possesses it is hardly to be cured of it. A prejudice from imitation can also be called the propensity to the passive use of reason, or to the mechanism of reason instead of its spontaneity under laws.",[]],"jaesche-s02-419":["Reason is indeed an active principle, which is to borrow nothing from the mere authority of others, not even, when it concerns its pure use, from experience. But the indolence of very many human beings brings it about that they would rather tread in others' footsteps than exert their own powers of understanding. Such human beings can always only become copies of others, and were all of that kind, the world would remain eternally on one and the same spot. It is therefore most necessary and important: not to hold youth, as usually happens, to mere imitating.",[]],"jaesche-s02-420":["There are so many things that contribute to accustoming us to the maxim of imitation and thereby to making reason a fertile soil of prejudices. To such aids of imitation belong",[]],"jaesche-s02-421":["1) Formulas. These are rules whose expression serves as the model for imitation. They are, moreover, uncommonly useful for relief in complicated propositions, and the most enlightened head therefore seeks to invent such.",[]],"jaesche-s02-422":["2) Sayings, whose expression has a great measuredness of a pregnant sense, so that it seems one could not encompass the sense with fewer words. Such pronouncements (*dicta*), which must always be borrowed from others to whom one ascribes a certain infallibility, serve, for the sake of this authority, as rule and as law. The pronouncements of the Bible are called sayings κατ' ἐξοχην.",[]],"jaesche-s02-423":["3) Sentences, i.e., propositions that recommend themselves and often preserve their prestige through centuries, as products of a mature power of judgment, through the emphasis of the thoughts that lie in them.",[]],"jaesche-s02-424":["4) *Canones*. These are universal doctrinal sayings that serve the sciences as foundation and indicate something sublime and thought-through. One can further express them in a sententious way, so that they please all the more.",[]],"jaesche-s02-425":["5) Proverbs (*proverbia*). These are popular rules of the common understanding, or expressions for designating its popular judgments. Since such merely provincial propositions serve only the common rabble as sentences and canons: they are not to be met with among people of finer upbringing.",[]],"jaesche-s02-426":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-427":["From the three universal sources of prejudices indicated above, and in particular also from imitation, there now arise so many particular prejudices, among which we want here to touch on the following, as the most usual.",[]],"jaesche-s02-428":["1) Prejudices of prestige. To these are to be reckoned:",[]],"jaesche-s02-429":["a) the prejudice of the prestige of the person. When in things that rest on experience and testimonies we build our cognition on the prestige of other persons: we do not thereby make ourselves guilty of any prejudices, for in matters of this kind, since we cannot experience everything ourselves and encompass it with our own understanding, the prestige of the person must be the foundation of our judgments. But when we make the prestige of others the ground of our taking to be true with regard to cognitions of reason: then we accept these cognitions on mere prejudice. For truths of reason are valid anonymously; here the question is not: Who said it, but What did he say? It matters nothing whether a cognition is of noble descent; but nevertheless the propensity to the prestige of great men is very common, partly because of the limitedness of one's own insight, partly from the desire to imitate that which is described to us as great. To this is added further: that the prestige of the person serves to flatter our vanity in an indirect way. Just as, namely, the subjects of a mighty despot are proud that they are all treated equally by him, in that the lowliest can fancy himself equal to the most eminent insofar as they are both nothing against the unlimited power of their ruler, so the admirers of a great man also judge themselves as equal, insofar as the advantages that they may have among themselves, considered against the merits of the great man, are to be esteemed insignificant. The highly praised great men therefore, for more than one reason, give no small assistance to the propensity to the prejudice of the prestige of the person.",[]],"jaesche-s02-430":["b) The prejudice of the prestige of the multitude. To this prejudice the rabble chiefly is inclined. For since it is not able to judge the merits, the capacities, and the knowledge of the person: it rather holds to the judgment of the multitude, under the presupposition that what all say must surely be true. Meanwhile this prejudice refers with it only to historical things; in matters of religion, in which it is itself interested, it relies on the judgment of the learned.",[]],"jaesche-s02-431":["It is in general remarkable that the ignorant one has a prejudice for learnedness, and the learned one, on the other hand, in turn a prejudice for the common understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s02-432":["When, for the learned one, after he has already run through the circle of the sciences fairly well, all his efforts do not procure the proper satisfaction: then he at last acquires a mis|trust",[]],"jaesche-s02-433":["of learnedness, in particular in regard to such speculations where the concepts cannot be made sensible and whose foundations are wavering, as, e.g., in metaphysics. But since he still believes the key to certainty about certain objects must be found somewhere: he now seeks it in the common understanding, after he had sought it so long in vain on the path of scientific inquiry.",[]],"jaesche-s02-434":["But this hope is very deceptive, for if the cultivated faculty of reason can accomplish nothing with regard to the cognition of certain things, then the uncultivated one surely will just as little. In metaphysics the appeal to the pronouncements of the common understanding is everywhere wholly inadmissible, because here no case can be exhibited *in concreto*. With morals, however, the matter is of course otherwise. Not only can all rules in morals be given *in concreto*, but practical reason also reveals itself in general more clearly and correctly through the organ of the common than through that of the speculative use of the understanding. Hence the common understanding often judges more correctly about matters of morality and duty than the speculative.",[]],"jaesche-s02-435":["c) The prejudice of the prestige of the age. Here the prejudice of antiquity is one of the most significant. We certainly have ground to judge favorably of antiquity, but that is only a ground for a moderate respect, whose bounds we all too often overstep by making the ancients treasurers of cognitions and sciences, raising the relative worth of their writings to an absolute one, and entrusting ourselves blindly to their guidance. To esteem the ancients so excessively means: to lead the understanding back into its childhood years and to neglect the use of one's very own talent. We would also err greatly if we believed that all from antiquity had written as classically as those whose writings have come down to us. Since, namely, time sifts everything and only that preserves itself which has an inner worth: we may not without ground assume that we possess only the best writings of the ancients.",[]],"jaesche-s02-436":["There are several causes through which the prejudice of antiquity is produced and maintained.",[]],"jaesche-s02-437":["When something exceeds the expectation according to a universal rule: then one is at first surprised at it, and this surprise then often passes over into admiration. This is the case with the ancients, when one finds with them something that one, in consideration of the circumstances of the time under which they lived, did not look for. Another cause lies in the circumstance that knowledge of the ancients and of antiquity proves a learnedness and erudition that always acquires respect, however common and insignificant the things in themselves may be that one has drawn from the study of the ancients. A third cause is the gratitude that we owe the ancients for having broken the path for us to much knowledge. It seems to be fair to show them a special high esteem for this, whose measure, however, we often overstep. A fourth cause, finally, is to be sought in a certain envy toward contemporaries. He who cannot compete with the moderns extols the ancients at their expense, so that the moderns cannot raise themselves above him.",[]],"jaesche-s02-438":["The opposite of this is the prejudice of novelty. At times the prestige of antiquity and the prejudice in its favor fell, in particular at the beginning of this century, when the famous Fontenelle took the side of the moderns. In cognitions that are capable of extension, it is very natural that we place more trust in the moderns than in the ancients. But this judgment also has ground only as a merely provisional judgment. If we make it a determining one: then it becomes prejudice.",[]],"jaesche-s02-439":["2) Prejudices from self-love or logical egoism, according to which one holds the agreement of one's own judgment with the judgments of others to be a dispensable criterion of truth. They are opposed to the prejudices of prestige, since they express themselves in a certain predilection for that which is a product of one's own understanding, e.g., of one's own doctrinal edifice.",[]],"jaesche-s02-440":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-441":["Whether it is good and advisable to let prejudices stand or even to favor them? It is astonishing that in our age su|ch",[]],"jaesche-s02-442":["questions, especially that concerning the favoring of prejudices, can still be posed. To favor someone's prejudices means as much as to deceive someone with a good intention. To leave prejudices untouched would still be acceptable; for who can occupy himself with uncovering and removing everyone's prejudices. But whether it should not be advisable to work with all one's powers at their rooting out, that is surely another question. Old and deep-rooted prejudices are of course hard to combat, because they answer for themselves and are as it were their own judges. One also seeks to excuse the letting stand of prejudices by saying that disadvantages would arise from their rooting out. But let one just always admit these disadvantages; in the sequel they will bring all the more good. Notes:",[]],"jaesche-s02-443":["*[1] Believing is not a special source of cognition. It is a kind of taking to be true that is, with consciousness, incomplete, and it is distinguished, when it is considered as restricted to a particular kind of objects (which belong only to believing), from opining not by degree but by the relation that it has as cognition to acting. Thus, e.g., the merchant, in order to strike a deal, needs not merely to opine that there will be something to gain by it, but //IX68// to believe it, i.e., that his opinion be sufficient for the undertaking on the uncertain. Now we have theoretical cognitions (of the sensible) in which we can bring it to certainty, and in regard to all that which we can call human cognition the latter must be possible. Just such certain cognitions, and indeed wholly *a priori*, we have in practical laws, but these are grounded on a supersensible principle (of freedom), and indeed in ourselves, as a principle of practical reason. But this practical reason is a causality in regard to a likewise supersensible object, the highest good, which is not possible in the world of sense through our faculty. Nevertheless nature, as object of our theoretical reason, must harmonize with it, for the sequel or effect of this idea is to be met with in the world of sense. We are thus to act in order to make this end actual.",[]],"jaesche-s02-444":["We find in the world of sense also traces of an artistic wisdom, and now we believe: the cause of the world acts also with moral wisdom toward the highest good. This is a taking to be true which is enough for acting, i.e., a belief. Now we do not need this for acting according to moral laws, for these are given through practical reason alone, but we need the assumption of a highest wisdom for the object of our moral will, toward which, besides the mere lawfulness of our actions, we cannot avoid directing our ends. Although this would objectively be no necessary relation of our power of choice: still the highest good is subjectively necessarily the object of a good (even human) will, and the belief in its attainability is necessarily presupposed for this.",[]],"jaesche-s02-445":["Between the acquisition of a cognition through experience (*a posteriori*) and through reason (*a priori*) there is no middle. But between the cognition of an object and the mere presupposition of its possibility there is a middle, namely an empirical ground or a ground of reason for assuming the latter in relation to a necessary extension of the field of possible objects beyond those whose cognition is possible for us. This necessity takes place only in regard to that where the object is cognized as practical and as practically necessary through reason, for to assume something for the sake of the mere extension of theoretical cognition is always contingent. This practically necessary presupposition of an object is that of the possibility of the highest good as object of the power of choice, hence also of the condition of this possibility (God, freedom and immortality). This is a subjective necessity of assuming the reality of the object for the sake of the necessary determination of the will. This is the *casus extraordinarius* without which practical reason cannot maintain itself in regard to its necessary end, and here *favor necessitatis* comes to its aid in its own judgment. It can acquire no object logically, but can only //IX69// oppose solely that which hinders it in the use of this idea, which belongs to it practically.",[]],"jaesche-s02-446":["This belief is the necessity of assuming the objective reality of a concept (of the highest good), i.e., the possibility of its object, as an object of the power of choice necessary *a priori*. If we look merely to actions, then we do not need this belief. But if we want through actions to extend ourselves to the possession of the end possible thereby: then we must assume that this is thoroughly possible. I can therefore only say: I see myself necessitated through my end according to laws of freedom to assume a highest good in the world as possible, but I can necessitate no other through grounds (belief is free).",[]],"jaesche-s02-447":["Rational belief can therefore never pertain to theoretical cognition, for there the objectively insufficient taking to be true is mere opinion. It is merely a presupposition of reason in a subjective, but absolutely necessary practical aim. The disposition according to moral laws leads to an object of the power of choice determinable through pure reason. The assuming of the feasibility of this object and thus also of the actuality of the cause for it is a moral belief or a free taking to be true, necessary in a moral aim for the completion of one's ends.",[]],"jaesche-s02-448":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-449":["*Fides* is properly fidelity in the *pacto* or subjective trust in one another, that the one will keep his promise to the other, fidelity and faith. The first when the *pactum* is made, the second when one is to conclude it.",[]],"jaesche-s02-450":["According to the analogy, practical reason is as it were the *Promittent*, the human being the *Promissarius*, the good expected from the deed the *Promissum*.",[]],"jaesche-s02-451":["*[2] This practical conviction is therefore the moral rational belief, which alone in the most proper sense must be called a belief and as such be opposed to knowledge and to all theoretical or logical conviction in general, because it can never raise itself to knowledge. The so-called historical belief, on the contrary, may not, as already remarked, be distinguished from knowledge, since, as a kind of theoretical or logical taking to be true, it can itself be a knowledge. We can with the same certainty accept an empirical truth on the testimony of others as if we had arrived at it through *facta* of our own experience. In the former kind of empirical knowledge there is something deceptive, but also in the latter.",[]],"jaesche-s02-452":["Historical or mediate empirical knowledge rests on the reliability of testimonies. To the requirements of an unimpeachable witness belong: authenticity (competence) and integrity.",[]],"jaesche-s02-453":["[Introduction.] X. Probability.",[]],"jaesche-s02-454":["— Definition of the probable. — Distinction of probability from plausibility. — Mathematical and philosophical probability. — Doubt — subjective and objective. — Skeptical, dogmatic and critical way of thinking or method of philosophizing. — Hypotheses.",[]],"jaesche-s02-455":["To the doctrine of the certainty of our cognition belongs also the doctrine of the cognition of the probable, which is to be regarded as an approximation to certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-456":["By probability is to be understood a taking to be true from insufficient grounds, which however have a greater relation to the sufficient ones than the grounds of the opposite. Through this definition we distinguish probability (*probabilitas*) from mere plausibility (*verisimilitudo*), a taking to be true from insufficient grounds insofar as these are greater than the grounds of the opposite.",[]],"jaesche-s02-457":["The ground of taking to be true can namely be either objectively or subjectively greater than that of the opposite. Which of the two it is, one can find out only by comparing the grounds of taking to be true with the sufficient ones; for then the grounds of taking to be true are greater than the grounds of the opposite can be. In probability, therefore, the ground of taking to be true is objectively valid, in mere plausibility, on the contrary, only subjectively valid. Plausibility is merely magnitude of persuasion, probability is an approximation to certainty. In probability there must always be a standard according to which I can estimate it. This standard is certainty. For since I am to compare the insufficient grounds with the sufficient ones, I must know how much belongs to certainty. Such a standard, however, falls away in mere plausibility, since here I compare the insufficient grounds not with the sufficient ones, but only with the grounds of the opposite.",[]],"jaesche-s02-458":["The moments of probability can be either homogeneous or heterogeneous. If they are homogeneous, as in mathematical cognition: then they must be numbered; if they are heterogeneous, as in philosophical cognition: then they must be pondered, i.e., estimated according to the effect; this, however, according to the overcoming of the hindrances in the mind. The latter give no relation to certainty, but only of one plausibility to another. From this it follows: that only the mathematician can determine the relation of insufficient grounds to the sufficient ground, the philosopher must content himself with plausibility, a taking to be true that is merely subjectively and practically adequate. For in philosophical cognition, because of the heterogeneity of the grounds, probability cannot be estimated; the weights here are, so to speak, not all stamped. Of mathematical probability one can therefore also properly only say: that it is more than half of certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-459":["Much has been said of a logic of probability (*logica probabilium*). But this is not possible; for if the relation of the insufficient grounds to the sufficient one cannot be weighed mathematically: then all rules are of no help. Nor can one give any universal rules of probability at all, except that error will not fall on one and the same side, but a ground of agreement must be in the object; likewise: that if there is erring from two opposite sides in equal amount and in equal degree, the truth is in the middle.",[]],"jaesche-s02-460":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-461":["Doubt is a counter-ground or a mere hindrance to taking to be true, which can be considered either subjectively or objectively. Subjectively, namely, doubt is sometimes taken as a state of an undecided mind, and objectively as the cognition of the inadequacy of the grounds for taking to be true. In the latter respect it is called an objection, that is: an objective ground for holding a cognition taken to be true to be false.",[]],"jaesche-s02-462":["A merely subjectively valid counter-ground of taking to be true is a scruple. With a scruple one does not know: whether the hindrance to taking to be true is grounded objectively or only subjectively, e.g., only in inclination, habit and the like. One doubts without being able to declare oneself distinctly and determinately about the ground of doubting and without being able to have insight: whether this ground lies in the object itself or only in the subject. Now if such scruples are to be able to be removed: then they must be raised to the distinctness and determinateness of an objection. For through objections certainty is brought to distinctness and completeness, and no one can be certain of a matter unless counter-grounds have been stirred up, through which it can be determined: how far one is still removed from certainty, or how near one still is to it. Nor is it enough: that each doubt merely be answered, one must also resolve it, that is: make comprehensible how the scruple arose. If this does not happen: then the doubt is only rejected, but not cancelled, the seed of doubting then always still remains. In many cases, to be sure, we cannot know: whether the hindrance to taking to be true in us has only subjective or objective grounds and thus cannot lift the scruple through uncovering of the illusion, since we cannot always compare our cognitions with the object, but often only among one another. It is therefore modesty to put forward one's objections only as doubts.",[]],"jaesche-s02-463":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-464":["There is a principle of doubting, which consists in the maxim of treating cognitions with the aim of making them uncertain and showing the impossibility of arriving at certainty. This method of philosophizing is the skeptical way of thinking or skepticism. It is op|posed to the dogmatic way of thinking or dogmatism,",[]],"jaesche-s02-465":["which is a blind trust in the faculty of reason to extend itself *a priori* through mere concepts without critique, merely for the sake of apparent success.",[]],"jaesche-s02-466":["Both methods are, if they become universal, faulty. For there are many cognitions in regard to which we cannot proceed dogmatically, and on the other side skepticism, by renouncing all assertoric cognition, destroys all our efforts to arrive at the possession of a cognition of the certain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-467":["But however harmful this skepticism is: so useful and purposive is nevertheless the skeptical method, provided one understands by it nothing further than only the way of treating something as uncertain and bringing it to the highest uncertainty, in the hope of coming upon the track of truth in this way. This method is therefore properly a mere suspension of judging. It is very useful to the critical procedure, by which is to be understood that method of philosophizing according to which one investigates the sources of one's assertions or objections, and the grounds on which they rest; a method which gives hope of arriving at certainty.",[]],"jaesche-s02-468":["In mathematics and physics skepticism does not take place. Only that cognition has been able to occasion it which is neither mathematical nor empirical: the purely philosophical. Absolute skepticism passes everything off as illusion. It therefore distinguishes illusion from truth and must hence after all have a mark of the distinction, consequently presuppose a cognition of truth, whereby it contradicts itself.",[]],"jaesche-s02-469":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s02-470":["We remarked above of probability that it is a mere approximation to certainty. This is now in particular also the case with hypotheses, through which we can never arrive at an apodictic certainty, but always only at a now greater, now lesser degree of probability in our cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-471":["A hypothesis is a taking to be true of the judgment of the truth of a ground for the sake of the adequacy of the consequences, or more briefly: the taking to be true of a presupposition as ground.",[]],"jaesche-s02-472":["All taking to be true in hypotheses is accordingly grounded on this, that the presupposition, as ground, is adequate to explain other cognitions, as consequences, from it. For we infer here from the truth of the consequence to the truth of the ground. But since this kind of inference, as already remarked above, gives an adequate criterion of truth and can lead to an apodictic certainty only when all possible consequences of an assumed ground are true: it is evident from this that, since we can never determine all possible consequences, hypotheses always remain hypotheses, that is: presuppositions to whose complete certainty we can never arrive. Notwithstanding, the probability of a hypothesis can still grow and raise itself to an *analogon* of certainty, namely when all consequences that have so far come before us can be explained from the presupposed ground. For in such a case there is no ground why we should not assume that all possible consequences will be able to be explained from it. We therefore surrender ourselves in this case to the hypothesis as if it were completely certain, although it is so only through induction.",[]],"jaesche-s02-473":["And something must after all also be apodictically certain in every hypothesis, namely",[]],"jaesche-s02-474":["1) the possibility of the presupposition itself. If, e.g., for the explanation of earthquakes and volcanoes we assume a subterranean fire: then such a fire must after all be possible, if not exactly as a flaming body, yet as a hot one. But for the sake of certain other appearances to make the earth into an animal in which the circulation of the inner juices effects the warmth is to put forward a mere fiction and no hypothesis. For actualities can well be fabricated, but not possibilities; these must be certain.",[]],"jaesche-s02-475":["2) The consequence. From the assumed ground the consequences must flow correctly; otherwise the hypothesis becomes a mere chimera.",[]],"jaesche-s02-476":["3) The unity. It is an essential requirement of a hypothesis that it be only One and need no auxiliary hypotheses for its support. If in a hypothesis we must already take several others to our aid: then it thereby loses very much of its probability. For the more consequences can be derived from a hypothesis, the more probable it is, the fewer, the more improbable. Thus, e.g., the hypothesis of Tycho de Brahe did not suffice for the explanation of many appearances; he therefore assumed for supplementation several new hypo|theses.",[]],"jaesche-s02-477":["Here it is now already to be guessed that the assumed hypothesis cannot be the genuine ground. On the other hand the Copernican system is a hypothesis from which everything that is to be explained from it, so far as it has so far come before us, can be explained. We need here no auxiliary hypotheses (*hypotheses subsidiarias*).",[]],"jaesche-s02-478":["There are sciences which permit no hypotheses, as, e.g., mathematics and metaphysics. But in the doctrine of nature they are useful and indispensable.",[]],"jaesche-s02-479":["[Introduction.] Appendix.",[]],"jaesche-s02-480":["On the distinction between theoretical and practical cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-481":["A cognition is called practical in opposition to theoretical, but also in opposition to speculative cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s02-482":["Practical cognitions are namely either:",[]],"jaesche-s02-483":["1) imperatives and to that extent opposed to theoretical cognitions; or they contain",[]],"jaesche-s02-484":["2) the grounds for possible imperatives and are to that extent opposed to speculative cognitions.",[]],"jaesche-s02-485":["By imperative in general is to be understood every proposition which states a possible free action whereby a certain end is to be made actual. Every cognition, therefore, which contains imperatives is practical, and indeed is to be called practical in opposition to theoretical cognition. For theoretical cognitions are those which state: not what ought to be, but what is, thus have not an acting but a being for their object.",[]],"jaesche-s02-486":["If, on the other hand, we oppose practical cognitions to speculative ones: then they can also be theoretical, provided only that imperatives can be derived from them. They are then, considered in this respect, practical as to content (*in potentia*) or objectively. By speculative cognitions, namely, we understand those from which no rules of conduct can be derived, or which contain no grounds for possible imperatives. Of such merely speculative propositions there are, e.g., in theology a multitude. Such speculative cognitions are therefore always theoretical, but not con|versely",[]],"jaesche-s02-487":["is every theoretical cognition speculative; it can, considered in another respect, also at the same time be practical.",[]],"jaesche-s02-488":["Everything in the end comes down to the practical, and in this tendency of everything theoretical and of all speculation in regard to its use consists the practical worth of our cognition. This worth is, however, an unconditioned one only when the end toward which the practical use of the cognition is directed is an unconditioned end. The sole, unconditioned and final end (ultimate end) to which all practical use of our cognition must in the end relate is morality, which for that reason we also call the simply or absolutely practical. And that part of philosophy which has morality for its object would accordingly have to be called practical philosophy κατ' ἐξοχην; although every other philosophical science can always also have its practical part, i.e., can contain, from the theories put forward, an instruction for their practical use for the realization of certain ends.",[]],"jaesche-s02-489":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-001":["I. Universal Doctrine of Elements.",[]],"jaesche-s03-002":["First Section. On Concepts. [§§ 1 – 16]",[]],"jaesche-s03-003":["§. 1. Concept in general and its distinction from intuition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-004":["All cognitions, that is: all representations related with consciousness to an object, are either intuitions or concepts. The intuition is a singular representation (*repraesentatio singularis*), the concept a universal (*repraesentatio per notas communes*) or reflected representation (*repraesentatio discursiva*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-005":["Cognition through concepts is called thinking (*cognitio discursiva*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-006":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-007":["Remark 1. The concept is opposed to intuition, for it is a universal representation or a representation of that which is common to several objects, thus a representation insofar as it can be contained in various ones.",[]],"jaesche-s03-008":["2. It is a mere tautology to speak of universal or common concepts, — a mistake which is grounded on an incorrect division of concepts into universal, particular and singular. Not the concepts themselves, only their use can be so divided.",[]],"jaesche-s03-009":["§. 2. Matter and form of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-010":["In every concept matter and form are to be distinguished. The matter of concepts is the object, their form universality.",[]],"jaesche-s03-011":["§. 3. Empirical and pure concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-012":["The concept is either an empirical or a pure concept (*vel empiricus vel intellectualis*). A pure concept is one which is not abstracted from experience, but arises from the understanding also as to content.",[]],"jaesche-s03-013":["The idea is a concept of reason, whose object can not at all be encountered in experience.",[]],"jaesche-s03-014":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-015":["Remark 1. The empirical concept arises from the senses through comparison of the objects of experience and receives through the understanding merely the form of universality. The reality of these concepts rests on actual experience, from which they are drawn, as to their content. But whether there are pure concepts of the understanding (*conceptus puri*) which, as such, arise independently of all experience solely from the understanding, metaphysics must investigate.",[]],"jaesche-s03-016":["2. The concepts of reason or ideas can not at all lead to actual objects, because these must all be contained in a possible experience. But they nevertheless serve to guide the understanding through reason in regard to experience and the use of its rules in the greatest perfection, or also to show that not all possible things are objects of experience, and that the principles of the possibility of the latter do not hold of things in themselves, nor of objects of experience as things in themselves.",[]],"jaesche-s03-017":["The idea contains the archetype of the use of the understanding, e.g., the idea of the world-whole, which must be necessary, not as constitutive principle for the empirical use of the understanding, but only as regulative principle for the sake of the thoroughgoing interconnection of our empirical use of the understanding. It is therefore to be regarded as a necessary basic concept, in order either to complete objectively the actions of the understanding of subordination or to regard them as unbounded. — Nor can the idea be obtained through composition, for the whole is prior to the part. Meanwhile there are nevertheless ideas to which an approximation takes place. This is the case with the mathematical ideas, or the ideas of the mathematical generation of a whole, which are essentially distinguished from the dynamical ones, which are wholly heterogeneous to all concrete concepts, because the whole is distinct from the concrete concepts not as to magnitude (as with the mathematical ones), but as to kind.",[]],"jaesche-s03-018":["One can procure objective reality for no theoretical idea or prove it, except only for the idea of freedom, and indeed because this is the condition of the moral law, whose reality is an axiom. The reality of the idea of God can be proved only through this and thus only in a practical aim, i.e., to act as if there were a God, thus only for this aim.",[]],"jaesche-s03-019":["In all sciences, chiefly those of reason, the idea of the science is the universal sketch or outline of it, thus the domain of all cognitions which belong to it. Such an idea of the whole — the first thing one has to look to in a science and that one has to seek, is architectonic, as, e.g., the idea of the science of right.",[]],"jaesche-s03-020":["The idea of humanity, the idea of a perfect republic, of a happy life and the like is lacking in most human beings. Many human beings have no idea of what they want, hence they proceed according to instinct and authority.",[]],"jaesche-s03-021":["§. 4. Given (*a priori* or *a posteriori*) and made concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-022":["All concepts are as to matter either given (*conceptus dati*) or made concepts (*conceptus factitii*). The former are given either *a priori* or *a posteriori*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-023":["All concepts given empirically or *a posteriori* are called concepts of experience, those given *a priori*, notions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-024":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-025":["Remark. The form of a concept as a discursive representation is always made.",[]],"jaesche-s03-026":["§. 5. Logical origin of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-027":["The origin of concepts as to mere form rests on reflection and on abstraction from the difference of the things which are designated through a certain representation. And so the question arises here: Which actions of the understanding make up a concept or — which is the same — belong to the generation of a concept from given representations?",[]],"jaesche-s03-028":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-029":["Remark 1. Since universal logic abstracts from all content of cognition through concepts, or from all matter of thinking: it can consider the concept only in respect of its form, i.e., only subjectively; not how it determines an object through a mark, but only how it can be related to several objects. Universal logic therefore does not have to investigate the source of concepts; not how concepts arise as representations, but solely how given representations become concepts in thinking; these concepts may otherwise contain something that is taken from experience, or also something fabricated, or borrowed from the nature of the understanding. — This logical origin of concepts — the origin as to their mere form — consists in reflection, whereby a representation common to several objects (*conceptus communis*) arises, as that form which is required for the power of judgment. Thus in logic merely the difference of reflection in concepts is considered.",[]],"jaesche-s03-030":["2. The origin of concepts in regard to their matter, according to which a concept is either empirical or arbitrary or intellectual, is considered in metaphysics.",[]],"jaesche-s03-031":["§. 6. Logical *actus* of comparison, reflection and abstraction.",[]],"jaesche-s03-032":["The logical *actus* of the understanding through which concepts are generated as to their form are:",[]],"jaesche-s03-033":["1) comparison, i.e., the comparing of representations with one another in relation to the unity of consciousness;",[]],"jaesche-s03-034":["2) reflection, i.e., the reflection on how various representations can be comprehended in One consciousness; and finally",[]],"jaesche-s03-035":["3) Abstraction or the separation of everything else in which the given representations differ.",[]],"jaesche-s03-036":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-037":["Remark 1. In order to make concepts out of representations one must therefore be able to compare, reflect and abstract, for these three logical operations of the understanding are the essential and universal conditions for the generation of every concept in general. I see, e.g., a spruce, a willow and a linden. By first comparing these objects with one another, I notice that they are different from one another in regard to the trunk, the branches, the leaves and the like; but now next I reflect only on that which they have in common among themselves, the trunk, the branches, the leaves themselves, and abstract from their magnitude, figure, etc.; thus I get a concept of tree.",[]],"jaesche-s03-038":["2. One does not always use the expression \"abstraction\" correctly in logic. We must not say: to abstract something (*abstrahere aliquid*), but to abstract from something (*abstrahere ab aliquo*). If, e.g., with scarlet cloth I think only the red color: then I abstract from the cloth, if I abstract also from this and think the scarlet as a material stuff in general: then I abstract from still more determinations, and my concept has thereby become still more abstract. For the more differences of things are left out of a concept or, the more determinations in it have been abstracted from: the more abstract is the concept. Abstract concepts should therefore properly be called abstracting ones (*conceptus abstrahentes*), i.e., those in which several abstractions occur. Thus, e.g., the concept body is properly not an abstract concept, for from body itself I surely cannot abstract, otherwise I would not have the concept of it. But I must indeed abstract from the magnitude, the color, the hardness or fluidity, in short: from all special determinations of particular bodies. The most abstract concept is that which has nothing in common with any concept distinct from it. This is the concept of Something, for that which is distinct from it is Nothing, and thus has nothing in common with the Something.",[]],"jaesche-s03-039":["3. Abstraction is only the negative condition under which universally valid representations can be generated, the positive is comparison and reflection. For through abstracting no concept comes to be, abstraction only completes it and encloses it in its determinate boundaries.",[]],"jaesche-s03-040":["§. 7. Content and domain of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-041":["Every concept, as partial concept, is contained in the representation of things, as ground of cognition, i.e., as mark, these things are contained under it. In the former respect every concept has a content, in the other a domain.",[]],"jaesche-s03-042":["Content and domain of a concept stand toward one another in inverse relation. The more, namely, a concept contains under itself, the less it contains in itself and conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-043":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-044":["Remark. The universality or universal validity of the concept rests not on the concept's being a partial concept, but on its being a ground of cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-045":["§. 8. Magnitude of the domain of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-046":["The domain or the sphere of a concept is the greater, the more things stand under it and can be thought through it.",[]],"jaesche-s03-047":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-048":["Remark. Just as one says of a ground in general that it contains the consequence under itself: so one can also say of the concept that as ground of cognition it contains under itself all those things from which it has been abstracted, e.g., the concept metal gold, silver, copper, etc. For since every concept, as a universally valid representation, contains that which is common to several representations of different things: all these things, which are to that extent contained under it, can be represented through it. And precisely this makes up the usefulness of a concept. Now the more things can be represented through a concept: the greater is its sphere. Thus, e.g., the concept body has a greater domain than the concept metal.",[]],"jaesche-s03-049":["§. 9. Higher and lower concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-050":["Concepts are called higher (*conceptus superiores*) insofar as they have other concepts under them, which in relation to them are called lower concepts. A mark of a mark — a remote mark — is a higher concept, the concept in relation to a remote mark, a lower one.",[]],"jaesche-s03-051":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-052":["Remark. Since higher and lower concepts are so called only relatively (*respective*): so One and the same concept can, in different relations, be at the same time a higher and a lower one. Thus, e.g., the concept human being is in relation to the concept Negro a higher one, but in relation to the concept animal a lower one.",[]],"jaesche-s03-053":["§. 10. Genus and species.",[]],"jaesche-s03-054":["The higher concept is called, in respect of its lower one, genus (*genus*), the lower concept in regard to its higher one species (*species*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-055":["Just as higher and lower, so also concepts of genus and of species are distinguished not according to their nature, but only in regard to their relation to one another (*termini a quo* or *ad quod*) in the logical subordination.",[]],"jaesche-s03-056":["§. 11. Highest genus and lowest species.",[]],"jaesche-s03-057":["The highest genus is that which is no species (*genus summum non est species*), as the lowest species is that which is no genus (*species, quae non est genus, est infima*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-058":["In accordance with the law of continuity, however, there can be neither a lowest nor a nearest species.",[]],"jaesche-s03-059":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-060":["Remark. If we think a series of several concepts subordinated to one another, e.g., iron, metal, body, substance, thing: then we can here obtain ever higher genera, — for every *species* is always at the same time to be regarded as *genus* in regard to its lower concept, e.g., the concept scholar in regard to the concept philosopher — until we finally come to a *genus* which cannot again be *species*. And at such a one we must in the end be able to arrive, because there must after all at the end be a highest concept (*conceptum summum*) from which, as such, nothing further can be abstracted without the whole concept disappearing. — But a lowest concept (*conceptum infimum*) or a lowest species, under which no other would any longer be contained, there is not in the series of species and genera, because such a one cannot possibly be determined. For even if we have a concept which we apply immediately to individuals: still in regard to it specific differences can be present, which we either do not notice or which we leave out of account. Only comparatively for use are there lowest concepts, which have received this significance as it were through convention, insofar as one has agreed not to go deeper here.",[]],"jaesche-s03-061":["In regard to the determination of the concepts of species and genus the following universal law therefore holds: There is a *genus* which can no longer be *species*, but there is no *species* which should not be able again to be *genus*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-062":["§. 12. Wider and narrower concept. — Reciprocal concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-063":["The higher concept is also called a wider one; the lower a narrower concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-064":["Concepts which have one and the same sphere are called reciprocal concepts (*conceptus reciproci*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-065":["§. 13. Relation of the lower to the higher, of the wider to the narrower concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-066":["The lower concept is not contained in the higher, for it contains more in itself than the higher; but it is nevertheless contained under it, because the higher contains the ground of cognition of the lower.",[]],"jaesche-s03-067":["Further, a concept is wider than the other not because it contains more under itself — for that one cannot know —, but insofar as it contains under itself the other concept, and besides it still more.",[]],"jaesche-s03-068":["§. 14. Universal rules with regard to the subordination of concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-069":["In regard to the logical domain of concepts the following universal rules hold:",[]],"jaesche-s03-070":["1) What belongs to or contradicts the higher concepts, that also belongs to or contradicts all lower concepts which are contained under those higher ones; and",[]],"jaesche-s03-071":["2) conversely: What belongs to or contradicts all lower concepts, that also belongs to or contradicts their higher concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-072":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-073":["Remark. Because that in which things agree flows from their universal properties, and that in which they are different from one another from their particular properties, one cannot infer: What belongs to or contradicts one lower concept, that also belongs to or contradicts other lower concepts which belong with it to One higher concept. Thus, e.g., one cannot infer: What does not belong to the human being, that also does not belong to the angels.",[]],"jaesche-s03-074":["§. 15. Conditions of the arising of higher and lower concepts: Logical abstraction and logical determination.",[]],"jaesche-s03-075":["Through continued logical abstraction ever higher concepts arise, just as on the contrary through continued logical determination ever lower ones. The greatest possible abstraction gives the highest or most abstract concept — that from which no further determination can be thought away. The highest completed determination would give a thoroughly determined concept (*conceptum omnimode determinatum*), i.e., one such that to it no further determination could any more be thought in addition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-076":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-077":["Remark. Since only single things or individuals are thoroughly determined: so there can also be thoroughly determined cognitions only as intuitions, but not as concepts; in regard to the latter the logical determination can never be regarded as completed (§. 11. Rem.).",[]],"jaesche-s03-078":["§. 16. Use of concepts *in abstracto* and *in concreto*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-079":["Every concept can be used universally and particularly (*in abstracto* and *in concreto*). *In abstracto* the lower concept is used in regard to its higher, *in concreto* the higher concept in regard to its lower.",[]],"jaesche-s03-080":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-081":["Remark 1. The expressions of the abstract and the concrete thus refer not so much to concepts in themselves — for every concept is an abstract concept — as rather only to their use. And this use can in turn have different degrees, — according as one treats a concept now more, now less abstractly or concretely, i.e., now more, now less determinations either leaves out or adds. Through the ab|stract",[]],"jaesche-s03-082":["use a concept comes nearer to the highest genus, through the concrete use on the contrary to the individual.",[]],"jaesche-s03-083":["2. Which use of concepts, the abstract or the concrete, has a precedence over the other? About this nothing can be decided. The worth of the one is not to be estimated lower than the worth of the other. Through very abstract concepts we cognize in many things little, through very concrete concepts we cognize in few things much; what we gain, therefore, on the one side, that we lose again on the other. A concept that has a large sphere is very usable insofar as one can apply it to many things; but in return there is also so much the less contained in it. In the concept substance, e.g., I do not think as much as in the concept chalk.",[]],"jaesche-s03-084":["3. To strike the relation between the representation *in abstracto* and *in concreto* in the same cognition, hence of the concepts and their presentation, whereby the maximum of cognition, as to domain as well as to content, is attained, in this consists the art of popularity.",[]],"jaesche-s03-085":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-086":["Second Section. Of Judgments. [§§ 17 — 40]",[]],"jaesche-s03-087":["§. 17. Definition of a judgment in general.",[]],"jaesche-s03-088":["A judgment is the representation of the unity of the consciousness of different representations or the representation of the relation of the same, insofar as they make up a concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-089":["§. 18. Matter and form of judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-090":["To every judgment belong as essential components of it matter and form. In the given cognitions, combined into unity of consciousness in the judgment, consists the matter, in the determination of the way and manner in which the different representations, as such, belong to One consciousness, the form of the judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-091":["§. 19. Object of logical reflection the mere form of judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-092":["Since logic abstracts from all real or objective difference of cognition: so it can concern itself with the matter of judgments as little as with the content of concepts. It has therefore merely to take into consideration the difference of judgments in regard to their mere form.",[]],"jaesche-s03-093":["§. 20. Logical forms of judgments: quantity, quality, relation and modality.",[]],"jaesche-s03-094":["The differences of judgments with respect to their form can be traced back to the four chief moments of quantity, quality, relation and modality, in regard to which just as many different kinds of judgments are determined.",[]],"jaesche-s03-095":["§. 21. Quantity of judgments: universal, particular, singular.",[]],"jaesche-s03-096":["As to quantity judgments are either universal or particular or singular, according as the subject in the judgment is either wholly included in or excluded from the notion of the predicate, or is only in part included in, in part excluded from it. In the universal judgment the sphere of one concept is enclosed wholly within the sphere of another; in the particular a part of the former is enclosed under the sphere of the other, and in the singular judgment finally a concept that has no sphere at all is enclosed, consequently merely as part, under the sphere of another.",[]],"jaesche-s03-097":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-098":["Remark 1. Singular judgments are, as to logical form, in use to be estimated equal to universal ones, for in both the predicate holds of the subject without exception. In the singular proposition, e.g.: Caius is mortal, there can just as little be an exception as in the universal: All human beings are mortal. For there is only One Caius.",[]],"jaesche-s03-099":["2. With respect to the universality of a cognition there takes place a real difference between general and universal propositions, which, however, to be sure, does not concern logic. General propositions, namely, are those which contain merely something of the universal of certain objects and consequently not sufficient conditions of subsumption, e.g., the proposition: one must make proofs thoroughly. Universal propositions are those which assert something universally of an object.",[]],"jaesche-s03-100":["3. Universal rules are either analytically or synthetically universal. The former abstract from the diversities, the latter attend to the differences and consequently do determine also in regard to them. The more simply an object is thought, the sooner is analytic universality in consequence of a concept possible.",[]],"jaesche-s03-101":["4. If universal propositions, without knowing them *in concreto*, cannot be had insight into in their universality, then they cannot serve as guideline and thus cannot hold heuristically in application, but are only problems for the investigation of the universal grounds of that which in particular cases first became known. The proposition, for example: Whoever has no interest in lying and knows the truth, speaks truth, — this proposition is not to be had insight into in its universality, because we know the restriction to the condition of the disinterested only through experience, namely that human beings can lie from interest, which comes from this, that they do not hold firmly to morality. An observation that teaches us to know the weakness of human nature.",[]],"jaesche-s03-102":["5. Of particular judgments it is to be noted that, if they are to be able to be had insight into through reason and thus have a rational, not merely intellectual (abstracted) form: then the subject must be a wider concept (*conceptus latior*) than the predicate. Let the predicate always be = O, the subject , then",[]],"jaesche-s03-103":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-104":["is a particular judgment, for some of what belongs under a is b, some not b, — that follows from reason. But let it be",[]],"jaesche-s03-105":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-106":["then at least all a can be contained under b, if it is smaller, but not if it is larger, thus it is only contingently particular.",[]],"jaesche-s03-107":["§. 22. Quality of judgments: affirmative, negative, infinite.",[]],"jaesche-s03-108":["As to quality judgments are either affirmative or negative or infinite. In the affirmative judgment the subject is thought under the sphere of a predicate, in the negative it is posited outside the sphere of the latter, and in the infinite it is posited in the sphere of a concept that lies outside the sphere of another.",[]],"jaesche-s03-109":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-110":["Remark 1. The infinite judgment indicates not merely that a subject is not contained under the sphere of a predicate, but that it lies outside the sphere of the same somewhere in the infinite sphere; consequently this judgment represents the sphere of the predicate as limited.",[]],"jaesche-s03-111":["Everything possible is either A or non A. If I say, therefore: something is non A, e.g., the human soul is non-mortal, some human beings are non-learned, and the like, then this is an infinite judgment. For through it is not determined, beyond the finite sphere A, under which concept the object belongs, but merely that it belongs in the sphere outside A, which is properly no sphere at all, but only the bordering of a sphere on the infinite or the bounding itself. Now although exclusion is a negation: yet the limiting of a concept is a positive action. Hence boundaries are positive concepts of limited objects.",[]],"jaesche-s03-112":["2. According to the principle of the exclusion of every third (*exclusi tertii*) the sphere of a concept relative to another is either excluding or including. Now since logic has to do merely with the form of the judgment, not with concepts as to their content: so the distinguishing of infinite from negative judgments does not belong to this science.",[]],"jaesche-s03-113":["3. In negative judgments the negation always affects the copula, in infinite ones not the copula, but the predicate is affected by the negation, which can best be expressed in Latin.",[]],"jaesche-s03-114":["§. 23. Relation of judgments: categorical, hypothetical, disjunctive.",[]],"jaesche-s03-115":["As to relation judgments are either categorical or hypothetical or disjunctive. The given representations in the judgment are, namely, subordinated one to the other for the unity of consciousness, either: as predicate to the subject, or: as sequel to the ground, or: as member of the division to the divided concept. Through the first relation the categorical, through the second the hypothetical and through the third the disjunctive judgments are determined.",[]],"jaesche-s03-116":["§. 24. Categorical judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-117":["In categorical judgments subject and predicate make up the matter of them, the form, through which the relation (of agreement or of conflict) between subject and predicate is determined and expressed, is called the copula.",[]],"jaesche-s03-118":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-119":["Remark. Categorical judgments do make up the matter of the remaining judgments, but one must not on that account believe, as several logicians do, that hypothetical as well as disjunctive judgments are nothing further than different clothings of categorical ones and could therefore all be traced back to the latter. All three kinds of judgments rest on essentially different logical functions of the understanding and must therefore be considered according to their specific difference.",[]],"jaesche-s03-120":["§. 25. Hypothetical judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-121":["The matter of hypothetical judgments consists of two judgments, which are connected with each other as ground and sequel. The one of these judgments, which contains the ground, is the antecedent proposition (*antecedens, prius*), the other, which relates to the former as sequel, the consequent proposition (*consequens, posterius*), and the representation of this kind of connection of both judgments with each other for the unity of consciousness is called the consequence, which makes up the form of hypothetical judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-122":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-123":["Remark 1. What for categorical judgments the *copula* is, that for hypothetical ones is thus the consequence — the form of them.",[]],"jaesche-s03-124":["2. Some believe it is easy to transform a hypothetical proposition into a categorical one. But this will not do, because both are by their nature entirely different from each other. In categorical judgments nothing is problematic, but everything assertoric, in hypothetical ones on the contrary only the consequence is assertoric. In the latter I can therefore connect two false judgments with each other, for here it comes down only to the correctness of the connection — the form of the consequence, on which the logical truth of these judgments rests. There is an essential difference between the two propositions: All bodies are divisible, and: If all bodies are composite, then they are divisible. In the former proposition I assert the matter straight out, in the latter only under a problematically expressed condition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-125":["§. 26. Kinds of connection in hypothetical judgments: *modus ponens* and *modus tollens*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-126":["The form of connection in hypothetical judgments is twofold: the positing (*modus ponens*) or the canceling (*modus tollens*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-127":["1) If the ground (*antecedens*) is true: then the sequel (*consequens*) determined through it is also true; is called the *modus ponens*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-128":["2) If the sequel (*consequens*) is false: then the ground (*antecedens*) is also false; *modus tollens*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-129":["§. 27. Disjunctive judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-130":["A judgment is disjunctive if the parts of the sphere of a given concept determine one another in the whole or to a whole as complements (*complementa*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-131":["§. 28. Matter and form of disjunctive judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-132":["The several given judgments, of which the disjunctive judgment is composed, make up the matter of it and are called the members of the disjunction or opposition. In the disjunction itself, i.e., in the determination of the relation of the different judgments, as members of the whole sphere of the divided cognition that reciprocally exclude one another and complement one another, consists the form of these judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-133":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-134":["Remark. All disjunctive judgments thus represent different judgments as in the community of a sphere and produce each judgment only through",[]],"jaesche-s03-135":["the limitation of the others in regard to the whole sphere, they thus determine each judgment's relation to the whole sphere, and thereby at the same time the relation that these different members of division (*membra disjuncta*) have among themselves. — One member thus determines here every other only insofar as they all stand in community as parts of a whole sphere of cognition, outside of which, in a certain respect, nothing can be thought.",[]],"jaesche-s03-136":["§. 29. Peculiar character of disjunctive judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-137":["The peculiar character of all disjunctive judgments, whereby their specific difference as to the moment of relation from the remaining ones, in particular from categorical judgments, is determined, consists in this: that the members of the disjunction are all problematic judgments, of which nothing else is thought than that they, like parts of the sphere of a cognition, each the complement of the other to the whole (*complementum ad totum*), taken together, are equal to the sphere of the first. And from this follows: that in One of these problematic judgments the truth must be contained or — which is the same ‑, that One of them must hold assertorically, because outside them the sphere of the cognition under the given conditions comprises nothing more and one is opposed to the other, consequently neither outside them something else, nor also among them more than One can be true.",[]],"jaesche-s03-138":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-139":["Remark. In a categorical judgment the thing, whose representation is considered as a part of the sphere of another subordinated representation, is considered as contained under this its higher concept, thus here in the subordination of the spheres the part of the part is compared with the whole. But in disjunctive judgments I go from the whole to all the parts taken together. What is contained under the sphere of a concept, that is also contained under one of the parts of this sphere. Accordingly the sphere must first be divided. If I, e.g., make the disjunctive judgment: A learned man is either a historical or a rational learned man, then I determine thereby that these concepts are, as to the sphere, parts of the sphere of the learned, but by no means parts of one another, and that all taken together they are complete.",[]],"jaesche-s03-140":["That in disjunctive judgments not the sphere of the divided concept, as contained in the sphere of the divisions, but that which is contained under the divided concept, as contained under one of the members of the division, is considered, the following schema of comparison between categorical and disjunctive judgments may make more intuitive.",[]],"jaesche-s03-141":["In categorical judgments x, which is contained under b, is also under a:",[]],"jaesche-s03-142":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-143":["In disjunctive ones x, which is contained under a, is contained either under b or c etc.:",[]],"jaesche-s03-144":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-145":["Thus the division in disjunctive judgments indicates the coordination not of the parts of the whole concept, but all parts of its sphere. Here I think many things through one concept, there one thing through many concepts, e.g., the *definitum* through all marks of the coordination.",[]],"jaesche-s03-146":["§. 30. Modality of judgments: problematic, assertoric, apodictic.",[]],"jaesche-s03-147":["As to modality, through which moment the relation of the whole judgment to the faculty of cognition is determined, judgments are either problematic or assertoric or apodictic. The problematic are accompanied with the consciousness of the mere possibility, the assertoric with the consciousness of the actuality, the apodictic finally with the consciousness of the necessity of the judging.",[]],"jaesche-s03-148":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-149":["Remark 1. This moment of modality thus indicates only the way and manner in which in the judgment something is asserted or denied: whether one settles nothing about the truth or untruth of a judgment, as in the proble|matic",[]],"jaesche-s03-150":["judgment: the soul of the human being may be immortal; or whether one determines something about it, as in the assertoric judgment: the human soul is immortal; or finally, whether one expresses the truth of a judgment even with the dignity of necessity, as in the apodictic judgment: the soul of the human being must be immortal. This determination of the merely possible or actual or necessary truth thus concerns only the judgment itself, by no means the matter about which it is judged.",[]],"jaesche-s03-151":["2. In problematic judgments, which one can also define as those whose matter is given with the possible relation between predicate and subject, the subject must always have a smaller sphere than the predicate.",[]],"jaesche-s03-152":["3. On the difference between problematic and assertoric judgments rests the true difference between judgments and propositions, which one is otherwise accustomed falsely to posit in the mere expression through words, without which one indeed could not judge at all. In the judgment the relation of different representations to the unity of consciousness is thought merely as problematic, in a proposition on the contrary as assertoric. A problematic proposition is a *contradictio in adjecto*. Before I have a proposition, I must indeed first judge; and I judge about much that I do not settle, which, however, I must do as soon as I determine a judgment as proposition. It is, moreover, good first to judge problematically before one accepts the judgment as assertoric, in order to test it in this way. Also it is not always necessary for our aim to have assertoric judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-153":["§. 31. Exponible judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-154":["Judgments in which an affirmation and negation are contained at the same time, but in a hidden way, so that the affirmation occurs distinctly, but the negation hiddenly, are exponible propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-155":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-156":["Remark. In the exponible judgment, e.g., few human beings are learned, there lies 1), but in a hidden way, the negative judgment: many human beings are not learned, and 2) the affirmative: some human beings are learned. Since the nature of exponible propositions depends solely on conditions of language, according to which one can express two judgments at once in brief: so the remark that there can be judgments in our language that must be exponed belongs not in logic, but in grammar.",[]],"jaesche-s03-157":["§. 32. Theoretical and practical propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-158":["Theoretical propositions are called those which refer to the object and determine what belongs or does not belong to it; practical propositions on the contrary are those which state the action whereby, as necessary condition of it, an object becomes possible.",[]],"jaesche-s03-159":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-160":["Remark. Logic has to treat only of practical propositions as to form, which are insofar opposed to theoretical ones. Practical propositions, as to content, and insofar distinguished from speculative ones, belong in morals.",[]],"jaesche-s03-161":["§. 33. Indemonstrable and demonstrable propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-162":["Demonstrable propositions are those which are capable of a proof; those which are capable of no proof are called indemonstrable.",[]],"jaesche-s03-163":["Immediately certain judgments are indemonstrable and thus to be regarded as elementary propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-164":["§. 34. Principles.",[]],"jaesche-s03-165":["Immediately certain judgments *a priori* can be called principles, insofar as other judgments can be proved from them, but they themselves can be subordinated to no other. They are for this reason also called principles (beginnings).",[]],"jaesche-s03-166":["§. 35. Intuitive and discursive principles: axioms and acroams.",[]],"jaesche-s03-167":["Principles are either intuitive or discursive. The former can be presented in intuition and are called axioms (*axiomata*), the latter can be expressed only through concepts and can be called acroams (*acroamata*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-168":["§. 36. Analytic and synthetic propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-169":["Analytic propositions are called those whose certainty rests on identity of the concepts (of the predicate with the notion of the subject). Propositions whose truth is not grounded on identity of the concepts must be called synthetic.",[]],"jaesche-s03-170":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-171":["Remark 1. Every x to which the concept of body (a+b) belongs, to it belongs also extension (b), is an example of an analytic proposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-172":["Every x to which the concept of body (a+b) belongs, to it belongs also attraction (c), is an example of a synthetic proposition. Synthetic propositions augment cognition *materialiter*, analytic ones merely *formaliter*. The former contain determinations (*determinationes*), the latter only logical predicates.",[]],"jaesche-s03-173":["2. Analytic principles are not axioms, for they are discursive. And synthetic principles are also axioms only when they are intuitive.",[]],"jaesche-s03-174":["§. 37. Tautological propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-175":["The identity of the concepts in analytic judgments can be either an explicit (*explicita*) or a non-explicit (*implicita*) one. In the former case the analytic propositions are tautological.",[]],"jaesche-s03-176":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-177":["Remark 1. Tautological propositions are *virtualiter* empty or empty of sequels, for they are without utility and use. Of this sort is, e.g., the tautological proposition: the human being is human being. For if I know nothing further to say of the human being than that he is a human being: then I know nothing further at all of him.",[]],"jaesche-s03-178":["Implicitly identical propositions, on the contrary, are not empty of sequels or of fruit, for they make the predicate, which lay undeveloped (*implicite*) in the concept of the subject, clear through development (*explicatio*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-179":["2. Propositions empty of sequels must be distinguished from those empty of sense, which are empty of understanding because they concern the determination of so-called hidden properties (*qualitates occultae*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-180":["§. 38. Postulate and problem.",[]],"jaesche-s03-181":["A postulate is a practical, immediately certain proposition or a principle that determines a possible action, in which it is presupposed that the way to carry it out is immediately certain.",[]],"jaesche-s03-182":["Problems (*problemata*) are demonstrable propositions in need of an instruction, or those which state an action whose way of carrying out is not immediately certain.",[]],"jaesche-s03-183":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-184":["Remark 1. There can also be theoretical postulates on behalf of practical reason. These are theoretical hypotheses necessary with a view to practical reason, like those of the existence of God, of freedom and of another world.",[]],"jaesche-s03-185":["2. To the problem belong 1) the question, which contains that which is to be accomplished, 2) the resolution, which contains the way and manner in which what is to be accomplished can be carried out, and 3) the demonstration that, when I shall have proceeded thus, what is required will occur.",[]],"jaesche-s03-186":["§. 39. Theorems, corollaries, lemmas and scholia.",[]],"jaesche-s03-187":["Theorems are theoretical propositions capable of and in need of a proof. Corollaries are immediate sequels from one of the preceding propositions. — Lemmas (*lemmata*) are called propositions which are not native in the science in which they are presupposed as proved, but are borrowed from other sciences. Scholia finally are mere propositions of clarification, which thus do not belong as members to the whole of the system.",[]],"jaesche-s03-188":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-189":["Remark. Essential and universal moments of every theorem are the thesis and the demonstration. The difference between theorems and corollaries one can, moreover, also posit in this, that the latter are inferred immediately, the former on the contrary are drawn through a series of sequels from immediately certain propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-190":["§. 40. Judgments of perception and of experience.",[]],"jaesche-s03-191":["A judgment of perception is merely subjective, an objective judgment from perceptions is a judgment of experience.",[]],"jaesche-s03-192":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-193":["Remark. A judgment from mere perceptions is not really possible except only through this, that I state my representation, as perception: I, who perceive a tower, perceive in it the red color. But I cannot say: it is red. For this would be not merely an empirical judgment, but also a judgment of experience, i.e., an empirical judgment through which I get a concept of the object. E.g. On touching the stone I sense warmth, is a judgment of perception, but: the stone is warm, a judgment of experience. It belongs to the latter that I do not count that which is merely in my subject as belonging to the object, for a judgment of experience is the perception from which a concept of the object arises; e.g., whether in the moon light points move or in the air or in my eye.",[]],"jaesche-s03-194":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-195":["Third Section. Of Inferences. [§§ 41 — 43]",[]],"jaesche-s03-196":["§. 41. Inference in general.",[]],"jaesche-s03-197":["By inferring is to be understood that function of thinking whereby one judgment is derived from another. An inference in general is thus the derivation of one judgment from the other.",[]],"jaesche-s03-198":["§. 42. Immediate and mediate inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-199":["All inferences are either immediate or mediate.",[]],"jaesche-s03-200":["An immediate inference (*consequentia immediata*) is the derivation (*deductio*) of one judgment from the other without a mediating judgment (*judicium intermedium*). An inference is mediate if one needs, besides the concept that a judgment contains in itself, still others in order to derive a cognition from it.",[]],"jaesche-s03-201":["§. 43. Inferences of the understanding, inferences of reason and inferences of the power of judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-202":["Immediate inferences are also called inferences of the understanding, all mediate inferences on the contrary are either inferences of reason or inferences of the power of judgment. We treat here first of the immediate or the inferences of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s03-203":["[Third Section. Of Inferences.] I. Inferences of the understanding.[§§ 44 — 55]",[]],"jaesche-s03-204":["§. 44. Peculiar nature of inferences of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s03-205":["The essential character of all immediate inferences and the principle of their possibility consists solely in an alteration of the mere form of the judgments, while the matter of the judgments, the subject and predicate, remains unaltered the same.",[]],"jaesche-s03-206":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-207":["Remark 1. Through this, that in immediate inferences only the form and by no means the matter of the judgments is altered, these inferences are essentially distinguished from all mediate ones, in which the judgments are distinguished also as to the matter, since here a new concept must be added as mediating judgment, or as middle concept (*terminus medius*), in order to conclude the one judgment from the other. If I, e.g., infer: All human beings are mortal, thus Caius too is mortal: then this is no immediate inference. For here I still need for the consequence the mediating judgment: Caius is a human being; through this new concept, however, the matter of the judgments is altered.",[]],"jaesche-s03-208":["2. A *judicium intermedium* can indeed also be made in inferences of the understanding, but then this mediating judgment is merely tautological. As, e.g., in the immediate inference: All human beings are mortal. Some human beings are human beings. Thus some human beings are mortal, the middle concept is a tautological proposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-209":["§. 45. *Modi* of inferences of the understanding.",[]],"jaesche-s03-210":["Inferences of the understanding go through all classes of the logical functions of judging and are consequently determined in their chief kinds by the moments of quantity, of quality, of relation and of modality. On this rests the following division of these inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-211":["§. 46. 1. Inferences of the understanding (in reference to the quantity of judgments) *per judicia subalternata*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-212":["In inferences of the understanding *per judicia subalternata* the two judgments are distinguished as to quantity, and here the particular judgment is derived from the universal, in consequence of the principle: From the universal the inference to the particular holds (*ab universali ad particulare valet consequentia*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-213":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-214":["Remark. A *judicium* is called *subalternatum* insofar as it is contained under the other; as, e.g., particular judgments under universal ones.",[]],"jaesche-s03-215":["§. 47. 2. Inferences of the understanding (in reference to the quality of judgments) *per judicia opposita*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-216":["In inferences of the understanding of this kind the alteration concerns the quality of the judgments, and indeed considered in reference to the opposition. Now since this opposition can be a threefold one, there results from this the following particular division of immediate inferring: through contradictorily opposed, through contrary and through subcontrary judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-217":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-218":["Remark. Inferences of the understanding through equivalent judgments (*judicia aequipollentia*) can properly be called no inferences, for here no sequel takes place, they are rather to be regarded as a mere substitution of the words that designate one and the same concept, whereby the judgments themselves remain unaltered also as to form. E.g.: Not all human beings are virtuous, and: Some human beings are not virtuous. Both judgments say one and the same.",[]],"jaesche-s03-219":["§. 48. a. Inferences of the understanding *per judicia contradictorie opposita*./",[]],"jaesche-s03-220":["In inferences of the understanding through judgments that are contradictorily opposed to one another and as such make up the genuine, pure opposition, the truth of the one of the contradictorily opposed judgments is concluded from the falsity of the other and conversely. For the genuine opposition that takes place here contains neither more nor less than what belongs to opposition. In consequence of the principle of the excluded third, therefore, both contradicting judgments cannot be true, but also [also] just as little can they both be false. If therefore the one is true, then the other is false and conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-221":["§. 49. b. Inferences of the understanding *per judicia contrarie opposita*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-222":["Contrary or conflicting judgments (*judicia contrarie opposita*) are judgments of which the one is universally affirmative, the other universally negative. Now since one of them states more than the other, and in the superfluity that it states over and above the mere negation of the other the falsity can lie: so they can indeed not both be true, but they can both be false. In regard to these judgments, therefore, only the inference from the truth of the one to the falsity of the other holds, but not conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-223":["§. 50. c. Inferences of the understanding *per judicia subcontrarie opposita*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-224":["Subcontrary judgments are those of which the one particularly (*particulariter*) affirms or denies what the other particularly denies or affirms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-225":["Since they can both be true, but not both be false, in regard to them only the following inference holds: If the one of these propositions is false, then the other is true; but not conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-226":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-227":["Remark. In subcontrary judgments no pure, strict opposition takes place, for in the one it is not denied or affirmed of the same objects what was affirmed or denied in the other. In the inference, e.g., Some human beings are learned, thus some human beings are not learned, in the first judgment that is not asserted of the same human beings which is denied in the other.",[]],"jaesche-s03-228":["§. 51. 3. Inferences of the understanding (with respect to the relation of judgments) *per judicia conversa sive per conversionem*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-229":["Immediate inferences through conversion concern the relation of judgments and consist in the transposition of the subjects and predicates in the two judgments, so that the subject of the one judgment is made into the predicate of the other judgment, and conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-230":["§. 52. Pure and altered conversion.",[]],"jaesche-s03-231":["In conversion the quantity of the judgments is either altered or it remains unaltered. In the former case the converted (*conversum*) is distinguished from the converting (*convertente*) as to quantity, and the conversion is called an altered one (*conversio per accidens*), in the latter case the conversion is called a pure one (*conversio simpliciter talis*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-232":["§. 53. Universal rules of conversion.",[]],"jaesche-s03-233":["With respect to inferences of the understanding through conversion the following rules hold:",[]],"jaesche-s03-234":["1) Universally affirmative judgments can be converted only *per accidens*; for the predicate in these judgments is a wider concept, and thus only some of it is contained in the concept of the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s03-235":["2) But all universally negative judgments can be converted *simpliciter*; for here the subject is lifted out of the sphere of the predicate. Likewise, finally, can",[]],"jaesche-s03-236":["3) All particularly affirmative propositions be converted *simpliciter*; for in these judgments a part of the sphere of the subject has been subsumed under the predicate, thus a part of the sphere of the predicate can also be subsumed under the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s03-237":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-238":["Remark 1. In universally affirmative judgments the subject is considered as a *contentum* of the predicate, since it is con|tained",[]],"jaesche-s03-239":["under the sphere of the same. I may therefore, e.g., only infer: All human beings are mortal, thus some of those that are contained under the concept mortals are human beings. But that universally negative judgments can be converted *simpliciter*, of this the cause is this: that two concepts universally contradicting one another contradict one another in equal domain.",[]],"jaesche-s03-240":["2. Many universally affirmative judgments can indeed also be converted *simpliciter*. But the ground of this lies not in their form, but in the particular constitution of their matter; as, e.g., the two judgments: Everything unalterable is necessary, and everything necessary is unalterable.",[]],"jaesche-s03-241":["§. 54. 4. Inferences of the understanding (in reference to the modality of judgments) *per judicia contraposita*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-242":["The immediate kind of inference through contraposition consists in that transposition (*metathesis*) of the judgments in which merely the quantity remains the same, the quality on the contrary is altered. They concern only the modality of the judgments, since they transform an assertoric into an apodictic judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-243":["§. 55. Universal rule of contraposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-244":["With respect to contraposition the universal rule holds:",[]],"jaesche-s03-245":["All universally affirmative judgments can be contraposed *simpliciter*. For if the predicate, as that which contains the subject under itself, hence the whole sphere, is denied: then a part of it must also be denied, i.e., the subject.",[]],"jaesche-s03-246":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-247":["Remark 1. The metathesis of judgments through conversion and that through contraposition are thus opposed to each other insofar as the former alters merely the quantity, the latter merely the quality.",[]],"jaesche-s03-248":["2. The kinds of immediate inference mentioned relate merely to categorical judgments.",[]],"jaesche-s03-249":["[Third Section. On Inferences.] II. Syllogisms. [§§ 56 — 80]",[]],"jaesche-s03-250":["§. 56. Syllogism in general.",[]],"jaesche-s03-251":["A syllogism is the cognition of the necessity of a proposition through the subsumption of its condition under a given universal rule.",[]],"jaesche-s03-252":["§. 57. Universal principle of all syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-253":["The universal principle on which the validity of all inferring through reason rests can be determinately expressed in the following formula:",[]],"jaesche-s03-254":["What stands under the condition of a rule, that stands also under the rule itself.",[]],"jaesche-s03-255":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-256":["Remark. The syllogism premises a universal rule and a subsumption under its condition. One thereby cognizes the conclusion a priori not in the individual, but as contained in the universal and as necessary under a certain condition. And this, that everything stand under the universal and be determinable in universal rules, is precisely the principle of rationality or of necessity (*principium rationalitatis sive necessitatis*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-257":["§. 58. Essential components of the syllogism.",[]],"jaesche-s03-258":["To every syllogism belong the following essential three pieces:",[]],"jaesche-s03-259":["1) a universal rule, which is called the major premise (*propositio major*),",[]],"jaesche-s03-260":["2) the proposition that subsumes a cognition under the condition of the universal rule and is called the minor premise (*propositio minor*), and finally",[]],"jaesche-s03-261":["3) the proposition that affirms or denies the predicate of the rule of the subsumed cognition: the conclusion (*conclusio*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-262":["The two former propositions, in their combination with each other, are called the antecedents or premises.",[]],"jaesche-s03-263":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-264":["Remark. A rule is an assertion under a universal condition. The relation of the condition to the assertion, namely how the latter stands under the former, is the exponent of the rule.",[]],"jaesche-s03-265":["The cognition that the condition obtains (somewhere) is the subsumption.",[]],"jaesche-s03-266":["The combination of that which has been subsumed under the condition with the assertion of the rule is the inference.",[]],"jaesche-s03-267":["§. 59. Matter and form of syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-268":["In the antecedents or premises consists the matter, and in the conclusion, insofar as it contains the consequence, the form of syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-269":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-270":["Remark 1. In every syllogism, therefore, first the truth of the premises and then the correctness of the consequence is to be examined. Never must one, in rejecting a syllogism, first reject the conclusion, but always first either the premises or the consequence.",[]],"jaesche-s03-271":["2. In every syllogism the conclusion is given at once, as soon as the premises and the consequence is given.",[]],"jaesche-s03-272":["§. 60. Division of syllogisms (according to relation) into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive.",[]],"jaesche-s03-273":["All rules (judgments) contain objective unity of the consciousness of the manifold of cognition, hence a condition under which one cognition belongs with the other to one consciousness. Now, however, only three conditions of this unity can be thought, namely: as subject of the inherence of the marks, or as ground of the dependence of one cognition on the other, or finally as combination of the parts in a whole (logical division). Consequently there can also be only just as many kinds of universal rules (*propositiones majores*), through which the consequence of one judgment from the other is mediated.",[]],"jaesche-s03-274":["And on this is grounded the division of all syllogisms into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive.",[]],"jaesche-s03-275":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-276":["Remark 1. Syllogisms can be divided neither according to quantity, for every *major* is a rule, hence something universal; nor in regard to quality, for it is equally valid whether the conclusion is affirmative or negative; nor finally with respect to modality, for the conclusion is always accompanied with the consciousness of necessity and consequently has the dignity of an apodictic proposition. Thus there remains solely relation as the only possible ground of division of syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-277":["2. Many logicians hold only the categorical syllogisms to be ordinary, the rest, on the contrary, to be extraordinary. But this is groundless and false. For all three of these kinds are products of equally correct, but from one another equally essentially different functions of reason.",[]],"jaesche-s03-278":["§. 61. Distinctive difference between categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-279":["What distinguishes the three kinds of syllogisms mentioned lies in the major premise. In categorical syllogisms the major is a categorical, in hypothetical it is a hypothetical or problematic, and in disjunctive a disjunctive proposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-280":["§. 62. 1. Categorical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-281":["In every categorical syllogism there are three principal concepts (*termini*), namely:",[]],"jaesche-s03-282":["1) the predicate in the conclusion, which concept is called the major concept (*terminus major*), because it has a greater sphere than the subject,",[]],"jaesche-s03-283":["2) the subject (in the conclusion), whose concept is called the minor concept (*terminus minor*), and",[]],"jaesche-s03-284":["3) a mediating mark (*nota intermedia*), which is called the middle concept (*terminus medius*), because through it a cognition is subsumed under the condition of the rule.",[]],"jaesche-s03-285":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-286":["Remark. This difference in the *termini* mentioned obtains only in categorical syllogisms, because only these alone infer through a *terminus medius*; the others, on the contrary, only through the subsumption of a proposition represented in the *major* problematically and in the *minor* assertorically.",[]],"jaesche-s03-287":["§. 63. Principle of categorical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-288":["The principle on which the possibility and validity of all categorical syllogisms rests is this:",[]],"jaesche-s03-289":["What belongs to the mark of a thing, that belongs also to the thing itself; and what contradicts the mark of a thing, that contradicts also the thing itself (*nota notae est nota rei ipsius; repugnans notae, repugnat rei ipsi*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-290":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-291":["Remark. From the principle just set up the so-called *Dictum de omni et nullo* can easily be deduced, and on that account it cannot count as the supreme principle either for syllogisms in general or for the categorical ones in particular.",[]],"jaesche-s03-292":["The genus- and species-concepts are namely universal marks of all the things that stand under these concepts. Accordingly the rule holds here: What belongs to or contradicts the genus or species, that also belongs to or contradicts all the objects that are contained under that genus or species. And this rule is called precisely the *Dictum de omni et nullo*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-293":["§. 64. Rules for categorical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-294":["From the nature and the principle of categorical syllogisms flow the following rules for them:",[]],"jaesche-s03-295":["1) In every categorical syllogism no more nor fewer principal concepts (*termini*) can be contained than three; for I am here to combine two concepts (subject and predicate) through a mediating mark.",[]],"jaesche-s03-296":["2) The antecedents or premises may not all deny (*ex puris negativis nihil sequitur*); for the subsumption in the minor premise must be affirmative, as that which states that a cognition stands under the condition of the rule.",[]],"jaesche-s03-297":["3) The premises also may not all be particular (*particulare*) propositions (*ex puris particularibus nihil sequitur*); for then there would be no rule, i.e., no universal proposition, from which a particular cognition could be concluded.",[]],"jaesche-s03-298":["4) The conclusion always directs itself according to the weaker part of the inference; i.e., according to the negative and particular proposition in the premises, as which is called the weaker part of the categorical syllogism (*conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*). If therefore",[]],"jaesche-s03-299":["5) one of the antecedents is a negative proposition: then the conclusion must also be negative, and",[]],"jaesche-s03-300":["6) if an antecedent is a particular proposition: then the conclusion must also be particular.",[]],"jaesche-s03-301":["7) In all categorical syllogisms the major must be a universal (*universalis*), the minor however an affirmative proposition (*affirmans*), and from this follows finally,",[]],"jaesche-s03-302":["8) that the conclusion must direct itself in regard to quality according to the major premise, but with respect to quantity according to the minor premise.",[]],"jaesche-s03-303":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-304":["Remark. That the conclusion must at all times direct itself according to the negative and particular proposition in the premises is easy to see.",[]],"jaesche-s03-305":["If I make the minor premise only particular and say: Something is contained under the rule, then I can also say in the conclusion only that the predicate of the rule belongs to something, because I have subsumed no more than this under the rule. And if I have a negative proposition as rule (major premise): then I must make the conclusion negative too. For if the major premise says: Of everything that stands under the condition of the rule this or that predicate must be denied: then the conclusion must also deny the predicate of that (subject) which has been subsumed under the condition of the rule.",[]],"jaesche-s03-306":["§. 65. Pure and mixed categorical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-307":["A categorical syllogism is pure (*purus*) if in it no immediate inference is mixed in, nor the lawful order of the premises altered; otherwise it is called an impure or mixed one (*ratiocinium impurum* or *hybridum*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-308":["§. 66. Mixed syllogisms through conversion of the propositions — figures.",[]],"jaesche-s03-309":["Among the mixed inferences are to be counted those that arise through the conversion of the propositions and in which, therefore, the position of these propositions is not the lawful one. This case obtains with the three latter so-called figures of the categorical syllogism.",[]],"jaesche-s03-310":["§. 67. Four figures of inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-311":["By figures are to be understood those four ways of inferring whose difference is determined by the particular position of the premises and their concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s03-312":["§. 68. Determining ground of their difference through the different position of the middle concept.",[]],"jaesche-s03-313":["The middle concept, namely, on whose position it here properly depends, can occupy either 1) in the major premise the place of the subject and in the minor premise the place of the predicate, or 2) in both premises the place of the predicate, or 3) in both the place of the subject, or finally 4) in the major premise the place of the predicate and in the minor premise the place of the subject. Through these four cases the difference of the four figures is determined. Let S designate the subject of the conclusion, P its predicate and M the *terminus medius*, then the schema for the four figures mentioned can be presented in the following table:",[]],"jaesche-s03-314":["{}",[]],"jaesche-s03-315":["§. 69. Rule for the first figure, as the only lawful one.",[]],"jaesche-s03-316":["The rule of the first figure is: that the major be a universal, the minor an affirmative proposition. And since this must be the universal rule of all categorical syllogisms in general: it results from this that the first figure is the only lawful one, which lies at the ground of all the rest, and to which all the rest, insofar as they are to have validity, must be led back through conversion of the premises (*metathesin praemissorum*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-317":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-318":["Remark. The first figure can have a conclusion of every quantity and quality. In the remaining figures there are only conclusions of a certain kind; some *modi* of them are here excluded. This already indicates that these figures are not perfect, but that certain restrictions are present with them, which prevent the conclusion from not being able to obtain in all *modi*, as in the first figure.",[]],"jaesche-s03-319":["§. 70. Condition of the reduction of the three latter figures to the first.",[]],"jaesche-s03-320":["The condition of the validity of the three latter figures, under which in each of them a correct *modus* of inferring is possible, comes down to this: that the *medius terminus* receive in the propositions such a place that from it, through immediate inferences (*consequentias immediatas*), its place according to the rules of the first figure can arise. — From this result the following rules for the three latter figures.",[]],"jaesche-s03-321":["§. 71. Rule of the second figure.",[]],"jaesche-s03-322":["In the second figure the minor stands right, thus the major must be converted, and indeed in such a way that it remains universal (*universalis*). This is possible only if it is universally negative; but if it is affirmative, then it must be contraposed. In both cases the conclusion becomes negative (*sequitur partem debiliorem*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-323":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-324":["Remark. The rule of the second figure is: That to which a mark of a thing contradicts, that contradicts the thing itself. Here I must now first convert and say: That to which a mark contradicts, that contradicts this mark, or I must convert the conclusion: That to which a mark of a thing contradicts, to that the thing itself contradicts, consequently it contradicts the thing.",[]],"jaesche-s03-325":["§. 72. Rule of the third figure.",[]],"jaesche-s03-326":["In the third figure the major stands right, thus the minor must be converted; yet so that an affirmative proposition arises from it. But this is possible only in that the affirmative proposition is particular, consequently the conclusion is particular.",[]],"jaesche-s03-327":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-328":["Remark. The rule of the third figure is: What belongs to or contradicts a mark, that also belongs to or contradicts some under which this mark is contained. Here I must first say: It belongs to or contradicts all that are contained under this mark.",[]],"jaesche-s03-329":["§. 73. Rule of the fourth figure.",[]],"jaesche-s03-330":["If in the fourth figure the major is universally negative: then it can be converted purely (*simpliciter*), likewise the minor as particular; thus the conclusion is negative. If on the contrary the major is universally affirmative: then it can either only be converted *per accidens* or contraposed; thus the conclusion is either particular or negative. If the conclusion is not to be converted (PS changed into SP): then a transposition of the premises (*metathesis praemissorum*) or a conversion (*conversio*) of both must take place.",[]],"jaesche-s03-331":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-332":["Remark. In the fourth figure it is inferred: the predicate hangs on the *medius terminus*, the *medius terminus* on the subject (of the conclusion), consequently the subject on the predicate; which, however, does not follow at all, but at most its converse. — In order to make this possible, the major must be made the minor and *vice versa*, and the conclusion converted, because with the former alteration the *terminus minor* is changed into the *major*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-333":["§. 74. General results concerning the three latter figures.",[]],"jaesche-s03-334":["From the rules given for the three latter figures it is evident,",[]],"jaesche-s03-335":["1) that in none of them is there a universally affirmative conclusion, but that the conclusion is always either negative or particular;",[]],"jaesche-s03-336":["2) that in each one an immediate inference (*consequentia immediata*) is mixed in, which is indeed not expressly designated, but yet must be tacitly understood along with it, that thus also on that account",[]],"jaesche-s03-337":["3) all these three latter *modi* of inferring must be called not pure but impure inferences (*ratiocinia hybrida, impura*), since every pure inference can have no more than three principal propositions (*termini*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-338":["§. 75. 2. Hypothetical syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-339":["A hypothetical inference is one that has a hypothetical proposition as its major. It thus consists of two propositions, 1) an antecedent (*antecedens*) and 2) a consequent (*consequens*), and here one concludes either according to the *modus ponens* or the *modus tollens*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-340":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-341":["Remark 1. Hypothetical syllogisms thus have no *terminus medius*, but with them the consequence of one proposition from the other is only indicated. In their major, namely, the consequence of two propositions from each other is expressed, of which the first is a premise, the second a conclusion. The minor is a transformation of the problematic condition into a categorical proposition.",[]],"jaesche-s03-342":["2. From the fact that the hypothetical inference consists only of two propositions, without having a middle concept, it is to be seen: that it is properly no syllogism, but rather only an immediate inference, to be demonstrated from an antecedent and consequent, according to the matter or according to the form (*consequentia immediata demonstrabilis [ex antecedente et consequente] vel quoad materiam vel quoad formam*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-343":["Every syllogism is to be a proof. Now, however, the hypothetical carries with it only the ground of proof. Consequently it is also clear from this that it cannot be a syllogism.",[]],"jaesche-s03-344":["§. 76. Principle of hypothetical inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-345":["The principle of hypothetical inferences is the principle of ground: *A ratione ad rationatum; a negatione rationati ad negationem rationis valet consequentia.*",[]],"jaesche-s03-346":["§. 77. 3. Disjunctive syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-347":["In disjunctive inferences the major is a disjunctive proposition and must therefore, as such, have members of the division or disjunction.",[]],"jaesche-s03-348":["Here one infers either 1) from the truth of one member of the disjunction to the falsity of the rest, or 2) from the falsity of all members, except one, to the truth of this one. The former happens through the *modus ponens* (or *ponendo tollens*), the latter through the *modus tollens* (*tollendo ponens*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-349":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-350":["Remark 1. All members of the disjunction, except one, taken together, make up the contradictory opposite of this one. There obtains here, therefore, a dichotomy, according to which, if one of the two is true, the other must be false and conversely.",[]],"jaesche-s03-351":["2. All disjunctive syllogisms of more than two members of the disjunction are thus properly polysyllogistic. For all true disjunction can only be *bimembris*, and logical division is also *bimembris*, but the *membra subdividentia* are, for the sake of brevity, placed under the *membra dividentia*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-352":["§. 78. Principle of disjunctive syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-353":["The principle of disjunctive inferences is the principle of the excluded third:",[]],"jaesche-s03-354":["*A contradictorie oppositorum negatione unius ad affirmationem alterius, a positione unius ad negationem alterius valet consequentia.*",[]],"jaesche-s03-355":["§. 79. Dilemma.",[]],"jaesche-s03-356":["A dilemma is a hypothetical-disjunctive syllogism, or a hypothetical inference whose *consequens* is a disjunctive judgment. The hypothetical proposition whose *consequens* is disjunctive is the major premise; the minor premise affirms that the *consequens* (*per omnia membra*) is false, and the conclusion affirms that the *antecedens* is false. (*A remotione consequentis ad negationem antecedentis valet consequentia.*)",[]],"jaesche-s03-357":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-358":["Remark. The ancients made very much of the dilemma and called this inference *cornutus*. They knew how to drive an opponent into a corner by reciting everything to which he could turn and then also refuting everything for him. They showed him many difficulties with every opinion that he adopted. But it is a sophistical artifice not to refute propositions directly, but only to show difficulties; which indeed is feasible with many, indeed with most things.",[]],"jaesche-s03-359":["Now if we want straightaway to declare everything false in which difficulties are found: then it is an easy game to reject everything. It is indeed good to show the impossibility of the opposite, but there lies in this something deceptive, if one takes the incomprehensibility of the opposite for its impossibility. Dilemmas therefore have much that is captious about them, although they infer correctly. They can be used to defend true propositions, but also to attack true propositions through difficulties that one raises against them.",[]],"jaesche-s03-360":["§. 80. Formal and hidden syllogisms (*ratiocinia formalia* and *cryptica*.)",[]],"jaesche-s03-361":["A formal syllogism is one that not only contains everything required according to the matter, but is also correctly and completely expressed according to the form. Opposed to formal syllogisms are the hidden ones (*cryptica*), among which can be counted all those in which either the premises are transposed, or one of the premises is left out, or finally the middle concept alone is combined with the conclusion. A hidden syllogism of the second kind, in which the one premise is not expressed but only thought along with it, is called a mutilated one or an enthymeme. Those of the third kind are called contracted inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-362":["[Third Section. On Inferences.] III. Inferences of the power of judgment. [§§ 81 — 93]",[]],"jaesche-s03-363":["§. 81. Determining and reflecting power of judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-364":["The power of judgment is twofold: the determining or the reflecting power of judgment. The former goes from the universal to the particular, the second from the particular to the universal. The latter has only subjective validity, for the universal to which it proceeds from the particular is only empirical universality — a mere *analogon* of the logical.",[]],"jaesche-s03-365":["§. 82. Inferences of the (reflecting) power of judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-366":["The inferences of the power of judgment are certain kinds of inference for coming from particular concepts to universal ones. They are thus not functions of the determining, but of the reflecting power of judgment; hence they also do not determine the object, but only the way of reflection on it, in order to arrive at acquaintance with it.",[]],"jaesche-s03-367":["§. 83. Principle of these inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-368":["The principle that lies at the ground of the inferences of the power of judgment is this: that many will not agree in one without a common ground, but that that which belongs to many in this way will be necessary from a common ground.",[]],"jaesche-s03-369":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-370":["Remark. Since such a principle lies at the ground of the inferences of the power of judgment, they cannot on that account be held to be immediate inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-371":["§. 84. Induction and analogy — the two kinds of inference of the power of judgment.",[]],"jaesche-s03-372":["The power of judgment, in proceeding from the particular to the universal in order to draw from experience, hence not a priori (empirically), universal judgments, infers either from many to all things of a kind, or from many determinations and properties in which things of one and the same kind agree, to the rest, insofar as they belong to the same principle. The former kind of inference is called inference through induction, the other inference according to analogy.",[]],"jaesche-s03-373":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-374":["Remark 1. Induction thus infers from the particular to the universal (*a particulari ad universale*) according to the principle of universalization: What belongs to many things of a genus, that belongs also to the rest. Analogy infers from particular similarity of two things to total, according to the principle of specification: Things of one genus, of which one knows much that agrees, agree also in the rest which we know in some of this genus, but do not perceive in others. Induction extends the empirically given from the particular to the universal in regard to many objects, analogy on the contrary the given properties of a thing to several of that very same thing — one in many, therefore in all: induction, many in one (which is also in others), therefore also the rest in the same: analogy. Thus, e.g., the ground of proof for immortality from the complete development of the natural predispositions of every creature is an inference according to analogy.",[]],"jaesche-s03-375":["In the inference according to analogy, however, the identity of the ground (*par ratio*) is not required. We infer according to analogy only to rational inhabitants of the moon, not to human beings. Also one cannot infer according to analogy beyond the *tertium comparationis*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-376":["2. Every syllogism must yield necessity. Induction and analogy are therefore not syllogisms, but only logical presumptions or also empirical inferences; and through induction one indeed gets general, but not universal propositions.",[]],"jaesche-s03-377":["3. The inferences of the power of judgment mentioned are useful and indispensable for the purpose of the extension of our cognition of experience. But since they give only empirical certainty: we must make use of them with caution and foresight.",[]],"jaesche-s03-378":["§. 85. Simple and composite syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-379":["A syllogism is called simple if it consists of only one, composite if it consists of several syllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-380":["§. 86. *Ratiocinatio polysyllogistica.*",[]],"jaesche-s03-381":["A composite inference in which the several syllogisms are not through mere coordination, but through subordination,",[]],"jaesche-s03-382":["i.e., as grounds and consequences combined with one another, is called a chain of syllogisms (*ratiocinatio polysyllogistica*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-383":["§. 87. Prosyllogisms and episyllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-384":["In the series of composite inferences one can infer in a twofold way, either from the grounds down to the consequences, or from the consequences up to the grounds. The first happens through episyllogisms, the other through prosyllogisms.",[]],"jaesche-s03-385":["An episyllogism is namely that inference in the series of inferences whose premise becomes the conclusion of a prosyllogism, thus of an inference which has the premise of the former as its conclusion.",[]],"jaesche-s03-386":["§. 88. Sorites or chain-inference.",[]],"jaesche-s03-387":["An inference consisting of several abbreviated inferences combined with one another into one conclusion is called a sorites or chain-inference, which can be either progressive or regressive; according as one ascends from the nearer grounds to the more remote, or descends from the more remote grounds to the nearer.",[]],"jaesche-s03-388":["§. 89. Categorical and hypothetical sorites.",[]],"jaesche-s03-389":["The progressive as well as the regressive chain-inferences can in turn be either categorical or hypothetical. The former consist of categorical propositions as a series of predicates, the latter of hypothetical ones, as a series of consequences.",[]],"jaesche-s03-390":["§. 90. Fallacy — paralogism — sophism.",[]],"jaesche-s03-391":["A syllogism which is false according to the form, although it has the illusion of a correct inference in its favor, is called a fallacy (*fallacia*). Such an inference is a paralogism insofar as one thereby deceives oneself, a sophism insofar as one thereby seeks with intention to deceive others.",[]],"jaesche-s03-392":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-393":["Remark. The ancients occupied themselves very much with the art of making such sophisms. Hence many of this kind have arisen; e.g., the *sophisma figurae dictionis*, in which the *medius terminus* is taken in different meaning — *fallacia a dicto secundum quid ad dictum simpliciter, sophisma heterozeteseos, elenchi, ignorationis* and the like.",[]],"jaesche-s03-394":["§. 91. Leap in inferring.",[]],"jaesche-s03-395":["A leap (*saltus*) in inferring or proving is the combination of one premise with the conclusion, such that the other premise is left out. Such a leap is legitimate (*legitimus*) if everyone can easily add the missing premise in thought, but illegitimate (*illegitimus*) if the subsumption is not clear. Here a remote mark is connected with a thing without an intermediate mark (*nota intermedia*).",[]],"jaesche-s03-396":["§. 92. *Petitio principii.* — *Circulus in probando.*",[]],"jaesche-s03-397":["By a *petitio principii* one understands the assumption of a proposition as ground of proof as an immediately certain proposition, although it still requires a proof. And one commits a circle in proving if one lays at the ground of its own proof that proposition which one has wanted to prove.",[]],"jaesche-s03-398":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-399":["Remark. The circle in proving is often hard to discover, and this error is commonly committed most frequently precisely where the proofs are difficult.",[]],"jaesche-s03-400":["§. 93. *Probatio plus* and *minus probans*.",[]],"jaesche-s03-401":["A proof can prove too much, but also too little. In the latter case it proves only a part of that which is to be proved, in the former it extends also to that which is false.",[]],"jaesche-s03-402":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s03-403":["Remark. A proof that proves too little can be true and is thus not to be rejected. But if it proves too much: then it proves more than what is true; and that is then false. Thus, e.g., the proof against suicide: that whoever has not given himself life also cannot take it, proves too much; for on this ground we could also not kill any animals. It is therefore false.",[]],"jaesche-s03-404":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-001":["II. General Doctrine of Method. [General Doctrine of Method.] [§§ 94 — 98]",[]],"jaesche-s04-002":["§. 94. Manner and method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-003":["All cognition and a whole of it must be in conformity with a rule. (Lawlessness is at the same time unreason.) But this rule is either that of manner (free) or that of method (constraint).",[]],"jaesche-s04-004":["§. 95. Form of science. — Method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-005":["Cognition, as science, must be arranged according to a method. For science is a whole of cognition as system and not merely as aggregate. It therefore requires a systematic cognition, hence one composed according to reflected rules.",[]],"jaesche-s04-006":["§. 96. Doctrine of method. — Its object and end.",[]],"jaesche-s04-007":["As the doctrine of elements in logic has the elements and conditions of the perfection of a cognition as its content: so the general doctrine of method, on the contrary, as the other part of logic, has to treat of the form of a science in general, or of the way and manner of proceeding, to connect the manifold of cognition into a science.",[]],"jaesche-s04-008":["§. 97. Means for the furtherance of the logical perfection of cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-009":["The doctrine of method is to set forth the way in which we arrive at the perfection of cognition. Now one of the most essential logi|cal",[]],"jaesche-s04-010":["perfections of cognition consists in the distinctness, the thoroughness and systematic arrangement of it into the whole of a science. The doctrine of method will accordingly have chiefly to state the means through which these perfections of cognition are furthered.",[]],"jaesche-s04-011":["§. 98. Conditions of the distinctness of cognition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-012":["The distinctness of cognitions and their combination into a systematic whole depends on the distinctness of concepts both in regard to that which is contained in them, and with respect to that which is contained under them.",[]],"jaesche-s04-013":["The distinct consciousness of the content of concepts is furthered through exposition and definition of them, the distinct consciousness of their domain, on the contrary, through the logical division of them. — First, therefore, here of the means for the furtherance of the distinctness of concepts in regard to their content.",[]],"jaesche-s04-014":["I. Furtherance of the logical perfection of cognition through definition, exposition and description of concepts. [§§ 99 — 109]",[]],"jaesche-s04-015":["§. 99. Definition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-016":["A definition is a sufficiently distinct and measured concept (*conceptus rei adaequatus in minimis terminis, complete determinatus*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-017":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-018":["Remark. The definition alone is to be regarded as a logically perfect concept, for in it are united the two most essential perfections of a concept: distinctness, and completeness and precision in distinctness (quantity of distinctness).",[]],"jaesche-s04-019":["§. 100. Analytic and synthetic definition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-020":["All definitions are either analytic or synthetic. The former are definitions of a given, the latter definitions of a made concept.",[]],"jaesche-s04-021":["§. 101. Given and made concepts a priori and a posteriori.",[]],"jaesche-s04-022":["The given concepts of an analytic definition are given either a priori or a posteriori; just as the made concepts of a synthetic definition are made either a priori or a posteriori.",[]],"jaesche-s04-023":["§. 102. Synthetic definitions through exposition or construction.",[]],"jaesche-s04-024":["The synthesis of made concepts, from which synthetic definitions arise, is either that of exposition (of appearances) or that of construction. The latter is the synthesis of arbitrarily made, the former the synthesis of empirically — i.e., from given appearances, as the matter of them, — made concepts (*conceptus factitii vel a priori vel per synthesin empiricam*). Arbitrarily made concepts are the mathematical ones.",[]],"jaesche-s04-025":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-026":["Remark. All definitions of mathematical concepts and — if indeed with empirical concepts definitions could obtain at all — also of concepts of experience must thus be made synthetically. For even with concepts of the latter kind, e.g., the empirical concepts water, fire, air and the like, I am not to analyze what lies in them, but to come to know through experience what belongs to them. All empirical concepts must thus be regarded as made concepts, whose synthesis, however, is not arbitrary but empirical.",[]],"jaesche-s04-027":["§. 103. Impossibility of empirically synthetic definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-028":["Since the synthesis of empirical concepts is not arbitrary but empirical and as such can never be complete (because one can always discover still more marks of the concept in experience): empirical concepts also cannot be defined.",[]],"jaesche-s04-029":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-030":["Remark. Synthetically, therefore, only arbitrary concepts can be defined. Such definitions of arbitrary concepts, which are not only always possible but also necessary, and must precede everything that is said by means of an arbitrary concept, one could also call declarations, insofar as one thereby declares one's thoughts or gives an account of what one understands by a word. This is the case with the mathematicians.",[]],"jaesche-s04-031":["§. 104. Analytic definitions through analysis of concepts given a priori or a posteriori.",[]],"jaesche-s04-032":["All given concepts, whether they be given a priori or a posteriori, can be defined only through analysis. For given concepts one can make distinct only insofar as one makes their marks successively clear. If all marks of a given concept are made clear: then the concept becomes completely distinct, if it also contains not too many marks, then it is at the same time precise and from this arises a definition of the concept.",[]],"jaesche-s04-033":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-034":["Remark. Since one can become certain through no test whether one has exhausted all the marks of a given concept through complete analysis: all analytic definitions are to be held to be uncertain.",[]],"jaesche-s04-035":["§. 105. Expositions and descriptions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-036":["Not all concepts, therefore, can be defined, but neither may they all be.",[]],"jaesche-s04-037":["There are approximations to the definition of certain concepts; these are partly expositions (*expositiones*), partly descriptions (*descriptiones*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-038":["The exposing of a concept consists in the connected (successive) representation of its marks, so far as these have been found through analysis.",[]],"jaesche-s04-039":["Description is the exposition of a concept insofar as it is not precise.",[]],"jaesche-s04-040":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-041":["Remark 1. We can expose either a concept or experience. The first happens through analysis, the second through synthesis.",[]],"jaesche-s04-042":["2. Exposition thus obtains only with given concepts, which are thereby made distinct; it is thereby distinguished from declaration, which is a distinct representation of made concepts.",[]],"jaesche-s04-043":["Since it is not always possible to make the analysis complete, and since in general a dissection, before it becomes complete, must first be incomplete: so an incomplete exposition, as part of a definition, is also a true and usable presentation of a concept. The definition here always remains only the idea of a logical perfection, which we must seek to attain.",[]],"jaesche-s04-044":["3. Description can take place only in the case of empirically given concepts. It has no determinate rules and contains only the materials for definition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-045":["§ 106. Nominal and real definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-046":["By mere name-explanations or nominal definitions are to be understood those which contain the meaning that one has wanted to give arbitrarily to a certain name, and which therefore designate only the logical essence of their object, or serve merely for distinguishing it from other objects. Thing-explanations or real definitions, on the other hand, are such as suffice for the cognition of the object, according to its inner determinations, in that they set forth the possibility of the object from inner marks.",[]],"jaesche-s04-047":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-048":["Note 1. If a concept is internally sufficient to distinguish the thing, then it is certainly also so externally, but if it is not internally sufficient: then it can nonetheless be externally sufficient merely in a certain relation, namely in the comparison of the *definitum* with others. But unrestricted external sufficiency is not possible without the internal.",[]],"jaesche-s04-049":["2. Objects of experience permit merely nominal explanations. Logical nominal definitions of given concepts of the understanding are taken from an attribute, real definitions on the other hand from the essence of the thing, the first ground of possibility. The latter thus contain that which at all times belongs to the thing — the real essence of it. Merely negative definitions also cannot be called real definitions, because negative marks can indeed serve for the distinguishing of a thing from others just as well as affirmative ones, but not for the cognition of the thing according to its inner possibility.",[]],"jaesche-s04-050":["In matters of morals real definitions must always be sought, thither all our striving must be directed. There are real definitions in mathematics, for the definition of an arbitrary concept is always real.",[]],"jaesche-s04-051":["3. A definition is genetic if it gives a concept through which the object can be presented *a priori in concreto*; of this kind are all mathematical definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-052":["§ 107. Chief requirements of definition.",[]],"jaesche-s04-053":["The essential and universal requirements that belong to the perfection of a definition in general can be considered under the four chief moments of quantity, quality, relation and modality:",[]],"jaesche-s04-054":["1) according to quantity — as concerns the sphere of the definition — the definition and the *definitum* must be reciprocal concepts (*conceptus reciproci*), and consequently the definition neither wider nor narrower than its *definitum*,",[]],"jaesche-s04-055":["2) according to quality the definition must be an exhaustive and at the same time precise concept,",[]],"jaesche-s04-056":["3) according to relation it must not be tautological, i.e., the marks of the *definitum* must, as grounds of cognition of it, be different from it itself, and finally",[]],"jaesche-s04-057":["4) according to modality the marks must be necessary and thus not such as come in addition through experience.",[]],"jaesche-s04-058":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-059":["Note. The condition: that the concept of the genus and the concept of the specific difference (*genus* and *differentia specifica*) are to make | up the definition",[]],"jaesche-s04-060":["holds only in regard to nominal definitions in comparison, but not for real definitions in derivation.",[]],"jaesche-s04-061":["§ 108. Rules for the testing of definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-062":["In the testing of definitions four actions are to be performed; it is namely to be investigated here: whether the definition",[]],"jaesche-s04-063":["1) considered as a proposition, is true, whether it",[]],"jaesche-s04-064":["2) as a concept, is distinct,",[]],"jaesche-s04-065":["3) whether as a distinct concept it is also exhaustive, and finally",[]],"jaesche-s04-066":["4) as an exhaustive concept is at the same time determinate, i.e., adequate to the thing itself.",[]],"jaesche-s04-067":["§ 109. Rules for the making of definitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-068":["The very same actions that belong to the testing of the definition are now also to be performed in the making of it. For this end, then, seek: 1) true propositions, 2) such whose predicate does not already presuppose the concept of the thing, 3) collect several of them and compare them with the concept of the thing itself, whether it be adequate, and finally 4) see to it whether one mark does not lie in another or is not subordinated to it.",[]],"jaesche-s04-069":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-070":["Note 1. These rules hold, as is indeed understood even without reminder, only of analytic definitions. — Since one can now never be certain here whether the analysis has been complete: so one may also set up the definition only as an attempt and make use of it only as if it were no definition. Under this restriction one can nonetheless use it as a distinct and true concept and draw corollaries from the marks of it. I shall namely be able to say: to that to which the concept of the *definitum* belongs, the definition also belongs, but of course not conversely, since the definition does not exhaust the whole *definitum*.",[]],"jaesche-s04-071":["2. To make use of the concept of the *definitum* in the explanation, or to lay the *definitum* at the ground in the definition, is called explaining through a circle (*circulus in definiendo*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-072":["II. Promotion of the perfection of cognition through logical division of concepts.[§§ 110 — 120]",[]],"jaesche-s04-073":["§ 110. Concept of logical division.",[]],"jaesche-s04-074":["Every concept contains a manifold under itself, insofar as it agrees, but also, insofar as it is different. The determination of a concept in regard to everything possible that is contained under it, insofar as it is opposed to one another, i.e., distinguished from one another, is called the logical division of the concept. The higher concept is called the divided concept (*divisum*), and the lower concepts the members of the division (*membra dividentia*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-075":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-076":["Note 1. To part a concept and to divide it is thus very different. In the parting of the concept I see what is contained in it (through analysis), in division I consider what is contained under it. Here I divide the sphere of the concept, not the concept itself. So far, then, from division being a parting of the concept: the members of the division rather contain more in themselves than the divided concept.",[]],"jaesche-s04-077":["2. We go up from lower to higher concepts and afterwards we can go down again from these to lower ones — through division.",[]],"jaesche-s04-078":["§ 111. Universal rules of logical division.",[]],"jaesche-s04-079":["In every division of a concept it is to be seen to:",[]],"jaesche-s04-080":["1) that the members of the division exclude one another or be opposed to one another, that they further",[]],"jaesche-s04-081":["2) belong under One higher concept (*conceptum communem*), and that they finally",[]],"jaesche-s04-082":["3) all taken together make up the sphere of the divided concept or be equal to it.",[]],"jaesche-s04-083":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-084":["Note. The members of the division must be separated from one another through contradictory opposition, not through a mere contrary (*contrarium*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-085":["§ 112. Codivision and subdivision.",[]],"jaesche-s04-086":["Different divisions of a concept, which are made with different aims, are called codivisions, and the division of the members of the division is called a subdivision (*subdivisio*).",[]],"jaesche-s04-087":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-088":["Note 1. Subdivision can be continued to infinity, comparatively, however, it can be finite. Codivision also goes to infinity, especially in the case of concepts of experience; for who can exhaust all relations of concepts?",[]],"jaesche-s04-089":["2. One can also call codivision a division according to the difference of the concepts of the same object (points of view), just as subdivision a division of the points of view themselves.",[]],"jaesche-s04-090":["§ 113. Dichotomy and polytomy.",[]],"jaesche-s04-091":["A division into two members is called dichotomy; but if it has more than two members, it is called polytomy.",[]],"jaesche-s04-092":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-093":["Note 1. All polytomy is empirical, dichotomy is the only division from principles *a priori*, thus the only primitive division. For the members of the division are to be opposed to one another and of every A the opposite is surely nothing more than non A.",[]],"jaesche-s04-094":["2. Polytomy cannot be taught in logic, for to it belongs cognition of the object. Dichotomy, however, needs only the principle of contradiction, without knowing the concept which one wants to divide, according to content. Polytomy needs intuition; either *a priori*, as in mathematics (e.g., the division of the conic sections), or empirical intuition, as in the description of nature. Yet division from the principle of synthesis *a priori* has trichotomy, namely: 1) the concept as the condition, 2) the conditioned, and 3) the derivation of the latter from the former.",[]],"jaesche-s04-095":["§ 114. Different divisions of method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-096":["As now in particular concerns, further, method itself in the working up and treatment of scientific cognitions: there are different chief kinds of it, which we can indicate here according to the following division.",[]],"jaesche-s04-097":["§ 115. 1. Scientific or popular method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-098":["The scientific or scholastic method distinguishes itself from the popular by this, that the former proceeds from fundamental and elementary propositions, the latter on the other hand from the ordinary and interesting. — The former aims at thoroughness and therefore removes everything heterogeneous, the latter aims at entertainment.",[]],"jaesche-s04-099":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-100":["Note. These two methods thus distinguish themselves according to kind and not according to mere presentation, and popularity in method is consequently something other than popularity in presentation.",[]],"jaesche-s04-101":["§ 116. 2. Systematic or fragmentary method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-102":["The systematic method is opposed to the fragmentary or rhapsodistic. If one has thought according to a method, and then this method is also expressed in the presentation and the transition from one proposition to another is distinctly indicated, then one has treated a cognition systematically. If on the contrary one has indeed thought according to a method, but not arranged the presentation methodically: then such a method is to be called rhapsodistic.",[]],"jaesche-s04-103":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-104":["Note. Systematic presentation is opposed to fragmentary, just as methodical to tumultuary. He who metho|dically",[]],"jaesche-s04-105":["thinks, can namely present systematically or fragmentarily. Presentation that is externally fragmentary, but in itself methodical, is aphoristic.",[]],"jaesche-s04-106":["§ 117. 3. Analytic or synthetic method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-107":["The analytic method is opposed to the synthetic. The former begins from the conditioned and grounded and progresses to the principles (*a principiatis ad principia*), the latter on the other hand goes from the principles to the consequences or from the simple to the composite. The first one could also call the regressive, just as the latter the progressive.",[]],"jaesche-s04-108":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-109":["Note. The analytic method is otherwise also called the method of discovery. For the end of popularity the analytic, but for the end of the scientific and systematic working up of cognition the synthetic method is more suitable.",[]],"jaesche-s04-110":["§ 118. 4. Syllogistic — Tabular method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-111":["The syllogistic method is that according to which a science is presented in a chain of inferences.",[]],"jaesche-s04-112":["Tabular is called that method according to which an already finished doctrinal edifice is presented in its entire interconnection.",[]],"jaesche-s04-113":["§ 119. 5. Acroamatic or erotematic method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-114":["Acroamatic is the method insofar as someone alone teaches, erotematic, insofar as he also asks. The latter method can in turn be divided into the dialogical or Socratic and into the catechetical, according as the questions are directed either to the understanding, or merely to memory.",[]],"jaesche-s04-115":["——————",[]],"jaesche-s04-116":["Note. Erotematically one can teach no otherwise than through the Socratic dialogue, in which both must question each other and also reciprocally an|swer",[]],"jaesche-s04-117":["so that it seems as if the pupil himself were also teacher. The Socratic dialogue teaches namely through questions, in that it teaches the learner to know his own principles of reason and sharpens his attention to them. Through common catechesis, however, one cannot teach, but only examine by questions what one has taught acroamatically. The catechetical method therefore also holds only for empirical and historical, the dialogical on the other hand for rational cognitions.",[]],"jaesche-s04-118":["§ 120. Meditating.",[]],"jaesche-s04-119":["By meditating is to be understood reflecting or a methodical thinking. Meditating must accompany all reading and learning, and for this it is required that one first of all undertake preliminary investigations and then bring one's thoughts into order or combine them according to a method.",[]],"jaesche-s04-120":["Logic (1800)",[]]};
